2 CONT Brychan
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Brychan Brycheiniog was a legendary 5th-century king of Brycheiniog (Brecknockshire) in South Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Celtic hagiography tells us that Brychan was born in Ireland, the son of a Prince Anlach, son of Coronac, and his wife, Marchel, heiress of the Welsh kingdom of Garthmadrun (Brycheiniog), which the couple later inherited. Upon his
2 CONC  father's death, he returned to Garthmadrun and changed its name to Brycheiniog.[1] Brychan's name may a Welsh version of the Irish name Broccán and that of his father Coronac may represent Cormac.[2] The Life of St. Cadoc by Lifr
2 CONC is (c. 1100) portrays Brychan fighting Arthur, Cai and Bedivere because of King Gwynllyw of Gwynllwg's abduction of his daughter St. Gwladys from his court in Talgarth.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Portraiture and veneration
2 CONT
2 CONT He is occasionally described as an undocumented saint [3] but the traditional literature does not call him a saint, referring to him as a patriarch instead, and he has no churches named for him.[4] A 15th century stained glass win
2 CONC dow in the parish church at St Neot in Cornwall, supposedly depicts Brychan, seated and crowned, holding in his arms eleven children. This, however, has been described by a standard modern guide as "God the Father with souls in hi
2 CONC s lap".
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children of Brychan
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Christian tradition, Brychan married three times: Prawst ferch Tydwal, Banhadlwedd ferch Banadi and Gwladys, and had a very large family. These are mentioned in several manuscripts, including those by William Worceste
2 CONC r, John Leland and Nicholas Roscarrock. The number of children attributed to him varies from twelve to sixty-three, the number most frequently encountered being twenty-four. There are two main lists however, one of Welsh origin an
2 CONC d one of Cornish origin. Most of his children appear to have travelled from Brecon to evangelise Cornwall and North Devon, where they are now venerated, but there is little agreement between the two lists. Some are referred to as 
2 CONC being 'in Manau' which has led to associations of Brychan with Manaw Gododdin in modern Scotland; although the Isle of Man seems more likely.
2 CONT
2 CONT The numbers of children may have grown over time, as more and more seculars as well as saints wished to claim descent from one of the 'Holy Families of Britain'. Listed below are children from Welsh, Cornish, Irish and Breton sour
2 CONC ces:
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Sons in Welsh sources
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons listed in the Cognacio Brychan, De Situ Brecheniauc and the genealogies of Jesus College MS 20 are Cynog, Rhain Dremrudd, Clydwyn, Arthen, Papai, Dingad, Berwyn and Rhydog. Also listed, but not in all three, are Cynon, Pa
2 CONC sgen, Cylflifer, Marthaerun and Rhun. Other Welsh sources claim the following additional sons: Caian, Cynbryd, Cynfran, Cynin, Dogfan, Dyfnan, Dyfrig, Hychan, Llecheu, Neffei, Rhawin, Llofan, Llonio, Heilin, Afallach, Gwynnen and 
2 CONC Gwynnws.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Daughters in Welsh sources
2 CONT
2 CONT The De Situ Brecheniauc lists: Meleri, Hunydd, Gwladys, Ceingar, Tudglid, Nyfain, Gwawr, Marchell, Lluan, Gwrygon Goddeu, Arianwen, Bethan, Ceinwen (Keyne), Cerddych, Clydai, Cynheiddon (identified with Saint Endelienta), Dwynwen,
2 CONC  Eiliwedd, Goleudydd, Gwen, Lludd, Tudful, Tudwystl and Tybie. Other Welsh sources claim the following additional daughters: Beiol, Tydieu, Eufail, Hawystl, Edwen, Gwenrhiw, Tudwen, Callwen, Gwenfyl, Gwennan and Mwynwen.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Descendants in Cornish sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed in the Life of Saint Nectan are, by his wife, Gwladys:
2 CONT
2 CONT Adwen, Canauc (Cynog), Cleder (Clether), Dilic (Illick), Endelient (Endelienta), Helie, Johannes (Sion), Iona, Juliana (Ilud), Kenhender (Cynidr), Keri (Curig), Mabon (Mabyn), Menfre (Menefrewy), Merewenne (Marwenna), Morewenna (M
2 CONC orwenna), Nectanus (Nectan), Tamalanc, Tedda (Tetha), Wencu (Gwencuff, Gwengustle, name of Saint Nennocha), Wenheden (Enoder), Wenna (Gwen), Wensent, Wynup (Gwenabwy) and Yse (Issey).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Robert Hunt, of the holy children that settled in Cornwall, we learn that the following gave their names to Cornish churches
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes at St Ive
2 CONT
2 CONT Endelient at St Endellion
2 CONT
2 CONT Menfre at St Minver
2 CONT
2 CONT Tedda at St Teath
2 CONT
2 CONT Mabon at St Mabyn
2 CONT
2 CONT Merewenne at Marhamchurch
2 CONT
2 CONT Wenna at St Wenn
2 CONT
2 CONT Keyne at St Keyne
2 CONT
2 CONT Yse at St Issey
2 CONT
2 CONT Morewenna at Morwenstow
2 CONT
2 CONT Cleder at St Clether
2 CONT
2 CONT Keri at Egloskerry
2 CONT
2 CONT Helie at Egloshayle
2 CONT
2 CONT Adwen at Advent
2 CONT
2 CONT Lanent at Lelant
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Irish sources
2 CONT
2 CONT The Book of Leinster lists the following sons by Brychan's wife, Dína daughter of the King of the Saxons: Mo-Goróc, Mo-Chonóc (Cynog), Diraid, Dubán (Dyfnan), Cairinne (Caian), Cairpre, Iast, Ellóc (Dilic), Paan, Cáemán and Mo-Beó
2 CONC c,
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Breton sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Breton tradition says that Brychan married Menedoc daughter of Constantine, King of the Scots. Together they were the parents of Saint Nennocha.
2 CONT
