2 CONT http://www.earlybritishkingdoms.com/bios/brochypw.html
2 CONT Brochfael Ysgythrog, King of Powys
2 CONT (Born c.AD 502)
2 CONT (Latin: Brogmaglus; English: Brockbald)
2 CONT
2 CONT Brochfael (alias Brochwel) of the Tusks presumably had very big teeth! He was a younger son of King Cyngen Glodrydd and his wife, St. Tudlwystl, a daughter of Brychan ap Gwyngwen ap Tewdr. The famous Taliesin was his bard for a ti
2 CONC me.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brochfael is particularly known for an incident involving St. Melangell. This stunning young woman had taken to the life of a hermit, living in a small cell in the Powys wilds. One day, Brochfael was out hunting a hare when the cr
2 CONC eature made for Melangell's hermitage and hid in her skirts. The dogs would not attack, and the King became so enamoured of the lady's pious beauty that he asked her to marry him. She humbly declined, so Brochfael gave her land to
2 CONC  build a monastery instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brochfael married Arddyn Benasgel, daughter of King Pabo Post Prydain of the Pennines, and together they had at least four sons: Cynan Garwyn, St. Tysilio, Mawn and Iago. Brochfael died around AD 560/70 and was buried at Pentrefoe
2 CONC las in Gwynedd where the grave of a six foot man, with a covering slab bearing the name "Brohomagli", has been uncovered. He was succeeded by his eldest son, Cynan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brochfael should not be identified with the Commander of Caer-Legion (Chester), of the same name, who died at the great battle there in AD 613.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id15.html
2 CONT We know a young lady named Annun was a member of the household of Madrun, daughter of Vortimer the Blessed, and is cited as her handmaiden. If the Annan of the Pillar were a female, she may have been the wife of Maun and a daughte
2 CONC r of Brydw. Those pedigrees which include "Cyngen ap Maucann"[12] in the Powys dynasty might be no more than a scribal attempt at rendering "Cyngen, son of Maun and Annan". Certainly there is no Maucann or Mawgan in Lluyd's list o
2 CONC f Pillar names. It is not necessary to conjecture whether the Pillar Annan was identical to the handmaiden of Modren vz Vortimer[13], nor even whether Vortimer was identical to Brydw.[14] But no one would argue that the Powys dyna
2 CONC sty proudly proclaimed it descended from a mere handmaiden of a granddaughter of Vortigern.
2 CONT
2 CONT The possibility that Annan was female and formed the connection between Vortigern and the Powys dynasty would place virtually all the ancient pedigrees into chronological alignment, something none of the other emendations have don
2 CONC e. The chart would look like this:
2 CONT 155 Rydeyrn[15]
2 CONT 185 Gwrtheyrn
2 CONT 220 Cadeyrn
2 CONT 250 Rhuddfedel Frych
2 CONT 285 Brydw Gloyw Gwallt Hir 280
2 CONT 315 Pasgen Gwidolin 310
2 CONT 350 Cadeyrn Gwidol 350
2 CONT 380 Cadell Ddyrnllwy Gwrtheyrn (Vortigern) 385
2 CONT 410 Pasgen Brydw 415
2 CONT 440 Maun====================Annan 450
2 CONT 475 Cyngen
2 CONT 510 Brochwel Ysgithrog
2 CONT 545 Cynan Garwyn
2 CONT 575 Selyf, obit 616
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id15.html
2 CONT Vortigern and the Powys Dynasty
2 CONT By Darrell Wolcott
2 CONT 385 Vortigern
2 CONT 415 Cadeyrn
2 CONT 445 Cadell Ddyrnllwg
2 CONT 475 Cyngen Glodrydd
2 CONT 510 Brochwel Ysgithrog
2 CONT 545 Cynan Garwyn
2 CONT 575 Selyf
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.earlybritishkingdoms.com/lists/powys.html
2 CONT Brochfael Ysgythrog (of the Tusks) fl. c.550
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id12.html
2 CONT NEST FERCH CADELL AP BROCHWEL
2 CONT By Darrell Wolcott
2 CONT The belief that a Nest ferch Cadell ap Brochwel "carried off the Province" of Powys[6] from its dynastic family probably arose at a point in history long after the era of Rhodri Mawr. There was another lady named Nest who was the 
2 CONC daughter of a king of Powys named Cadell ap Brochwel; she was born c. 970. Her father was Cadell ap Brochwel ap Aeddon and she married Gwerystan ap Gwaethfoed, giving birth to a son named Cynfyn about 985.[7] Following the death o
2 CONC f King Gruffudd ap Llewelyn in 1063, Rhiwallon ap Cynfyn took the kingship of Powys and was succeeded by his brother Bleddyn ap Cynfyn in 1069. This conquest or usurpation of Powys is noted in one version of the Brut which says th
2 CONC e brothers Rhiwallon and Bleddyn "took the sovereignty of the land of Powys from the lineage of Brochwel Ysgithrog, which was contrary to right".[8] Thus it would seem the loss of Powys to its dynastic family did not occur until t
2 CONC he late 11th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT The dynastic family continued its rule through Brochwel ap Aeddan, the son of Cyngen's cousin Aeddan ap Cyngen ap Brochwel ap Eliseg.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brochwel_Ysgithrog
2 CONT
2 CONT Brochwel son of Cyngen (Welsh: Brochwel ap Cyngen, died c. 560), better known as Brochwel Ysgrithrog, was a king of Powys in eastern Wales. The unusual epithet Ysgithrog has been translated as "of the canine teeth", "the fanged" o
2 CONC r "of the tusk" (perhaps because of big teeth, horns on a helmet or, most likely, his aggressive manner).
2 CONT
2 CONT Brochwel was the son of King Cyngen Glodrydd and his wife St. Tudlwystl, a daughter of Brychan ap Gwyngwen ap Tewdr. As far as is known Brochwel married Arddyn Benasgel, sometimes written Arddun Penasgell (Wing Headed), daughter o
2 CONC f King Pabo Post Prydain. They were the parents of King Cynan Garwyn and Saint Tysilio, the founder of the old church at Meifod.
2 CONT Poetry and tradition
2 CONT
2 CONT Powys has been frequently called "the land of Brochwel", but little is recorded of the events of this monarch's reign. Some details are available from Old Welsh poetry, but this is difficult to interpret, and none of the extant po
2 CONC ems about this period seem to pre-date the 9th century. Some are from as late as the 11th century. Brochwel is presented as a warrior hero and ruler of wide lands. These sources suggest that he was passionately fond of hunting, an
2 CONC d one of his chief resorts was the Vale of Meifod which he made his "May-Abode" or summer residence. On his summer visits to Mathrafal, he often visited the shrine of St. Gwyddfarch. Upon his saintly son, St Tysilio, he bestowed t
2 CONC he Bishopric of that part of his kingdom. St Tysilio and Brochwel are linked with the foundation of the Church at Meifod, but none of the stones of the current Church of St. Mary date from this period.
2 CONT The traditional arms of Brochwel Ysgithrog.
2 CONT
2 CONT The arms later assigned by the College of Arms to Brochwel, and that can be used by his male heirs, are 'Sable, three nags' heads, erased argent' which may represent three beheaded Saxon white horses. Many later tribes and family 
2 CONC lines in the area claim descent from Brochwel and include his arms within theirs. Most of the genealogies of these families were first documented by the heralds in the 16th century when the view taken of Brochwel can be illustrate
2 CONC d by the following quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT Brochwel Yscithroc, Consul of Chester, who dwelt in a town then called Pengwerne Powys, and now Shrewsbury (Salopia), whose dwelling house was in the verie same place where the college of St Chad's now standeth.
2 CONT —?Dr Powel, Historie of Cambrie (1584 ed.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Pengwern was certainly a Welsh kingdom or Royal residence which appears to have been located somewhere in Shropshire. It is unclear whether it was ruled by Brochwel. However, there does seem to have been a tradition that he was bu
2 CONC ried in St. Chad's College in Shrewsbury which he is said to have founded. Alternatively, some believe that Brochwel was buried at Pentrefoelas in Gwynedd where the grave has been uncovered of a six-foot man, with a covering slab 
2 CONC bearing the name 'Brohomagli'.
2 CONT False Brochwel
2 CONT According to Bede (Bk II, Ch 2), a 'Brochmail' was also one of the defending force when the monks of Bangor-on-Dee were slain by Æthelfrith of Northumbria at the Battle of Chester in circa 613. The account of the battle in the Wel
2 CONC sh Chronicles is consistent with Bede, but since it was written after his work the author was probably aware of it. However, this man is clearly not Brochwel as his grandson, Selyf ap Cynan, was King of Powys at this time and desc
2 CONC ribed as dying in the battle. References to the subject as 'Brochfael' are probably due to a mistaken identification with the person referred to by Bede.
