2 CONC , Pt. 1.
2 CONT ''Note'': There is a [https://www.amazon.com/Early-Sources-Scottish-History-1286/dp/0342995502 2018 reprint] of this book – supposedly with edits to correct errata et al. (Ken Shelley, July 2022)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Notes for Aedan of Argyll King Of Dalriada  Weis' "Ancestral Roots. . ." (170:5). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is taken from an Internet posting of Michael R. Davidson of Edinburgh. Scotland, on 23 Oct 1995:  Aedan mac Gabran  Aedan succeeded to the kingship upon his cousin Conall's death in 574. There is an entertaining stor
2 CONC y in Adomnan's _Life of Columba_ which relates how Columba would have preferred to support Eoganan as king. According to the life, an angel commanded Columba three times to support Aedan, and Columba did not relent until the angel
2 CONC  struck him with a scourge. In 575 Aedan attended the Convention of Druim Cett in Ireland, which apparently convened to decide the political relationship between Dal Riata and the kings of the Northern Ui Neill in Ireland, whose p
2 CONC ower was growing. In 581 he led an expedition to the Orkney islands, and he won a victory at the Isle of Man in the following year. In 590, he won a battle against the Maetae, his British neighbours, but lost two of his sons in th
2 CONC e battle. In 596, in the first battle between Scots and English, two more of his sons were slain. In 600, he lead an army against the English of Northumbria, but was decisively defeated at Degsastan. He was victorious in a battle 
2 CONC against the Picts sometime between 596 and 606. He died, at the age of seventy-four according to the annals, in 606, and was succeeded by his son Eochaid Buid. The _Senchus_ notes that he fathered seven sons, but other sources tel
2 CONC l of two others, Artur and Domangart.  Children:  1. *Eochaid Buid.  2. Eochaid Find, killed 590.  3. Tuathal.  4. Bran, killed 596.  5. Baithine.  6. Conaing, drowned 622.  7. Gartnait. (The same Gartnait that was king of the Pic
2 CONC ts and died in 599?)  8. Artur, killed 590.  9. Domangart, killed 596. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.southing.net/BondFam019%20Web%20Cards/PS02/PS02_129.HTM
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT "The Treacherous" King of Dal Riata Based on merged profiles, Born circa 530 or 532 Died circa 604, 608, or 629 -SPF
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Áedán mac Gabráin (pronounced [ˈaiðaːn mak ˈɡavraːnʲ] in Middle Irish) was a king of Dál Riata from circa 574 until his death, perhaps on 17 April 609. The kingdom of Dál Riata was situated in modern Argyll and Bute, Scotland, and
2 CONC  parts of County Antrim, Ireland. Genealogies record that Áedán was a son of Gabrán mac Domangairt.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a contemporary of Saint Columba, and much that is recorded of his life and career comes from hagiography such as Adomnán of Iona's Life of Saint Columba. Áedán appears as a character in Old Irish and Middle Irish language w
2 CONC orks of prose and verse, some now lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Irish annals record Áedán's campaigns against his neighbours, in Ireland, and in northern Britain, including expeditions to the Orkney Islands, the Isle of Man, and the east coast of Scotland. As recorded by Bede, Áedán was de
2 CONC cisively defeated by Æthelfrith of Bernicia at the Battle of Degsastan. Áedán may have been deposed, or have abdicated, following this defeat.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sources for Áedán's life include Bede's Historia ecclesiastica gentis Anglorum; Irish annals, principally the Annals of Ulster and the Annals of Tigernach; and Adomnán's Life of Saint Columba. The Senchus fer n-Alban, a census
2 CONC  and genealogy of Dál Riata, purports to record his ancestry and that of his immediate descendants. None of these sources are contemporary. Adomnán's work was written in the very late 7th century, probably to mark the centenary of
2 CONC  Columba's death. It incorporates elements from a now lost earlier life of Columba, De virtutibus sancti Columbae, by Cumméne Find. This was written perhaps as early as 640. However, neither the elements incorporated from Cumméne'
2 CONC s work nor Adomnán's own writings can be treated as simple history. Bede's history was written some 30 years after Adomnán's. The surviving Irish annals contain elements of a chronicle kept at Iona from the middle of the 7th centu
2 CONC ry onwards, so that these too are retrospective when dealing with Áedán's time.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rawlinson B. 502 manuscript, dated to c. 1130, contains the tale Gein Branduib maic Echach ocus Aedáin maic Gabráin (The Birth of Brandub son of Eochu and of Aedán son of Gabrán).:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''References'''
2 CONT * Adomnán (1995), Sharpe, Richard, ed., Life of St Columba, London: Penguin, ISBN 0-14-044462-9 
2 CONT * Anderson, Alan Orr (1990), Early Sources of Scottish History A.D 500–1286, I (2nd ed.), Stamford: Paul Watkins, ISBN 1-871615-03-8 
2 CONT * Anderson, M. O. (1980), Kings and Kingship in Early Scotland (2nd ed.), Edinburgh: Scottish Academic Press, ISBN 0-7011-1604-8 
2 CONT * Bannerman, John (1974), Studies in the History of Dalriada, Edinburgh: Scottish Academic Press, ISBN 0-7011-2040-1 
2 CONT * Bede (1990), Farmer, D. H.; Sherley-Price, Leo, eds., Ecclesiastical History of the English People, London: Penguin, ISBN 0-14-044565-X 
2 CONT * Bromwich, Rachel (2006), Trioedd Ynys Prydein: The Triads of the Island of Britain, University of Wales Press, ISBN 0-7083-1386-8 
2 CONT * Broun, Dauvit (2001), "Aedán mac Gabráin", in Lynch, Michael, The Oxford Companion to Scottish History, Oxford: Oxford University Press, ISBN ISBN 0-19-211696-7 
2 CONT * Byrne, Francis John (2005), "Ireland and her neighbours, c.1014–c.1072", in Ó Cróinín, Dáibhí, Prehistoric and Early Ireland, A New History of Ireland, I, Oxford: Oxford University Press, pp. 862–898, ISBN 0-19-922665-8 
2 CONT * Byrne, Francis John (1973), Irish Kings and High-Kings, London: Batsford, ISBN 0-7134-5882-8 
2 CONT * Charles-Edwards, T. M. (2000), Early Christian Ireland, Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, ISBN 0-521-39395-0 
2 CONT * Fraser, James E. (2009), From Caledonia to Pictland: Scotland to 795, The New Edinburgh History of Scotland, 1, Edinburgh: Edinburgh University Press, ISBN 0-7486-1232-1 
2 CONT * Ó Cróinín, Dáibhí (1995), Early Medieval Ireland: 400–1200, London: Longman, ISBN 0-582-01565-0 
2 CONT * Ó Cróinín, Dáibhí (2005), "Ireland 400–800", in Ó Cróinín, Dáibhí, Prehistoric and Early Ireland, A New History of Ireland, I, Oxford: Oxford University Press, pp. 182–234, ISBN 0-19-922665-8 
2 CONT * Kirby, D. P. (1991), The Earliest English Kings, London: Unwin, ISBN 0-04-445692-1 
2 CONT * MacQuarrie, Alan (1997), The Saints of Scotland: Essays in Scottish Church History AD 450–1093, Edinburgh: John Donald, ISBN 0-85976-446-X 
2 CONT * Smyth, Alfred P. (1984), Warlords and Holy Men: Scotland AD 80–1000, Edinburgh: Edinburgh UP, ISBN 0-7486-0100-7 
2 CONT * Woolf, Alex (1998), "Pictish matriliny reconsidered", The Innes Review 49 (2): 147–167, ISSN 0020-157X 
2 CONT * Yorke, Barbara (2006), The Conversion of Britain: Religion, Politics and Society in Britain, c.600–800, London: Longman, ISBN 0-582-77292-3
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps05/ps05_459.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Also spelled AEDAN, king of the Scottish kingdom of Dalriada. He was the son of Gabran, king of Dalriada. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aidan was crowned at Iona by St. Columba. He refused to allow his kingdom to remain dependent on the Irish Dalriada; but, coming into collision with his southern neighbours, he led a large force against Aethelfrith, king of the No
2 CONC rthumbrians, and was defeated at Degsanstan, probably in Liddesdale
2 CONT
2 CONT Aidan ruled the Scottish kingdom of Dalriada, succeeding his kinsman King Conall; Aidan was crowned at Iona by St. Columba; he refused to remain dependent upon the Irish Dalriada; he was defeated by Aethelfrith, King of Northumber
2 CONC land, at a place called Daeganstane (probably Liddesdale). {see Encyclopaedia Britannica, 1956 Ed., 1:441; 6:994.} Dalriada was
2 CONT
2 CONT the name of two Gaelic kingdoms, one in Ireland and one in Scotland (settled from northern Antrim in Ireland ca. 500 A.D.),
2 CONT
2 CONT the latter based in Argyllshire and dependent upon the Irish Dalrieda until about 575 when Aidan asserted its independence. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was consecrated by his cousin St. Columba.
2 CONT
2 CONT He invaded Northumbria in 603. He was defeated at Daegastan in 603.
2 CONT
2 CONT Events in the life of Àedán "the Treacherous" mac Gabráin 
2 CONT
2 CONT birth 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT ABT 0533, in "near the Forth". 
2 CONT
2 CONT † death 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT 17 Apr 0608, in Kilkerran. 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT 0574, in Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ·was crowned and anointed King of Scots Dalriada and Pendragon of the Celtic Isle (Aedàn Pen Draco Insularis) by (his 3rd cousin) St. Columba of Iona 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT BEF 0590.
2 CONT
2 CONT ·asked St. Columba which of his three sons-Artúr, Eochaid Find, or Domangart-will succeed him 
2 CONT
2 CONT burial 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT in Kilkerran.
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT 0603, in the Battle of Degsastan, Liddesdale, Lothian. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ·defeated by Aethelfrith, King of the North-Eastern kingdom of Bernicia, though both sides had heavy losses, and Aedan lost another son, Domangart 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT BETWEEN 0590 AND 0596, in the Battle of Miathi. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ·defeated the Southern Picts, the Maeatae, in the battle which he lost two sons, Arthur and Eochaid Find, 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT 0574.
2 CONT
2 CONT ·succeeded his 1st cousin, Conall I, to the throne of Dál Riata 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT 0573, in the Battle of Arfderydd. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ·served, probably, as a chief of the Gododdin Britons, commanding the lands around Aberfoyle, the region where he subsequently granted land to St. Berach for a monastery, and this capacity fought for the Britons 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT 0575, in Drumceat, Derry, Ireland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ·concluded an agreement with the High King of Ireland, Aed mac Ainmerech, in which Aedan retained authority, to tax and collect tribute, over the Dál Riatan peoples who still lived in the original homeland of Fergus in Ulster, but
2 CONC  these Dál Riatans were ultimately answerable to the Irish High King for military support, showing the Dál Riatan kings, even in Scotland, were subject to some degree to the High Kings of Ireland, 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT 0582.
2 CONT
2 CONT ·ejected Baetán mac Cairill from the Isle of Man 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT 0580.
2 CONT
2 CONT ·undertook a raid on the Orkney Islands, otherwise the territory of the Picts and King Brude, whose piratical inhabitants, Norseman, were conducting raids on Dál Riatan territory, most likely Iona, 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT BETWEEN 0580 AND 0584.
2 CONT
2 CONT ·fought a series of skirmishes with the Picts under King Brude, and generally won the advantage, 
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT AFT 0603, in Kilkerran. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ·is said to have abdicated after his defeat at the hands of the Angles of Bernicia and retired to a monastery
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Aidan(Aedan) acceded the Throne circa 574 and died circa 608. He was consecrated by his cousin St. Columba. In addition to Eochaid Buide, Aidan(Aedan) had six other sons: Eochaid Find, Tuathal, Bran, Baithine, Conaing, and Gartnai
2 CONC t. These six sons produced over twenty additional sons.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of Dál Riata, ca. 573-ca. 604.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king lists make Áedán the successor of Conall mac Comgall (d. ca. 573), and the predecessor of his son Eochu Buide, who succeeded about 604. Áedán is given a reign length of 24 years [Duan Albanach, 131] or 34 years [Poppleton
2 CONC  MS, KKES, 253; Regnal Lists "D", "F", "I", "K", KKES, 264, 270, 281, 286]. During the reign of Áedán, the well known Convention of Druim Cett was held, involving him and the Irish king Áed mac Ainmirech [Adomnán i, 49 (p. 89); Ba
2 CONC nnerman (1974), 157-170], the purpose of which is believed to have been to decide the status of Scottish and Irish Dál Riata with respect to the Irish king [On the date, see Meckler (1997); Jaski (1998)].
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Aedon, King of Dalriada, d. 608 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Gabran "the Treacherous", King of Dalriada, d. 559, He became King of Dalriada, ca. 538 in Dungad, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Ingenach
2 CONT
2 CONT King Aidan secured the independence of Dalriada in about 575. He was ordained King of Dalriada by Saint Columba.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Artur 
2 CONT
2 CONT Conaing 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gafron 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eugene IV, King of Dalriada, d. 630, He became King of Dalriada, ca. 608 in Dungad, Scotland 
2 CONT
