2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT
2 CONT 574-608: King of Dal Riata [Ref: Tapsell Dynasties p180] ruled Dalriada in Scotland for about 37 years 571-608 [Ref: Weis AR7 #170]
2 CONT
2 CONT ordained King by his cousin St. Columbia [Ref: Moncreiffe RoyalAnc p20]
2 CONT
2 CONT details of his life and those of his children and grandchildren are well attested in the near-contemporary life of St. Columa, by Adamnan [Ref: Weis AR7 #170]
2 CONT
2 CONT succeeded to the kingship upon his cousin Conall's death in 574. [Ref: Michael Davidson SGM 10/23/1995-115700]
2 CONT
2 CONT 575: attended the Convention of the Druim Cett in Ireland, which apparently convened to decide the political relationship between Dal Riata and the kings of the Northern Ui Neill in Ireland, whose power was growing. [Ref: Michael 
2 CONC Davidson SGM 10/23/1995-115700]
2 CONT
2 CONT 581: led an expedition to the Orkney Islands, and he won a victory at the Isle of Man in the following year. [Ref: Michael Davidson SGM 10/23/1995-115700]
2 CONT
2 CONT 590: won a battle against the Maetae, his British neighbours, but lost two of his sons in the battle. [Ref: Michael Davidson SGM 10/23/1995-115700]
2 CONT
2 CONT 596: in first battle between Scots and English, two more of his sons were slain. [Ref: Michael Davidson SGM 10/23/1995-115700]
2 CONT
2 CONT 600: he led an army against the English of Northumbria, but was decisively defeated at Degsastan [Ref: Michael Davidson SGM 10/23/1995-115700]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT   Gabhran, King of DalRiada married Lleian of South Wales, daughter of Brachan (Brychan) of South Wales. Gabhran, King of DalRiada died in 560.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT c.603 Worried about the expansive policies of King Aethelfrith of Bernicia, King Aedan invades his territory, wit a large contingent of Irish allies.  While the Scots are busy despoiling the country, the Bernicians gather a strong
2 CONC  army and the two clash at the Battle of Degsastan (Addinston)  King Aethelfrith is victorious, but suffers heavy losses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT c.606 King Aedan of Dalriada abdicates his throne in favor of his son, Eochaide Buide, and retires to the monastery of Kilkerran.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.earlybritishkingdoms.com/adversaries/kingdoms/scot598.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.keithblayney.com/Blayney/KingArthur.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Arthur of legend was only called a king after the 12th century cleric Geoffrey of Monmouth wrote a fabled account called "The History of the Kings of Britain", in which he called Arthur a king, placing him in the first half of the
2 CONC  6th century AD. Before Geoffrey's fictional story there are no historical references to a King Arthur - only references to a warlord Arthur and a Prince Arthur (559-603), the son of Aidan, King of the Scots of Dalriada. This Prin
2 CONC ce Arthur of Argyll (Prince of Scotland) married Gwenhwyfar De Bretagne (Guinevere of Brittany, daughter of Leo de Grance). "The two would be later known through the romanticization of actual history as King Arthur of the Round Ta
2 CONC ble and his wife, Gwenivere." [12]. This opinion or interpretion is shared by many authors. [19] [21] [22] [23]
2 CONT
2 CONT This Prince Arthur was the third of eight children (2nd son) of Aedan (Aidan/Aedham) Mac Gabran, King of Dalriada (b.525 r574-604) and Ygerna ferch Amlawdd of Wales. Aeden's parents were Gabran (Gabhran) of Argyll Mac Domangart (~
2 CONC 500 - ~560) (King of Dalriada & Scots) and Lleian (Liuan) b~526 (a.k.a. Ingrain). [11] [15]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Notes for Aidan MacGabhran: "The Treacherous"
2 CONT
2 CONT MacGabhran, Aidan of Argyll; consecrated by his cousin as St. Columba. Aedan succeeded to the kingship upon his cousin Conall's death in 574. A story in Adomnan's "Life of Columba" related how Colimba would have preferred to suppo
2 CONC rt Eoganan as king, but an angel commanded Columba three times to support Aedan, and Columba did not relent until the angel struck him with a scourge.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 575, Aedan attended the Convention of Druim Cet in Ireland, which apparently convened to decide the political relationship between Dalriada and the kings of northern Ui Neill in Ireland, whose power was growing. In 581, he led 
2 CONC an expedition to the Orkney Isles, and he won a victory at the Isle of Man in the following year. In 590, he won a battle against the Maetae, his British neighbors, but lost two of his sons (Eochaid Find and Artur) in the battle. 
2 CONC In 596, in the first battle between the Scots and English, two more of his sons (Bran and Domangart) were slain. In 600, he led an army against the English of Northumbria, but was decisively defeated at Degsatan. He was victorious
2 CONC  in a battle against the Picts sometime between 596 and 606. He died at the age of seventy-four, and was succeeded by his eldest son Eochaid Buide. The Senchus Notes say he fathered seven sons, but other sources tell of two others
2 CONC : Artur and Domangart.
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Acceded about 574. Consecrated by his cousin St. Columba. In 575 Aedan attended the Convention of Druim Cett in Ireland, which apparently convened to decide the political relationship between Dal Riata and the kings of the Norther
2 CONC n Ui Neill in Ireland, whose power was growing. In 581 he led an expedition to the Orkney islands, and he won a victory at the Isle of Man in the following year. In 590, he won a battle against the Maetae, his British neighbours, 
2 CONC but lost two of his sons in the battle. In 596, in the first battle between Scots and English, two more of his sons were slain. In 600, he lead an army against the English of Northumbria, but was decisively defeated at Degsastan. 
2 CONC He was victorious in a battle against the Picts sometime between 596 and 606.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Aidan MacGabhran are: i.+I Eochaidh, b. 565, Scotland, d. 629, Scotland.
