2 CONT Page 43:
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.    Aedan (Aidinus), c.533-c.608; succeeded Conall; crowned c.574 by his kinsman St. Columba on Iona.  In 576 at a synod at Drumceatt, County Londonderry, he succeeded in breaking the bond of allegiance which had made the two Da
2 CONC lriadas interdependent.  He was thus the first independent ruler of the Caledonian Scots.  He was defeated 603 by Ethelfryth, the neighboring King of Bernicia.  He may have retired to a monastery before his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amie, Adam and FAL.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 144 line (170-5):
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  Aedan.  Details of his life and those of his children and grandchildren are well attested in the near-contemporary life of St. Columba, by Adamnan.  Died about 608 A.D., after ruling Dalriada in Scotland for about 37 years.
