1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The David Nash Ford article cited in this profile [https://www.earlybritishkingdoms.com/bios/gwladys.html] is basically a recap of legendary stories. It is not suitable for historical or genealogical work. Ford him
2 CONC self says "Records of St. Gwaldys (sic) date back to the 11th century. She is generally considered legendary."
2 CONT
2 CONT The profile mixed two woman as Gwladys Gwawr ferch Brychan. Brychan was born c. 400, so any child of his should be born within the first half of the 5th century. But a look at Bartrum's Early Tables 9 ( https://www.geni.com/docume
2 CONC nts/view?doc_id=6000000173392948868 ) & 27 ( https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173393115923 ) clearly show that they were 2 separate women. Gwladys, with Bartrum penciling a date of c. 460, after setting her at Gen
2 CONC  5 (c.470) was married to Gwynllyw ap Glywys (this profile had her erroneously married to Elidir Lydanwyn) while Gwawr was married to that Elidyr Lydanwyn. Elidir falls c. 485, so we would expect his wife to be at c. 495-500.
2 CONT While it is remotely possible that a man at this time would live to father children at an advanced age, the dates for both woman (Darrell Wolcott charts Gwladys at c. 490 in "Meirion Meirionydd, 'Grandson' of Cunedda" ( http://www
2 CONC .ancientwalesstudies.org/id3.html ) and has Gwynllyw at c.475, make it obvious that Brychan was not the father of these women, nor was Prawst their mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolcott charts Gwladys as a daughter of Brychan II ap Rhain ap Brychan. Likewise, Gwawr would fall as a daughter of Brychan II as well. (Steven Ferry, March 3, 2024.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Saint Gwladys/ Saint Gwawr (Latin: Claudia; English: Gladys),
2 CONT Princess of Brycheiniog Wales,
2 CONT Queen-Consort of Gwynllwg Wales===
2 CONT (Born c.AD 460)
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess Gwladys was the eldest - and best attested - daughter of the saintly Irish-immigrant Saint Brychan King of Brycheiniog in Wales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT With her countless brothers and sisters, she was raised at the Royal & Christian Court at Garthmadrun Talgarth, where she grew into a beautiful young woman. Before long she came to the notice of some of the most eligible bachelors
2 CONC  around, particularly Brycheiniog's menacing neighbour King Gwynllyw Farfog "Woolos the Bearded" of Gwynllwg Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Gwynllyw sent envoys to King Brychan requesting the hand of his daughter in marriage, but the saintly king sent them away. Gwynllyw was a rough pagan warrior king, quite unsuitable for his delicate offspring. Gwynllyw, howeve
2 CONC r, was not so easily put off and decided he would take Gwladys by force. With three hundred men to help him, he made a daring raid on Brycheiniog and made off with Princess Gwladys. King Brychan pursued him as far as Fochriw where
2 CONC  the two were accosted by their High-King, Arthur. Struck by the lady's beauty, this historic Arthur was, at first, tempted to take her for himself; but his fellows persuaded him to support King Gwynllyw's cause, and King Brychan 
2 CONC was eventually brought around to it.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Gwynllyw's Queen-Consort, Gwladys reigned with her husband as a pious and wise monarch, tempering his often rash behaviour. They became the parents of Cadoc (later Saint Cadoc the Wise), Eigion, Cyfyw, Maches, and Glywys. Cadog
2 CONC  - if not all the children - was raised as a Catholic by St. Tathyw at Caerwent, probably at his mother's insistence, and he later converted his father to Catholicism. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In her last years, Queen Gwladys lived apart from her husband as a hermit in Pencarnau and, latterly, at the Capel Wladus in Gelligaer. Here she was buried, and a Celtic cross slab found there is thought to be her memorial, and ca
2 CONC n now be seen in the Gelligaer parish church. Since her death, she has been revered as a Saint. Her feast day is the same as her husband - March 29th.
2 CONT
2 CONT Records of St. Gwaldys date back to the 11th century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT REFERENCE: 
2 CONT https://www.earlybritishkingdoms.com/bios/gwladys.html 
2 CONT
