1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173393013932 (February 4, 2023; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Early Ceredigion and its Rulers; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id158.html. (Steven Ferry, September 11, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Foundations of 'The Men of the North' - Part 2; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id279.html. (Steven Ferry, July 9, 2021.)
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Saint David''' (''c''. 500–589) (Welsh: Dewi Sant) was a Welsh church official during the 6th century; he was later regarded as a saint and as the patron saint of Wales. David was a native of Wales, and a relatively large amoun
2 CONC t of information is known about his life. However, his birth date is still uncertain, as suggestions range from 462 to 512.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents: [http://www.geni.com/people/Sandde-ap-Cedig/6000000011211343794 Sandde ap Cedig] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Non-ferch-Gynyr/6000000011211495734 St. Non verch Cynyr]
2 CONT
2 CONT == Early life ==
2 CONT David became a pupil of Saint [http://www.geni.com/people/Illtud-ap-Bigan/6000000011125876788 Illtud] at Llanilltud Fawr like Saints [http://www.geni.com/people/Samson-ap-Amwn/6000000011124108047 Samson of Dol], [http://www.geni.c
2 CONC om/people/Gildas-ap-Caw/6000000000437023603 Gildas] and St. Paul Aurelian.  Rhygyfarch, the late 11th century author of the saint's life story (see below), wrote that David was the son of ''sanctus rex ceredigionis'', where ''Sanc
2 CONC tus'' has been interpreted as a proper name and its owner honoured by Welsh Christians as [http://www.geni.com/people/Sandde-ap-Cedig/6000000011211343794 King Sandde of Ceredigion]. However, this Latin phrase can equally well mean
2 CONC  simply "holy king of Ceredigion". The king of Ceredigion around the time of David's birth would have been [http://www.geni.com/people/Usai-ap-Ceredig/6000000003827288095 Usai]. According to Rhygyfarch, Sandde was his brother, so 
2 CONC probably only a king of part of Ceredigion. They were sons of [http://www.geni.com/people/Ceredig-ap-Cunedda/6000000002665402337 King Ceredig], founder of Ceredigion. The saint was conceived through violence and his poor mother, [
2 CONC http://www.geni.com/people/Non-ferch-Gynyr/6000000011211495734 Saint Non], the daughter of [http://www.geni.com/people/Cynyr-o-Caer-Gawch-yn-Mynyw/6000000011198844738 Lord Cynyr of Caer Goch] (in Pembrokeshire), gave birth to him 
2 CONC on a cliff top during a violent storm. The site is marked by the Chapel of St Non. David was educated at what is usually taken to be Whitland in Carmarthenshire under Saint Paulinus of Wales and was baptised by St. Ailbe, Bishop o
2 CONC f Emly.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Monasticism ==
2 CONT He became renowned as a teacher and preacher, founding monastic settlements and churches in Wales, Dumnonia and Brittany in a period when neighbouring tribal regions (that were to be overrun by Anglo-Saxon or Frankish tribes over 
2 CONC the following three hundred years) were still mostly pagan. He rose to a bishopric, and presided over two synods, as well as going on pilgrimages to Jerusalem (where he was anointed as an archbishop by the Patriarch) and Rome. St 
2 CONC David's Cathedral stands on the site of the monastery he founded in the 'Glyn Rhosyn' valley, in Pembrokeshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Monastic Rule of David prescribed that monks had to pull the plough themselves without draught animals; to drink only water; to eat only bread with salt and herbs; and to spend the evenings in prayer, reading and writing. No p
2 CONC ersonal possessions were allowed: to say "my book" was an offence. He lived a simple life and practiced asceticism, teaching his followers to vegetarianism|refrain from eating meat or drinking beer. His symbol, also the symbol of 
2 CONC Wales, is the leek. This is questioned by some authorities and largely comes from reference in Shakespeare's Henry V, VI 1) ; his emblem is a dove.
2 CONT
2 CONT The best-known miracle associated with Saint David is said to have taken place when he was preaching in the middle of a large crowd at the Synod of Llanddewi Brefi. When those at the back complained that they could not see or hear
2 CONC  him, the ground on which he stood is reputed to have risen up to form a small hill so that everyone had a good view. A white dove was seen settling on his shoulder—a sign of God's grace and blessing. John Davies notes that one ca
2 CONC n scarcely "conceive of any miracle more superfluous" in that part of Wales—a more mundane version of this story is that he simply recommended that the synod participants move to the hilltop. Though miraculously producing a platfo
2 CONC rm on which to preach is not irrelevant. In works of art, David is frequently shown with a dove on his shoulder. The village of Llanddewi Brefi is said to stand on the spot where the miracle occurred.
2 CONT
2 CONT The document that contains much of the traditional tales about David is ''Buchedd Dewi'', a hagiography written by Rhygyfarch in the late 11th century. Rhygyfarch claimed it was based on documents found in the cathedral archives. 
2 CONC Modern historians are sceptical of some of its claims. One of Rhygyfarch's aims was that his document could establish some independence for the Welsh church, which was risking losing its independence following the Norman invasion 
2 CONC of England in 1066. It is significant that David is said to have denounced Pelagianism during the incident before the ground rose beneath him. Rhygyfarch's Life states that David was anointed as an archbishop by the Patriarch in J
2 CONC erusalem, a position Rhygyfarch said was confirmed at the Synod of Llanddewi Brefi by popular acclaim. This claim of St David's Metropolitan Status as an archbishopric (and thus of the same status as Canterbury) was later supporte
2 CONC d by Bernard, Bishop of St David's, Geoffrey of Monmouth, and Gerald of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Connections to Glastonbury===
2 CONT Rhygyfarch stated that Glastonbury Abbey was amongst the many churches David founded. Around forty years later William of Malmesbury believing the Abbey was older than this, said that David visited Glastonbury intending only to re
2 CONC dedicate the Abbey, as well as to donate a travelling altar including a great sapphire. He had a vision of Jesus, who said that "the church had been dedicated long ago by Himself in honour of His Mother, and it was not seemly that
2 CONC  it should be re-dedicated by human hands". So David instead commissioned an extension to be built to the abbey, east of the Old Church. (The dimensions of this extension given by William were verified archaeologically in 1921). O
2 CONC ne manuscript indicates that a sapphire altar was among the items Henry VIII confiscated from the abbey at its dissolution a thousand years later. There are unverifiable indications that the sapphire may now be among the Crown Jew
2 CONC els.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Death ==
2 CONT It is claimed that David lived for over 100 years, and he died on a Tuesday 1 March (now St David's Day). It is generally accepted that this was around 590, making the actual year 589. The monastery is said to have been 'filled wi
2 CONC th angels as Christ received his soul.' His last words to his followers were in a sermon on the previous Sunday. Rhygyfarch transcribes these as 'Be joyful, and keep your faith and your creed. Do the little things that you have se
2 CONC en me do and heard about. I will walk the path that our fathers have trod before us.' 'Do the little things in life' ('Gwnewch y pethau bychain mewn bywyd') is today a very well-known phrase in Welsh.
2 CONT
2 CONT David was buried at St David's Cathedral at St David's, Pembrokeshire, where his shrine was a popular place of pilgrimage throughout the Middle Ages. During the 10th and 11th centuries the Cathedral was regularly raided by Vikings
2 CONC  who removed the shrine from the church and stripped off the precious metal adornments. In 1275 a new shrine was constructed, the ruined base of which remains to this day (see photo),  which was originally surmounted by an ornamen
2 CONC tal wooden canopy with murals of St David, [http://www.geni.com/people/St-Patrick-of-Ireland/6000000008173864486 St Patrick] and St Denis of France.  The relics of St David and [http://www.geni.com/people/Justinian-I/6000000003645
2 CONC 853347 St Justinian] were kept in a portable casket on the stone base of the shrine.  It was at this shrine that Edward I came to pray in 1284. During the reformation Bishop Barlow (1536–48), a staunch Protestant, stripped the shr
2 CONC ine of its jewels and confiscated the relics of David and Justinian.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Reputation==
2 CONT David's popularity in Wales is shown by the Armes Prydein Vawr c.930, a popular prophetic poem in which the poet prophesied that in the future, when all might seem lost, the Cymry (the Welsh people) would unite to follow David as 
2 CONC their leader: A lluman glân Dewi a ddyrchafant (And they will raise the pure banner of Dewi) and defeat the English.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike many contemporary 'saints' of Wales, David was officially recognised by the Vatican by Pope Callixtus II in 1120, thanks to the work of Bernard, Bishop of St David's.   Music for his Liturgy of the Hours has been edited by 
2 CONC O.T. Edwards in ''Matins, Lauds and Vespers for St David’s Day: the Medieval Office of the Welsh Patron Saint in National Library of Wales MS 20541 E'' (Cambridge, 1990)
2 CONT
2 CONT David's life and teachings have inspired a choral work by Welsh composer Karl Jenkins, ''Dewi Sant''. It is a seven-movement work that is best known for the classical crossover series Adiemus, which intersperses movements reflecti
2 CONC ng the themes of David's last sermon with those drawing from three Psalms. An oratorio by another Welsh composer Arwel Hughes, also entitled ''Dewi Sant'', was composed in 1950.
2 CONT
2 CONT == References ==
2 CONT *''[http://www.worldcat.org/title/vitae-sanctorum-britanniae-et-genealogiae/oclc/2040695 Vitae Sanctorum Britanniae et Genealogiae]'' (Latin), ed. [[Arthur Wade-Evans|Arthur W. Wade-Evans]]. [[Cardiff]]: [[University of Wales Pres
2 CONC s]], 1944.
2 CONT * [http://books.google.com/books?id=XeVNjavhDpIC&pg=PA198&lpg=PA198&dq=%22David+the+Briton%22&source=bl&ots=w59OVmy30v&sig=ijj1jrpl9yjK9ujt-z_b8zDR4K4&hl=en&ei=6yP0TKKpKoOdlge55OWfDQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=2&ved=0CBw
2 CONC Q6AEwAQ#v=onepage&q=%22David%20the%20Briton%22&f=false St. David the Briton], in ''St. Joseph of Arimathea at Glastonbury Or the Apostolic Church of Britain'' by Lionel Smithett Lewis, page 198
2 CONT * [http://books.google.com/books?id=IJ09AAAAYAAJ&pg=PA102&lpg=PA102&dq=%22now+from+him+called+St.+David's%22&source=bl&ots=R_EKkyh5ES&sig=Wl1wO783Yu7jaVizMoro8gUD_zU&hl=en&ei=Tij0TPzxBISclgeRkdH4DA&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&re
2 CONC snum=1&ved=0CBMQ6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=%22now%20from%20him%20called%20St.%20David's%22&f=false St. David's Day] section of ''Observations on the popular antiquities of Great Britain: chiefly chiefly illustrating the origin of our vulg
2 CONC ar customs, ceremonies and superstitions'', Volume 1 (Google Books facsimile) by [[John Brand]], page 102
2 CONT * [http://www.archive.org/stream/observationsonpo01branuoft/observationsonpo01branuoft_djvu.txt St. David's Day] section of ''Observations on popular antiquities, chiefly illustrating the origin of our vulgar customs, ceremonies a
2 CONC nd superstitions : Arranged and rev., with additions'', Volume 1 (ASCII text) by [[John Brand]]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==External links==
2 CONT *[http://www.wales-calling.com/culture/st-david.htm St David &mdash; patron saint of Wales]
2 CONT *[http://saints.sqpn.com/saint-david-of-wales/ Saints SQPN.com » Saint David of Wales]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/176869552/david-of_wales
2 CONT
2 CONT St David of Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT St David of Wales (6th c.), also called Dewi Sant by the Welsh, was a missionary priest, Celtic monk, archbishop, miracle-worker, and the founder of many monasteries in Wales and western England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was descended from Welsh royalty from Caerfai, Pembrokeshire, and in medieval times many believed he was the nephew of the famed King Arthur. His great leadership abilities gave him influence over many Church affairs. In the do
2 CONC zen monasteries he founded he established strict asceticism modeled after the early desert hermits. St David is often depicted standing on a mound with a dove on his shoulder. According to legend, one day while preaching a dove re
2 CONC sted on his shoulder, and the earth rose to lift him above the crowd so that all could hear him speak.
2 CONT
2 CONT During a battle with the Saxons, St David advised the Welsh soldiers to wear leeks in their hats to distinguish themselves from their enemy; this is the origin of the leek as an emblem of Wales. St David is one of the great saints
2 CONC  of the 6th century whose work helped to establish Christianity in Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though the exact date of his death is not certain, tradition holds that he died on March 1, 589 in Mynyw (now St Davids). He is the patron saint of Wales, and his feast, "St David's Day," is a popular cultural celebration. The Cat
2 CONC hedral of St David's in Pembrokeshire was built over his remains and became a pilgrimage destination for centuries. St David of Wales' feast day is March 1st.
2 CONT ∼
2 CONT St. Dewi, Bishop of Mynyw
2 CONT (Welsh: Dewydd; Latin: Davidus; English: David)
2 CONT The life-story and legends of St. David are largely based on his biography written by one Rhygyfarch in the late 11th century. He is generally accepted as having been the son of a lady of noble Irish birth living in Dyfed. Lady No
2 CONC n by name, she had taken on a religious life, joining the convent at Ty Gwyn near Whitesands Bay. However, her beauty brought her to the attention of Sandde, a prince of the adjoining Kingdom of Ceredigion, while he was travelling
2 CONC  nearby. His advances were, of course, vehemently rejected but the Royal lord would not take no for an answer and forced his passions upon the unfortunate Non.
2 CONT
2 CONT The poor girl fell pregnant with the future St. David: a man of such holiness that even from the womb he, apparently, performed miracles. For an old story tells how, during her pregnancy, Non entered a certain church to listen the
2 CONC  preaching of the local priest - he is said to have been St. Gildas but he was somewhat younger than David - and immediately the man was struck dumb. Because her child was soon to excel all religious teachers, the cleric found him
2 CONC self unable to continue whilst in the great man's presence.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was eventually born in the middle of a violent storm at Caerfai, on the coast just south of Mynyw (St. Davids), where a ruined chapel still marks the very spot. Traditionally, this was around AD 462, though recent work by Carne
2 CONC y suggests AD 487 to be a more likely date. Non named her son, Dewidd, but he was commonly called Dewi from the local Dyfed pronunciation. David is an English version taken from the Latin, Davidus. He was brought up, by his mother
2 CONC , in Henfeynyw (Vetus Rubus) near Aberaeron and, at a young age, was baptised by his maternal cousin, St. Eilfyw. David may have been educated by St. Colman of Dromore, but this seems unlikely.
2 CONT
2 CONT David was greatly attracted to the Welsh Church and, when he became a man, he was soon ordained a priest. He travelled to the island of Wincdi-Lantquendi (possibly Whitland) in order to study under St. Paulinus of Wales. He stayed
2 CONC  there for at least ten years, but is also said to have studied under St. Illtud at Llanilltud Fawr (Llantwit Major) around this time. David was a star pupil and even cured Paulinus of his blindness.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our saint then began to travel the country, evangelising as he went. He is said to have founded twelve monasteries in Southern Wales, though many of these are erroneous later claimants. PC Bartrum suggests that possible genuine fo
2 CONC undations may have included Glasgwm (Elfael), Colfa (Elfael), Llangyfelach (Gwyr), Llanarthne (Ystrad Tywi) and Betws (Ystrad Tywi). He also visited the court of King Proprius of Ergyng - probably King Peibio Clafrog - and cured h
2 CONC is blindness too.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, David returned to Henfeynyw where he met up with his relation, Bishop Gwestlan. The two were neighbours and companions for some time, before the Welsh patron moved on to nearby Rhoson Uchaf (Rosina Vallis) near Mynyw (
2 CONC St. Davids). He was accompanied by a number of disciples, including Aeddan, Teilo and Ysfael, and together they founded the monastery of Mynyw (St. Davids). An Irish chieftain, named Bwya, living at nearby Castell Penlan, was not 
2 CONC best pleased at this invasion of monks and plotted to drive them out. His wife sent her maidservants to bathe naked in the River Alun and tempt David and his followers, but the clerics were far from impressed. Misfortune soon befe
2 CONC ll the Irish couple and David was able to settle down without further harassment.
2 CONT
2 CONT By this time, David's fame as a spiritual leader was becoming widespread throughout Britain. He became known as 'the Waterman' - David Aquaticus (Dewi Dyfyrwr) - because he encouraged his followers to live drink and bathe in cold 
2 CONC water. He attracted pupils from many walks of life, including retired monarchs like St. Constantine of Dumnonia. From Mynyw (St. Davids), they spread the Word of God, travelling across the country and especially to Ireland. St. Ae
2 CONC ddan crossed the Irish Sea and founded the monastery of Ferns from where a premonition warned him that David was about to be poisoned. He sent his companion, Ysgolan, to save the great saint from assassination; which he did. Other
2 CONC  Irish visitors included Bishop Barre to whom David lent a miraculous horse which carried him home across the sea!
2 CONT
2 CONT David then decided to undertake a pilgrimage to Jerusalem with SS. Teilo and Padarn. It is said that they were there consecrated bishops by the patriarch. Upon his return to Wales, in AD 545, David was persuaded by SS. Deiniol, Bi
2 CONC shop of Bangor Fawr, and Dyfrig, Bishop of Ergyng (and said to be Archbishop of Wales) to attend the Synod of Llandewi Brefi, which had been convened to discuss disipline within the church and to stamp out the Pelagian Heresy. St.
2 CONC  Paulinus of Wales had recommended his old pupil, since his six-foot stature made him ideal for addressing the vast crowds. The story goes that David spoke so eloquently before his peers that a hill miraculously raised up beneath 
2 CONC him. Dyfrig resigned his Archiepiscopate in David's favour; and he moved the cathedral from Caerleon to his own foundation at Mynyw (St. Davids). The elderly St. Gildas is said to have disputed the appointed, but SS. Cadog and Fin
2 CONC nian of Clonard ruled in favour of David. In fact, it is unlikely that an archiepiscopal see existed in Wales at this time, but David's monastery does seem to have eclipsed the influence of the more easterly church. A second synod
2 CONC , of Victoria, was summoned some years later, in AD 569, to re-assert the anti-pelagian decrees agreed at Brefi.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was possibly around this period that David is said to have visited Glastonbury in Somerset. He had learnt of the abbey's great sanctity and wished to dedicate the building. However, upon his arrival, he apparently had a dream i
2 CONC n which the Lord appeared to him and declared that he had already dedicated the church in honour of his mother, St. Mary. So, David decided instead to extend the so-called 'Old Church' erected by St. Joseph of Arimathea and constr
2 CONC ucted a more extensive building to the east.
2 CONT
2 CONT David died at Mynyw (St. Davids) on Tuesday 1st March AD 589 and was buried in his cathedral, where his relics are still venerated to this day.
