2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Eochaidh I Buidhe Of Argyll King Of Dalriada 
2 CONT Weis' "Ancestral Roots. . ." (170:6). Also called king of the Picts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is taken from an Internet posting of Michael R. Davidson of Edinburgh. Scotland, on 23 Oct 1995: 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Eochaid Buid mac Aedan''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eochaid succeeded to the kingship upon his father's death in 606. Adomnan's _Life of Columba_ notes that Columba, (correctly of course) prophesized the death of Eochaid's older brother's, and Eochaid's succession. Eochaid reign ap
2 CONC pears to have been quiet until the end, but in 627, the forces of Dal Riata, led by Eochaid's successor, Connad Cerr, were victorious in a battle in Ireland. Eochaid died in 629, and was succeeded by his second cousin Connad Cerr.
2 CONC  The _Senchus_ records his eight sons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. *Domnall Brecc. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Domnall Dond. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Conall Crandomna, joint king of Dal Riata with Dunchad son of Duban 
2 CONT
2 CONT from 650 until his death in 660. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Conall Becc. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Connad Cerr. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Failbe, killed 629. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Domangart. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Cu-cen-mathair, died 604. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.southing.net/BondFam019%20Web%20Cards/PS02/PS02_128.HTM
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of Picts, Scots and Dal Riata
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on merged profiles, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born circa 567 or 568
2 CONT
2 CONT Died circa 629 or 630 -SPF
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eochaid Buide was king of Dál Riata from around 608 until 629. "Buide" refers to the colour yellow, as in the colour of his hair.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a younger son of Áedán mac Gabráin and became his father's chosen heir upon the death of his elder brothers. Adomnán's Life of Saint Columba has Columba foresee that Eochaid, then a child, will succeed his father in prefere
2 CONC nce to his adult brothers Artúr, Eochaid Find and Domangart.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the last two years of his reign, 627–629, Eochaid was apparently co-ruler with Connad Cerr, who predeceased him. Eochaid was followed by his son Domnall Brecc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eochaid's other sons named by the Senchus fer n-Alban are Conall Crandomna, Failbe (who died at the Battle of Fid Eoin), Cú-cen-máthair (whose death is reported in the Annals of Ulster for 604), Conall Bec, Connad or Conall Cerr (
2 CONC who may be the same person as Connad Cerr who died at Fid Eoin), Failbe, Domangart and Domnall Donn (not the same person as Domnall Donn unless his obituary is misplaced by 45 years like that of Ferchar mac Connaid)
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Fled Dúin na nGéd, Eochaid Buide was the grandfather of Congal Cáech. The story has anachronistic features as it has Eochaid alive at the time of the battle of Mag Rath (securely dated to within a year of 637), bu
2 CONC t it is chronologically feasible that Congal Cáech could have been the son of Eochaid's daughter if the identification of Cú-cen-máthair and the dating of his death is correct.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adomnán, Life of St Columba, tr. & ed. Richard Sharpe. Penguin, London, 1995. ISBN 0-14-044462-9
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History A.D 500–1286, volume 1. Reprinted with corrections. Paul Watkins, Stamford, 1990. ISBN 1-871615-03-8
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Bannerman, John, Studies in the History of Dalriada. Scottish Academic Press, Edinburgh, 1974. ISBN 0-7011-2040-1
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In addition to Domnall Brecc, Eochaid Buide had seven other sons: Domnall Dond, Conall Crandomna,  Conall Becc Connad Cerr, Failbe, Domangart,and Cu-cen-Mathair.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps05/ps05_458.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Eochy I Buide reigned 608-629, The surname Boyd is said to derive from his name. He is also called King of the Picts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The system of succession to kingship amongst the Scots differed from the one we follow today. Now, our monarchs are chosen by a hereditary system known as primogeniture: generally, the eldest son of a deceased king succeeds to the
2 CONC  throne. Primogeniture is relatively straightforward, but has the disadvantage of sometimes resulting in minorities, which occur whenever the heir to the throne is too young to fulfil his duties. A regency has to be established un
2 CONC til the king comes of age, and this often produces political instability. The Scots, in contrast, followed the Celtic law of tanistry. Tanistry requires that a brother or cousin of the king be nominated by him as his successor. Th
2 CONC us, the throne tends to pass between different branches of the royal family. The advantage of this system is that there is always a mature king on the throne. The disadvantage, as the Scots learned throughout their history, is tha
2 CONC t it sometimes leads to assassination and to warfare between competing claimants to the throne. Amongst the kings of Dalriada, the succession alternated between the descendants of Erc, with those of Fergus dominating. In fact, aft
2 CONC er Loarn, the kingship did not pass to this branch of the family until Ferchar Fota, who died in 697. Two sons, and then two grandsons of Ferchar Fota came to the throne, but after this it remained firmly in the hands of the desce
2 CONC ndants of Fergus. At the time of Ferchar Fota's reign, Dalriada was a weakened kingdom; the throne was fleetingly held by the various claimants, and the Scots were dominated by the Picts. Only Selbach of the Tribe of Loarn had a l
2 CONC ong reign of twenty-three years.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eochu succeeded his father Áedán as king of Dál Riata in about 604. Adomnán, who states that he was one of Áedán's younger sons, and was chosen as Áedán's successor by St. Columba, refers to him by the similar name Eochaid ["Echod
2 CONC ius autem Buide post patrem in regnum successit." Adomnán, 1, 9 (p. 33); see the page of Eochaid mac Echach for comments on the two names Eochu and Eochaid]. He was succeeded about 632 by Connad Cerr, who was probably his son (see
2 CONC  below). The Duan Albanach, although it correctly places his reign between the reigns of Áedán and Connad Cerr, gives him an unbelievable 70 year reign [Duan Albanach, 131]. Most of the Latin Lists give him a 16 year reign [Popple
2 CONC ton MS, Lists "F", "I", "K", KKES, 253, 270, 281, 286; cf. 15 years in List "D", ibid, 264]. The number of years has apparently been corrupted at some point, but it is difficult to give a correction, because the chronology of the 
2 CONC annals is especially troublesome during the same period.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eugene IV, King of Dalriada, d. 630, He became King of Dalriada, ca. 608 in Dungad, Scotland 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Aedon, King of Dalriada, d. 608
2 CONT
2 CONT His Gaelic name was Eochu Buide (the Fair).
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Donald IV, King of Dalriada, d. 642 in Strathcarron, Scotland, He became King of Dalriada, ca. 629 in Dungad, Scotland, lost Irish Dalriada 637 
2 CONT
2 CONT Conal Cean Gamha, d. 659 
2 CONT
2 CONT Connad Cear, b. ca. 600
2 CONT
