1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1.         PEADA (-murdered [17 Apr] 656[475]).  William of Malmesbury names (in order) "Weda, Wulfer, Ethelred, Merewald, Mercelin" as the sons of King Penda & his wife[476].  Bede records that "Peada filio Pendan
2 CONC  regis" was baptised by Finan, Bishop of Lindisfarne, at the time of his marriage, dating the event to two years before the death of King Penda[477].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that he succeeded his father in 654[478] as P
2 CONC EADA King of Mercia, but only ruled south of the river Trent under the overlordship of Oswiu King of Northumbria, his father-in-law.  That part of Mercia north of the river Trent was effectively annexed by Northumbria after Penda'
2 CONC s defeat.  Finan Bishop of Lindisfarne consecrated Diuma in 654 as bishop of a see comprising Mercia, Middle Anglia and Lindsay.  Bede records that "Peada" was killed was killed in spring [656] "by the treachery, as is said, of hi
2 CONC s wife"[479].  After his death, Oswiu King of Northumbria became ruler of the whole of Mercia until 657 when Peada's brother Wulfhere was proclaimed king.  '''m (653) ALHFLÆD [Alehfleda], illegitimate daughter of OSWIU King of Ber
2 CONC nicia [Northumbria] & his mistress --- (-after 656).  Bede records that "Peada filio Pendan regis" married "Osuiu…filiam eius Alchfledam" on condition that he accepted Christainity[480].  William of Malmesbury states that Peada ma
2 CONC rried the (unnamed) daughter of King Oswiu "on condition of renouncing his idols and embracing Christianity" and that Peada's death was "hastened as they say by the intrigues of his wife"[481].  The primary source which confirms t
2 CONC hat she was illegitimate has not yet been identified.  Bede records that "Peada" was killed was killed in spring [656] "by the treachery, as is said, of his wife"[482].'''
2 CONT *https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#_Toc389126246
2 CONT
2 CONT OSWIU, son of ÆTHELFRITH King of Bernicia & his wife Ucha of Deira ([610/11]-15 Feb 670, bur Whitby Abbey).  ...He succeeded his brother in 641 as OSWIU King of Bernicia. ..  Following the death of his nephew Æthelwald, he was acc
2 CONC epted as King of Deira, becoming OSWIU King of Northumbria.
2 CONT King Oswiu had one illegitimate daughter by Mistress (1):
2 CONT
2 CONT >6.          '''ALHFLÆD [Alehfleda].  Bede records that "Peada filio Pendan regis" married "Osuiu…filiam eius Alchfledam" on condition that he accepted Christainity[980].  William of Malmesbury states that Peada married the (unnam
2 CONC ed) daughter of King Oswiu "on condition of renouncing his idols and embracing Christianity" and that Peada's death was "hastened as they say by the intrigues of his wife"[981].  The primary source which confirms that she was ille
2 CONC gitimate has not yet been identified.  She betrayed her husband who was murdered as a result of the plot[982].  m (653) PEADA of Mercia, son of PENDA King of Mercia & his wife Cynewise (-murdered 656[983]).  He succeeded his fathe
2 CONC r 654 as PEADA King of Mercia, but only ruled south of the river Trent under the overlordship of Oswiu King of Northumbria, his father-in-law.''' 
2 CONT * https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20AngloSaxon%20&%20Danish%20Kings.htm#AlhflaedNorthumbriaMPeadaMercia
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT *Alchfled of Northumbria
2 CONT
2 CONT father:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Oswy King of Northumbria
2 CONT
2 CONT born about 0612
2 CONT
2 CONT died 15 February 0669/70
2 CONT
2 CONT mother:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Riemmelth (Rhiainmelt) verch Rhoeth
2 CONT
2 CONT siblings:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfrith of Northumbria
2 CONT
2 CONT spouse:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Peda of Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Koneswitha of Mercia
2 CONT
2 CONT biographical and/or anecdotal:
2 CONT
2 CONT notes or source:
2 CONT
2 CONT ancestry.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Bede, describing Peada as "an excellent youth, and most worthy of the title and person of a king", wrote that he sought to marry Alchflaed, the daughter of King Oswiu of Northumbria; Oswiu, however, made this conditional upon Pead
2 CONC a's baptism and conversion to Christianity, along with the Middle Angles (Peada was, at this time, still a pagan, like his father). Bede says that Peada eagerly accepted conversion:
2 CONT
2 CONT When he heard the preaching of truth, the promise of the heavenly kingdom, and the hope of resurrection and future immortality, he declared that he would willingly become a Christian, even though he should be refused the virgin; b
2 CONC eing chiefly prevailed on to receive the faith by King Oswy's son Aifrid, who was his relation and friend, and had married his sister Cyneburga, the daughter of King Penda.
2 CONT
