2 TITL Ancestry of Cynan Tyndaethwy of Gwynedd
1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT REFN: 7027
1 NOTE He was the last King of the Britons. He was killed in the plague of 664.
1 NOTE [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Kraentzler 1748; History of Morgan Family.   King of Gwynedd 654. "Known as Cadwaladr Fendigaid, the Blessed. Recovered the throne of Gwynedd after it had been usurped by Cadafel and was the last King of a united Britain.
2 CONC  His standard was the symbol of a red dragon on a green background, which remains the flag of Wales to this day. (As a tribute to his Welsh heritage, Henry Tudor carried this flag during the War of the Roses. His defeat of King Ri
2 CONC chard III at the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485 established the Tudor dynasty of England with Henry as King Henry VII).   "Cadwaladr led the fight against the Saxon invaders and Merlin, in the Tales of King Arthur, prophesied th
2 CONC at 'Cadwaladr, the Blessed, would return after many days and drive the cursed Szson from the land.' Cadwaladr died of the plague in 688. His son was Idwal Iwrch."
2 CONT    K: Cadwallader (Catguallart), Prince of Britain.   An AF record calls him "The Blessed" and King of the Britons.
2 CONT Cadwaladr of Gwynedd was the son of Cadwallon, King of the Britons, who, as an ally of King Penda of Mercia, defeated Edwin of Northumbria in 633.  His son Cadwaladr died of plague in 664. [Sources 2 and 3]
2 CONT King of Gwynedd, 654.  "Known as Cadwaladr Fendigaid, the Blessed. Recovered the throne of Gwynedd after it had been usurped by Cadafel and was the last King of a united Britain.  His standard was the symbol of a red dragon on a g
2 CONC reen background, which remains the flag of Wales to this day.  (As a tribute to his Welsh heritage, Henry Tudor carried this flag during the War of the Roses.  His defeat of King Richard III at the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485
2 CONC  established the Tudor dynasty of England with Henry as King Henry VII). Cadwaladr led the fight against the Saxon invaders and Merlin, in the Tales of King Arthur, prophesied that 'Cadwaladr, the Blessed, would return after many 
2 CONC days and drive the cursed Saxon from the land.'  Cadwaladr died of the plague in 688.  His son was Idwal Iwrch."  [Source 1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
2 CONT   1.  Morgan, Dennis.  _A History of the Morgan Family_.  2.  The American Genealogist (TAG), Vol. 41, No. 4, Oct. 1965, "The Paternal
2 CONT       Ancestry of Rhodri Mawr".  3.  The American Genealogist (TAG), Vol. 41, No. 1, Jan. 1965, "The House of
2 CONT       Cunedda".
2 CONT             Cadwaladr Fendigaid ap Cadwallon.
2 CONT Cadwaladr of Gwynedd was the son of Cadwallon, King of the Britons, who, as an ally of King Penda of Mercia, defeated Edwin of Northumbria in 633.  His son Cadwaladr died of plague in 664. [Sources 2 and 3]
2 CONT King of Gwynedd, 654.  "Known as Cadwaladr Fendigaid, the Blessed. Recovered the throne of Gwynedd after it had been usurped by Cadafel and was the last King of a united Britain.  His standard was the symbol of a red dragon on agr
2 CONC een background, which remains the flag of Wales to this day.  (As a tribute to his Welsh heritage, Henry Tudor carried this flag during the War of the Roses.  His defeat of King Richard III at the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485 
2 CONC established the Tudor dynasty of England with Henry as King Henry VII). Cadwaladr led the fight against the Saxon invaders and Merlin, in the Tales of King Arthur, prophesied that 'Cadwaladr, the Blessed, would return after many d
2 CONC ays and drive the cursed Saxon from the land.'  Cadwaladr died of the plague in 688.  His son was Idwal Iwrch."  [Source 1]
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES:
