2 CONT  Rhodri the Bald & Grey was the son of King Idwal Iwch of Gwynedd and his wife, Princess Angharad of Brittany. There is an old story that Rhodri invaded or conquered Dumnonia - possibly during his youth - but he was forced to retu
2 CONC rn to North Wales by the Saxons. The year of his accession in Gwynedd is variously given as AD 712, 720, 722 or 730. The earliest date seems most likely. He married an Irish princess, Margaret ferch Duplory and had one son, Cynan 
2 CONC Dindaethwy. He died in AD 754, when the throne was seized by Caradog ap Meirion.  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ID: I104149 Name: RHODRI MOLWYNOG AP IDWAL Prefix: KING OF GWYNEDD Sex: M Birth: 690 CE in , , Wales Death: 754 CE 1 Event: KING OF GWYNEDD Reign Between 712 and 754 CE Change Date: 9 Nov 2008 at 13:02 Note: Alias: the Bald and Gr
2 CONC ey/
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: IDWAL LWRCH AP CADWALADR b: 664 CE in , , Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Spouse Unknown Married: Change Date: 13 Jan 2009 Children
2 CONT  Cynan Dindaethwy Ap Rhodri b: 745 CE
2 CONT  Hywel Ap Rhodri b: Abt 745 CE
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Abbrev: Sutton Folk Family Tree 3175463.ged Title: Sutton Folk Family Tree  Sutton Folk Family Tree 3175463.ged Author: Folk, Linda Sutton  Publication: www.worldconnect.rootsweb.com
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Rhodri ap Idwal (c. 690-c. 754; reigned from c. 720) (Latin: Rodericus; English: Roderick), also known as Rhodri Molwynog ('the Bald and Gray'), and by some contemporary historians as Rhodri I, was a king of Gwynedd, referred to a
2 CONC s a king of the Britons by the Annals of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Idwal Iwrch and his wife, Princess Angharad of Brittany, comparatively little is known of Rhodri's life or accomplishments. There is even some debate as to when he actually assumed the throne of Gwynedd, with the years 
2 CONC 712, 720, 722 or 730 being the most probable candidates. Legend suggests that Rhodri successfully invaded and occupied Dumnonia for a time, before being expelled by the Saxons. This story may indeed be apocryphal, and others have 
2 CONC suggested that Rhodri instead focused on strengthening the island of Anglesey, which, by this time, had become the stronghold of the kingdom. This argument is based on the understanding that Æthelbald, king of Mercia and self-styl
2 CONC ed Bretwalda, was continuing to press the kingdoms of Wales, and that Rhodri would have been far more likely to have been forced to adopt a defensive, rather than offensive, policy in dealing with incursions from Mercia and other 
2 CONC Anglo-Saxon principalities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri married Margaret ferch Duplory, an Irish princess, who bore him a son by the name of Cynan Dindaethwy. Because of the limited reliable information surrounding this period in Welsh history, it is not entirely clear who succe
2 CONC eded Rhodri upon his death, although the most likely candidate seems to be Caradog ap Meirion, a distant cousin, and not his son, who would ascend to the throne only after the death of Caradog.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
