2 CONT  Rhodri (Molwynog) ap Idwal   
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri, known as Molwynog, was born in 0690 in Wales.1   Molwynog's father was Idwal (Iwrch) ap Cadwaladr and his mother was Afadda verch Alain.  His paternal grandparents were Cadwaladr (The Blessed, Fendigaid) ap Cadwallon and <
2 CONC Unknown>; his maternal grandparents were King of Brittany Alain II Hir and <Unknown>.  He was an only child.  He died at the age of 64 in 0754.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT   Cynan (Dindaethwy) ap Rhodri
2 CONT
2 CONT Cynan, known as Dindaethwy, was born about 0745 in Wales.2   He died in 0817.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Rhodri Molwynog ap Idwal (English: Rhodri the Bald and Grey, son of Idwal) was King of Gwynedd (reigned ? – 754). This era in the history of Gwynedd was not notable, and given the lack of reliable information available, serious hi
2 CONC stories of Wales, such that as by Davies, do not mention Rhodri, while that of Lloyd mentions his name in passing only to quote the year of his death given in the Annales Cambriae.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Rhodri Molwynog ("Rhodri the Bald and Grey"; died c. 754), also known as Rhodri ab Idwal ("Rhodri son of Idwal") was an 8th-century king of Gwynedd. He was listed as a King of the Britons by the Annals of Wales. This era in the hi
2 CONC story of Gwynedd is very obscure and, given the lack of reliable information available, several serious histories of medieval Wales—including John Davies's[1]—do not mention Rhodri at all, while others—including John Lloyd's[2]—me
2 CONC ntion him only in passing, quoting the undated entry of the Annals of Wales recording his death.[3] Phillimore's reconstruction places the entry in the year 754.[3] The Annals do not mention the death of an earlier king within a r
2 CONC easonable time frame, so the date that he became king is not known, nor is the name of his predecessor. Rhodri's name also appears in genealogies such as those in Jesus College MS. 20 (where he is described as the son of Idwal Iwr
2 CONC ch son of Cadwaladr Fendigiad[4]) and the Harleian genealogies (where he is described as the son of Tutgual son of Cadwaladr[5]). It remains, however, unclear to what extent the genealogies at that point were recording the lineage
2 CONC  of the Cuneddion dynasty regardless of their rule or recording the rulers regardless of their connection to the main branch of the dynasty. The Annals of Wales mention a war in Cornwalll around 722 without giving the names of the
2 CONC  individuals involved.[6] The Brut y Saeson says that in 721 there was "an extensive war between Rhodri Molwynawg and the Saxons in Cornwall".[7] The Brut Aberpergwm also recorded this event but, while it was accepted for a time b
2 CONC y the editors of the Myvyrian Archaiology, Thomas Stephens has since shown that it was one of Iolo Morganwg's many forgeries.[8] The Rotri appearing in the Annals has sometimes been misidentified as a ruler of Alt Clut (modern Dum
2 CONC barton Rock), the Brythonic kingdom later known as Strathclyde.
