1 NOTE {geni:about_me} His famous but spurious son can be found at https://www.geni.com/people/Eochaid-IV-The-Venomous-king-of-D%C3%A1l-Riata/6000000013311406265
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%81ed_Find
2 CONT
2 CONT Domnall Brecc =>  Domangart mac Domnaill =>  Eochaid mac Domangairt =>  Eochaid mac Eochaid =>  Áed Find mac Eochaid =>  Eochaid mac Áeda Find =>  Alpín mac Eochaid =>  Cináed mac Ailpín
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Áed Find''' (Áed the White) or '''Áed mac Echdach''' (before 736&ndash;778) was king of [[Dál Riata]] (modern western [[Scotland]]). Áed was the son of [[Eochaid mac Echdach]], a descendant of [[Domnall Brecc]] in the main line
2 CONC  of [[Cenél nGabráin]] kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to later genealogies, Áed was the great-grandfather of [[Kenneth I of Scotland|Kenneth MacAlpin]] (Cináed mac Ailpín) who is traditionally counted as the first [[king of Scots]]. This descent ran through Áed's son [[Eoch
2 CONC aid mac Áeda Find]] and Eochaid's son [[Alpín mac Echdach]]. The evidence for the existence of Eochaid and Alpín is late and uncompelling, and shows signs of fabrication in the [[Scotland in the High Middle Ages|High Middle Ages]]
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The [[Annals of Ulster]] in 768 report "Bellum i Fortrinn iter Aedh & Cinaedh": a battle in [[Fortriu]] between Áed and Cináed. This is usually read as meaning Áed Find and the [[Picts|Pictish]] king [[Ciniod I of the Picts|Ciniod
2 CONC  I]], who is called "Cinadhon" in the notice of his death in 775. The [[Annals of the Four Masters]], a less reliable source, give a different version, placing this battle in [[Leinster]] and naming the victor as Cináed mac Flainn
2 CONC  of the Uí Failgi and his defeated enemy as one Áed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Áed's death in 778 is noted by the Annals of Ulster. He appears to have been followed as king by his brother [[Fergus mac Echdach]].
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Laws of Áed Eochaid's son" are mentioned by the [[Chronicle of the Kings of Alba]] in the reign of Áed's supposed great-grandson [[Donald I of Scotland|Donald MacAlpin]] (Domnall mac Ailpín): "In his time the [[Gaels]] with t
2 CONC heir king made the rights and laws of the kingdom [that are called the laws] of Áed Eochaid's son, in [[Forteviot]]." What these laws concerned is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==References==
2 CONT ''For primary sources, see also'' '''External links''' ''below''
2 CONT {{refbegin}}
2 CONT * [[Alan Orr Anderson|Anderson, Alan Orr]], ''Early Sources of Scottish History A.D 500&ndash;1286'', volume 1. Reprinted with corrections. Paul Watkins, Stamford, 1990. ISBN 1-871615-03-8
2 CONT * [[John Bannerman (historian)|Bannerman, John]], "The Scottish Takeover of Pictland" in [[Dauvit Broun]] & [[Thomas Owen Clancy]] (eds.) ''Spes Scotorum: Hope of Scots. Saint Columba, Iona and Scotland.'' T & T Clark, Edinburgh, 
2 CONC 1999. ISBN 0-567-08682-2
2 CONT * [[Dauvit Broun|Broun, Dauvit]], ''The Irish Identity of the Kingdom of the Scots.'' Boydell, Woodbridge, 1999. ISBN 0-85115-375-5
2 CONT * Broun, Dauvit, "Pictish Kings 761&ndash;839: Integration with Dál Riata or Separate Development" in Sally M. Foster (ed.), ''The St Andrews Sarcophagus: A Pictish masterpiece and its international connections.'' Four Courts, Dub
2 CONC lin, 1998. ISBN 1-85182-414-6
2 CONT {{refend}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ==External links==
2 CONT *[http://celt.ucc.ie/index.html CELT: Corpus of Electronic Texts] at [http://www.ucc.ie/ University College Cork]
2 CONT **The '''Corpus of Electronic Texts''' includes the ''Annals of Ulster'', ''Tigernach'', ''the Four Masters'' and ''Innisfallen'', the ''Chronicon Scotorum'', the ''Lebor Bretnach'' (which includes the ''Duan Albanach''), Genealog
2 CONC ies, and various Saints' Lives. Most are translated into English, or translations are in progress
2 CONT *[http://historical.library.cornell.edu/cgi-bin/cul.cdl/docviewer?did=cdl360 ''Annals of Clonmacnoise''] at [http://historical.library.cornell.edu/cdl/index.html Cornell]
2 CONT *[http://www.arts.ed.ac.uk/scothist/booklets/sh1/documents-alba.html The Chronicle of the Kings of Alba]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Called Aedh Fionn, the White. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Re-established independence from Pictish overlordship, and the kingship of Dal Riata, when the Picts were defeated by the Strathclyde British in 750. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Succeeded in Kingship by his brother Fergus.
2 CONT
2 CONT 768: at war with Picts
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Aed Find "the White", King of Dalriada, d. 771 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Eugene VII, King of Dalriada, d. 727, He became King of Dalriada, ca. 726 in Dungad, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Spondan
2 CONT
2 CONT His Gaelic name was Aodh Airgneach.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Fergina 
2 CONT
