1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000173393063941 (April 28, 2022; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Legendary Kingdom of Seisyllwg; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id123.html. (Steven Ferry, July 3, 2017.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Royal Family of Ceredigion; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id158.html. (Steven Ferry, July 5, 2017.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Pedigree of the Ancient Lords of Ial; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id93.html. (Steven Ferry, July 14, 2017.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Merfyn Frych's connection to https://www.geni.com/people/Nesta-verch-Cadell/5357588559490061786, which is discussed in the history below, is now considered speculative, having been invented later as part of a larger project to agg
2 CONC randize Rhodri Mawr, Merfyn's son.  Please click through on Nest's profile for links to various aspects of the discussion.
2 CONT ****************
2 CONT
2 CONT Merfyn Frych seized control of Gwynedd in 825 on the death of Hywel ap Rhodri Molwynog, though he may have held power in Anglesey since 818. Merfyn was not a member of the traditional dynasty of Gwynedd, the direct male line of Ma
2 CONC elgwn Gwynedd, and his succession marked the start of a new dynasty. His claim was apparently based on the fact that his mother, Esyllt, was the daughter of Cynan Dindaethwy ap Rhodri and the niece of Hywel ap Rhodri. According to
2 CONC  bardic tradition, Merfyn came "from the land of Manaw", but it is uncertain whether this refers to the Isle of Man ("Ynys Manaw" in Welsh) or to Manaw Gododdin, the area around the Firth of Forth. It would seem likely that it was
2 CONC  the latter on account of the probability he would be a blood relative of Cunedda, the founder of the Gwynedd dynasty, who was a prince of Manaw Gododdin. On the other hand there is an inscription "Crux Guriat" on a cross in the I
2 CONC sle of Man.[1] This cross has been dated to the eighth or ninth century and might possibly refer to Merfyn's father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Merfyn allied himself to the royal house of Powys by marrying Nest, daughter of Cadell ap Brochwel and sister of Cyngen king of Powys. He had a reputation as a patron of scholars; for example the Historia Britonum attributed to Ne
2 CONC nnius is thought to have been written in Gwynedd during his reign, possibly by request of Merfyn himself. A manuscript found at Bamberg gives a further insight into Merfyn's scholarly interests. Irish visitors to his court were gi
2 CONC ven a cryptogram which could only be solved by transposing the letters from Latin into Greek.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite Danish raids, Merfyn was able to maintain his position and on his death in 844 to hand the kingdom over intact to his son Rhodri the Great. He is said to have died in battle, but the circumstances are not recorded. His des
2 CONC cendants came to rule not only Gwynedd but also Powys and Deheubarth and played a major role in Welsh politics until the end of Welsh independence in 1283.
2 CONT
2 CONT Powys was united with Gwynedd when king Merfyn Frych of Gwynedd married princess Nest, the sister of king Cyngen of Powys, the last representative of the Gwertherion dynasty. With the death of Cyngen in 855 Rhodri became king of P
2 CONC owys, having inherited Gwynedd the year before. This formed the basis of Gwynedd's continued claims of overlordship over Powys for the next 443 years.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Battle of Cyfeiliog<br/>
2 CONT https://books.google.com/books?id=Az4LAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA64&lpg=PA64&dq=Battle+of+Cyfeiliog&source=bl&ots=-DHmg2e2dD&sig=Rb7u6DfgOjtYaLoDurDxYhG2EKM&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwirtqS7vt3LAhUI1GMKHfPuAy4Q6AEIHDAA#v=onepage&q=Battle%20of%20Cyf
2 CONC eiliog&f=false<br/>
2 CONT The History of the Princes, the Lords Marcher, and the Ancient Nobility of Powys Fadog, and the Ancient Lords of Arwystli, Cedewen, and Meirionydd, Volume 1, by Jacob Youde William LLoyd. London, 1881. p. 64
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 838 the Battle of Cyfeiliog was fought between Merfyn Ferch and Berthrwyd, King of Mercia; and in this battle Merfyn was slain. In Merfyn's time, the Britons residing in England were obliged to turn Saxons, or quit the country
2 CONC  and their homes in three months."
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Merfyn Frych From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Merfyn Frych ap Gwriad (or Merfyn the Freckled) (died 844) was a King of Gwynedd and possibly also of Powys. He is referred to as King of the Britons in two documents composed at his court: the Historia Brittonum and the Bamberg C
2 CONC ryptogram[1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Merfyn Frych seized control of Gwynedd in 825 on the death of Hywel ap Rhodri Molwynog, though he may have held power in Anglesey since 818. Merfyn was not , the direct male line of Maelgwn Gwynedd, and his succession marked the s
2 CONC tart of a new dynasty. His claim was apparently based on the fact that his mother, Esyllt, was the daughter of Cynan Dindaethwy ap Rhodri and the niece of Hywel ap Rhodri. According to bardic tradition, Merfyn came "from the land 
2 CONC of Manaw", but it is uncertain whether this refers to the Isle of Man ("Ynys Manaw" in Welsh) or to Manaw Gododdin, the area around the Firth of Forth. It would seem likely that it was the latter on account of the probability he w
2 CONC ould be a blood relative of Cunedda, the founder of the Gwynedd dynasty, who was a prince of Manaw Gododdin. On the other hand there is an inscription "Crux Guriat" on a cross in the Isle of Man.[2] This cross has been dated to th
2 CONC e eighth or ninth century and might possibly refer to Merfyn's father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Merfyn allied himself to the royal house of Powys by marrying Nest, daughter of Cadell ap Brochwel and sister of Cyngen king of Powys. He had a reputation as a patron of scholars; for example the Historia Britonum attributed to Ne
2 CONC nnius is thought to have been written in Gwynedd during his reign, possibly by request of Merfyn himself. A manuscript found at Bamberg gives a further insight into Merfyn's scholarly interests. Irish visitors to his court were gi
2 CONC ven a cryptogram which could only be solved by transposing the letters from Latin into Greek.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite Danish raids, Merfyn was able to maintain his position and on his death in 844 to hand the kingdom over intact to his son Rhodri the Great. He is said to have died in battle, but the circumstances are not recorded. His des
2 CONC cendants came to rule not only Gwynedd but also Powys and Deheubarth and played a major role in Welsh politics until the end of Welsh independence in 1283.
2 CONT
