2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri was the son of Merfyn Frych, who had claimed Gwynedd upon the extinction of Cunedda's male line. Rhodri then inherited the realm after his father's death around 844.  Merfyn hailed from "Manaw" which may either refer to the
2 CONC  Isle of Man or Manau, the ancestral homeland of all Gwynedd's kings since Cunedda.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to later genealogies, his mother or grandmother was Nest ferch Cadell of the ruling dynasty in Powys, and Rhodri inherited the kingdom through his uncle Cyngen and then the rule of the southern realms on the death of Gwg
2 CONC on, Rhodri's brother in law.  Although surviving texts of Welsh law expressly forbid inheritance along the maternal line, Nest and Rhodri's supposed inheritance was later used to justify Gwynedd's annexation of Powys after the c. 
2 CONC 855 death of Cyngen ap Cadell in preference to Cyngen's other heirs.
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodri_the_Great
2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT Rhodri the Great (c. 820 – 878 AD) was one of the most prominent and influential rulers in early medieval Wales. His reign marked a critical period in Welsh history, during which he achieved significant political consolidation, mi
2 CONC litary success, and territorial expansion. His power and legacy were felt not only in his lifetime but in the following centuries as his descendants became key figures in Welsh resistance to foreign domination. Here are the major 
2 CONC aspects of his life and legacy:
2 CONT Consolidation of Welsh Kingdoms
2 CONT
2 CONT Before Rhodri’s reign, Wales was fragmented into several smaller kingdoms, including Gwynedd, Powys, and Seisyllwg. Rhodri’s family lineage gave him rights to multiple territories:
2 CONT Gwynedd, which he inherited from his father, Merfyn Frych.
2 CONT Powys, which he gained through his mother’s line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Seisyllwg, added through his marriage to Angharad, daughter of the King of Seisyllwg.
2 CONT By consolidating these regions under his rule, Rhodri is considered one of the first leaders to unite a significant portion of Wales, which would later serve as the foundation for the Welsh identity.
2 CONT Military Leadership and Defense Against Viking Invasions
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri’s era was characterized by increasing Viking raids across the British Isles. He earned widespread acclaim for his ability to defend Wales from these incursions, particularly in 856 AD, when he won a major victory against th
2 CONC e Vikings, killing the Viking leader Horm. His military successes not only safeguarded Welsh lands but also increased his stature as a powerful ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Resistance Against Anglo-Saxon Encroachment==
2 CONT While Rhodri was known for his battles against the Vikings, his reign was also marked by conflicts with the expanding Kingdom of Wessex under Alfred the Great and his predecessors. Rhodri’s territories in Powys faced persistent pr
2 CONC essure from the Anglo-Saxons, and this struggle set the stage for centuries of Anglo-Welsh conflict. He was forced to flee to Ireland at one point due to Anglo-Saxon attacks but returned to resume his rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Death and Legacy==
2 CONT Rhodri died in 878 AD in battle, either against the Vikings or the Anglo-Saxons (historical sources differ). After his death, his kingdom was divided among his sons, which led to a period of fragmentation but also ensured that his
2 CONC  dynasty, the House of Aberffraw, would continue to play a significant role in Welsh politics. His descendants, particularly through his grandson Hywel Dda (Hywel the Good), who codified Welsh laws, shaped Welsh governance for cen
2 CONC turies.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Rhodri’s Influence on Later Welsh Kings==
2 CONT Rhodri’s dynasty became the ruling line of several Welsh kingdoms. His legacy as a unifier and defender against external threats was a touchstone for future rulers. Figures like Llywelyn the Great and Llywelyn ap Gruffudd drew fro
2 CONC m Rhodri’s example as they sought to consolidate Wales under their rule in the 12th and 13th centuries. Rhodri’s legacy was one of both military prowess and political unity, making him a revered figure in Welsh history.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Symbolism and Welsh Identity==
2 CONT In modern Welsh culture, Rhodri Mawr is seen as a symbol of Welsh independence and resistance. His ability to unite disparate kingdoms into a single entity is often viewed as a precursor to the idea of a unified Wales. He also sta
2 CONC nds as a figure of resilience in the face of external threats, be it from Viking invaders or Anglo-Saxon expansion.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Conclusion==
2 CONT Rhodri the Great was more than just a king; he was a pivotal figure in shaping medieval Wales through his military successes, consolidation of power, and the establishment of a dynasty that would influence Welsh history for genera
2 CONC tions. His reign marked the beginning of a more unified Welsh identity, with a legacy that reverberated well beyond his death.
2 CONT https://historiesandcastles.com/historic-figures/rhodri-mawr-rhodri-the-great/?v=0b3b97fa6688
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT Rhodri the Great (in Welsh, Rhodri Mawr; occasionally in English, Roderick the Great) (c. 820–878) was the first ruler of Wales to be called 'Great', and the first to rule most of present-day Wales. He was called King of the Brito
2 CONC ns by the Annals of Ulster. In some later histories, he is referred to as "King of Wales" but he did not rule all of Wales nor was this term used contemporaneously to describe him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Merfyn Frych, King of Gwynedd, and Nest ferch Cadell of the Royal line of Powys, he inherited the Kingdom of Gwynedd on his father's death in 844.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his maternal uncle Cyngen ap Cadell ruler of Powys died on a pilgrimage to Rome in 855 Rhodri inherited Powys. In 872 Gwgon, ruler of Seisyllwg in southern Wales, was accidentally drowned, and Rhodri added his Kingdom to his 
2 CONC domains by virtue of his marriage to Angharad, Gwgon's sister. This made him the ruler of the larger part of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri faced pressure both from the English and increasingly from the Danes, who were recorded as ravaging Anglesey in 854. In 856 Rhodri won a notable victory over the Danes, killing their leader Gorm (sometimes given as Horm). T
2 CONC wo poems by Sedulius Scotus written at the court of Charles the Bald, King of the Western Franks, celebrate the victory of "Roricus" over the Norsemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 876 Rhodri fought another battle against the Norse invaders on Anglesey, after which he had to flee to Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return the following year, he and his son Gwriad were said to have been killed by the English under Alfred the Great, though the precise manner of his death is unknown. When his son, Anarawd ap Rhodri won a victory over the
2 CONC  Mercians a few years later, it was hailed in the annals as "God's vengeance for Rhodri".
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical Event:  Tsar Simeon the Great was the ruler of Bulgaria, who reigned 893 - 927. He was 27 when he took the throne from his brother Vladimir, the son of Prince Boris, who was deposed and possibly blinded by his own fathe
2 CONC r after his attempt to return Bulgaria to paganism. One of the first decisions which he took after taking over the throne was to move the capital of Bulgaria from Pliska to Preslav. 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Lineage & inheritance ==
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Merfyn Frych, King of Gwynedd, and Nest ferch Cadell of the Royal line of Powys, he inherited the Kingdom of Gwynedd on his father's death in 844.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his uncle Cyngen ap Cadell ruler of Powys died on a pilgrimage to Rome in 855 Rhodri inherited Powys. In 872 Gwgon, ruler of Seisyllwg in southern Wales, was accidentally drowned, and Rhodri added his Kingdom to his domains b
2 CONC y virtue of his marriage to Angharad, Gwgon's sister. This made him the ruler of the larger part of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Resistance against Danes ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri faced pressure both from the English and increasingly from the Danes, who were recorded as ravaging Anglesey in 854. In 856 Rhodri won a notable victory over the Danes, killing their leader Gorm (sometimes given as Horm). T
2 CONC wo poems by Sedulius Scotus written at the court of Charles the Bald, King of the Western Franks, celebrate the victory of "Roricus" over the Norsemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 877 Rhodri fought another battle against the Norse invaders on Anglesey, this time being forced to flee to Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Defeat and death ==
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return the following year, he and his son Gwriad were said to have been killed by the English under Alfred the Great, though the precise manner of his death is unknown. When his son, Anarawd ap Rhodri won a victory over the
2 CONC  Mercians a few years later, it was hailed in the annals as "God's vengeance for Rhodri".
2 CONT
2 CONT == Succession ==
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Cadell ap Rhodri conquered Dyfed, which was later joined with Seisyllwg by Rhodri's grandson Hywel Dda to become Deheubarth. Like his grandfather, Hywel would come to rule most of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodri_the_Great Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT He united Gwynedd, Powys & Deheubarth thru war and marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT History: Dinefwr district, Dyfed county, southern Wales. Created in the administrative reorganization of 1974, it covers an area of 375 square miles (971 square km) and rises from an elevation of just over 100 feet (30 m) in the T
2 CONC ywi valley in central Dinefwr to heights of more than 2,000 feet (600 m) in the Black Mountains to the south. Dinefwr district is bordered by the districts of Ceredigion to the north, Brecknock to the east, Lliw Valley and Llanell
2 CONC i to the south, and Carmarthen to the west. The district's town of Llandovery was of strategic importance in Roman times, and the Church of Llanfair (restored 1915) nearby was built within the ramparts of a Roman fort. The ruins o
2 CONC f the 13th-century Dynevor Castle are located just west of Llandeilo. Originally built in AD 876 by the Welsh nobleman Rhodri Mawr, the castle was taken by the English in the 13th century and rebuilt; the Welsh leader Owen Glendow
2 CONC er tried to retake it in 1408 but failed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dinefwr is a rural district in which dairy farming is the main agricultural activity. Both Llandovery and Llandeilo are agricultural market towns, and Llandeilo is also the administrative seat for the district. Dinefwr's limited i
2 CONC ndustry is located in Ammanford, a mining centre situated on an anthracite coalfield surrounded by scenic countryside. Many tourists visit the district as most of its eastern half lies within Brecon Beacons National Park. The Coun
2 CONC ty Agricultural College is in Llandeilo. A highway and a railway extend south from Llandovery through Llandeilo and Ammansford. Pop. (1986 est.) 37,200.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright © 1994-2001 Encyclopædia Britannica, Inc.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT First ruler of Wales to be called 'Great', and the first to rule most of present-day Wales. He is referred to as "King of the Britons" by the Annals of Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri faced pressure both from the English and increasingly from the Danes, who were recorded as ravaging Anglesey in 854. In 856 Rhodri won a notable victory over the Danes, killing their leader Gorm (sometimes given as Horm). T
2 CONC wo poems by Sedulius Scotus written at the court of Charles the Bald, King of the Western Franks, celebrate the victory of "Roricus" over the Norsemen.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 877 Rhodri fought another battle against the Norse invaders on Anglesey, this time being forced to flee to Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return the following year, he and his son Gwriad were said to have been killed by the English under Alfred the Great, though the precise manner of his death is unknown. When his son, Anarawd ap Rhodri won a victory over the
2 CONC  Mercians a few years later, it was hailed in the annals as "God's vengeance for Rhodri".
2 CONT
2 CONT == Family ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri died leaving three sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT His heir, Anarawd ap Rhodri, who became the new king of the britons, taking kingship of the Kingdom of Gwynedd;
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Cadell ap Rhodri, who conquered Dyfed, which was later joined with Seisyllwg by Rhodri's grandson Hywel Dda to become Deheubarth. Like his grandfather, Hywel would come to rule most of Wales; and
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Merfyn ap Rhodri, who became the king of the Kingdom of Powys.
2 CONT
