2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 80. Rhodri ap Merfyn (Rhodri Mawr)
2 CONT
2 CONT Leaders
2 CONT
2 CONT 820 – 878 
2 CONT
2 CONT Warrior, prince and the first Welsh ruler to be called “Great”.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son of Merfyn Frych, Rhodri inherited the principality of Gwynedd on his father"s death in 844, and Powys from his uncle. He proceeded to marry Angharad, daughter of the ruler of Seisyllwg, which he duly inherited on the accid
2 CONC ental death of his brother-in-law in 871. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This enlarged kingdom covered all of northern and central Wales, stretching down to the Gower peninsula in the south. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri"s fame sprang from his success as a warrior after his many battles with the Vikings. Anglesey bore the brunt of the attacks, and it was there in 856 that Rhodri won his great victory over Horn, the leader of the Danes.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was not only from the west that the kingdom of Rhodri was threatened. By becoming the ruler of Powys, his mother"s land, he inherited the old struggle with the kingdom of Mercia and in 878 Rhodri and his son, Gwriad, were kille
2 CONC d in battle against the English. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Apart from these bare facts, little information about Rhodri survives to the present. Yet his prestige was such that to be “of the line of Rhodri” was a powerful factor in later Welsh power struggles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri’s grandson Hywel Dda built on the foundations he had laid, creating the kingdom of Deheubarth. This extensive geographical unit with its common legal system was, arguably, the closest there has ever been to a unified and in
2 CONC dependent Welsh state.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodri_the_Great
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhodri was the son of Merfyn, king of Gwynedd, and Nest of Powys. Through marriage more than conquest, Rhodri was ruler of a realm stretching from Anglesey to the Gower peninsula by the time of his death in 877. Although the exten
2 CONC t of his kingdom would have been enough to make Rhodri's fame, he was more renown as a warrior, a renown that was noted in places as far afield as Ulster and Liege.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodri_Mawr
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.earlybritishkingdoms.com/kingdoms/805.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of Gwynedd from his father, Powys from his mother, Deheubarth from his wife. Nationlist & protagonist of contemporary anti-English poetry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
