1 NAME Tryffin /ap Cadell/
2 GIVN Tryffin
2 SURN ap Cadell
2 _MARNM ap Cadell
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 910
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, https://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/6516/TABLES%20-%20EARLY%20SERIES_42.png?sequence=13&isAllowed=y (May 27, 2018; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT The connection of Tryffin as a son of Cadell ap Rhodri is tenuous. In his chart #42, Bartrum pencils the connection with a "?." In his chart #43, he again indicates that the connection is speculative, citing Dwnn 1, pgs. 297 & 313
2 CONC , and adding "?" for Cadell ap Rhodri as an alternative to Cadell Ddyrnlling. A review of Dwnn reveals that Dwnn also had doubts as to the father of Tryffin. In Dwnn 1, 297, he cites Tryffin as "ap Cadell Deyrallyg,King of Powis" 
2 CONC adding a footnote that this is chronologically not possible. In Dwnn 1, 313, he cites Tryffin as " descended from Cadell Deyrallyg, King of Powis."
2 CONT
2 CONT Wolcott enters the debate with a note to one of his charts in the paper The Enigmatic Elystan Glodrydd: "Dwnn i, 297 & 313 say Ifor ap Seferus married Isabel ap Tryffin ap Cadell, king of Powys, or Tryffin descended from Cadell.  
2 CONC While this identifies the lady as a part of the First Powys Dynasty, insufficient lineage data is given to date her.  " 
2 CONT
2 CONT If we assign a date of c. 945 to make her an age appropriate wife of a c. 930 Ifor, that would put Tryffin at c. 910, meaning Cadell would have been near 60 when Tryffin was born. If we make Isabel the same age as Ifor, as in perh
2 CONC aps a second marriage, it does line up a bit better with Cadell ap Rhodri at c.850. But there is nothing in Dwnn to suggest this as a second marriage, and it does not justify putting Cadell ap Rhodri into the First Powys Dynasty. 
2 CONC  (Steven Ferry, August 7,2018.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Enigmatic Elystan Glodrydd; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id199.html. (Steven Ferry, August 11, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darrell Wolcott, Center for the Study of Ancient Wales: "Not that this family is likely to ever be of great historical interest, but I did chart it as a Powys line.  I made this Cadell a son of Selyf I and brother to Aeddan I
2 CONC I and to Beli I.  I dated Cadell to 880, and Dryffin to 910." (Steven Ferry, August 17, 2020.)
