2 CONT REFN: 7019
2 CONT Hywel ap Cadell ap Rhodri, is known in history as Hywel Dda (Hywel the
2 CONT Good: 890-950). A grandson of Rhodri Mawr, he came into possession of
2 CONT D yfed around 904 and Brycheiniog some years later. The combined kingdoms
2 CONT becam e known as Deheubarth, a unit seen by Dr. Davies as of central
2 CONT importance in the history of Wales during the next four centuries. In
2 CONT 942, Deheubarth was u nited with Gwynedd and Powys, gving Hwyel control
2 CONT over most of Wales with the exception of Glamorgan. He wisely kept peace
2 CONT with the English through a poli cy of conciliation, earning a reputation
2 CONT more as a diplomat than warrior. The king gained his reputation for his
2 CONT consolidation of the Law of Wales, one of "the most splendid creations of
2 CONT the culture of the Welsh" (Davies. p.88).
2 CONT I n the Cyfraith Hywel, his accomplishment was to systemize the ancient
2 CONT legal c ustoms of Wales: it is notable for its elements of mercy, common
2 CONT sense and a great respect for women and children that has been lacking
2 CONT from many legal sy stems of other countries right up to the present day.
2 CONT In "Brut y Tywysogyon," the unusually gifted ruler was described as "the
2 CONT chief and most praiseworthy of all the Britons." No other Welsh king
2 CONT gained the title of Dda (The good).
1 NOTE Known as "King of the Welsh". He was under-king to the Saxon monarchs.
1 NOTE [large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT REF:  MFHOWELLJR@aol.com (HOWELL-L@rootsweb.com, Howell readers group):Present Welsh, English and American Howells derive from the old Welshfamily of Abernent in Carmarthenshire, the linage and name deriving fromHywel Dda the King
2 CONC  of Wales from 943 to 950. He was grandson of RhodriMawr, the Great, who united almost all of ancient Wales under hissupremacy. He (Hywel Dda I presume) was married to Ellen, daughter of thelast of the Kings of Dyfed. The reign of
2 CONC  Howel Dda was remarkable for itspeacefulness, and is the only ruler of ancient Wales designated "TheGood". He was also the only King ever to issue his own coinage. On avisit to Rome in 928, became a student of jurisprudence, and 
2 CONC the code oflaws he composed is his lasting claim to fame. Three parts of his codeare still found in English Common Law.  He was such a wise and just manthat he was called "the head and glory of all the Britons." There is aplace to
2 CONC day in Wales called Crickhowell, which is considered to be eitherhis home and/or pob.
1 NOTE REF:  MFHOWELLJR@aol.com (HOWELL-L@rootsweb.com, Howell readersgroup): Present Welsh, English and American Howells derive from theold Welsh family of Abernent in Carmarthenshire, the linage and namederiving from Hywel Dda the King
2 CONC  of Wales from 943 to 950. He wasgrandson of Rhodri Mawr, the Great, who united almost all of ancientWales under his supremacy. He (Hywel Dda I presume) was married toEllen, daughter of the last of the Kings of Dyfed. The reign of
2 CONC  HowelDda was remarkable for its peacefulness, and is the only ruler ofancient Wales designated "The Good". He was also the only King ever toissue his own coinage. On a visit to Rome in 928, became a student ofjurisprudence, and t
2 CONC he code of laws he composed is his lasting claimto fame. Three parts of his code are still found in English CommonLaw.  He was such a wise and just man that he was called "the head andglory of all the Britons." There is a place to
2 CONC day in Wales calledCrickhowell, which is considered to be either his home and/or pob.
1 NOTE OR "HYWEL"; PRINCE OF SOUTH WALES; KING OF ALL WALES 907-948; SURNAMED "THE
2 CONT GOOD""DDA/DHA"; CELEBRATED AS THE JUSTINIAN OF CUMBRIO; INSTITUTED A TRIAL BY
2 CONT JURY OF TWLEVE GOOD MEN AND A JUSGE (AS WITH CHRIST AND THE 12 APOSTLES)
1 NOTE Created the Kingdom of Deheubarth
2 CONT
2 CONT Canon Law - Page 93 History of Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT A man's standing in a network of kindred rather than his standing as the citizen of a state which determined his social status, his economic rights, and his legal obligation...
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 85 of History of Wales
2 CONT       Llywarch ap Hyfaidd, King of Dyfed, died abt 904, passed possession to Hywel...(the ruler of Seisyllwg adn the husband of Elen, Llywarth's sister.)  Plus he gained possession of Brycheiniog about 930 from Tewdwr ap Giffri.
