2 CONT *Gruffydd Ap Llewelyn King of Gwynedd & Powys
2 CONT
2 CONT born Abt 1011 Of, Rhuddlan, Flintshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT died 5 Aug 1063
2 CONT
2 CONT biographical and/or anecdotal:
2 CONT
2 CONT notes or source:
2 CONT
2 CONT LDS
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Llywelyn ap Seisyll (died 1023) was a King of Gwynedd and of Deheubarth in north-west and south-west Wales, also called King of the Britons by the Annals of Ulster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn won control of Gwynedd in 1018 when he defeated Aeddan ap Blegywryd in battle, killing him and his four sons. He later gained control of Deheubarth, defeating Rhain, an Irish pretender who claimed to be the son of Maredud
2 CONC d ab Owain, at Abergwili in 1022.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the annals in Brut y Tywysogion, Llywelyn's reign was a period of prosperity, "there was no one needy in his realm, and there was no town empty or deserted". His reign was cut short by his premature death in 1023.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, though still a youth when his father died, was later able to gain control of almost the whole of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Edward Lloyd (1911), A history of Wales: from the earliest times to the Edwardian conquest. Longmans, Green & Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Edward Lloyd and R.T. Jenkins (ed.)(1959). The Dictionary of Welsh Biography Down to 1940. Oxford  
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llywelyn_ap_Seisyll
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn ap Seisyll, b. ca. 974 in Rhuddlan, Flintshire, Wales, d. 1023, He became King of Gwynedd, 1005 in Wales, He became King of Deheubarth, 1018 in Wales 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.geocities.com/missourimule_2000/princesofwales5.html
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Llywelyn ap Seisyll (died 1023) was a King of Gwynedd and of Deheubarth in north-west and south-west Wales, also called King of the Britons by the Annals of Ulster. Also called Llywelyn ap Seisyllt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage:
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about Llywelyn's father Seisyll, who may not have been of Royal blood. According to some genealogies Siesyll and his son Llywelyn were associated with Rhuddlan, perhaps originally as lords of the Rhuddlan commote i
2 CONC n Rhos.[1] Llywelyn's wife Angharad was the daughter of Maredydd ab Owain, who ruled much of both northern and southern Wales for a period.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military successes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn won control of Gwynedd in 1018 when he defeated Aeddan ap Blegywryd in battle, killing him and his four sons. He later gained control of Deheubarth, defeating Rhain, an Irish pretender who claimed to be the son of Maredud
2 CONC d ab Owain, at Abergwili in 1022.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the annals in Brut y Tywysogion, Llywelyn's reign was a period of prosperity, "there was no one needy in his realm, and there was no town empty or deserted".
2 CONT
2 CONT Succession:
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, though still a youth when his father died, was later able to gain control of almost the whole of Wales.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llywelyn_ap_Seisyll
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Llywelyn ap Seisyll, King of Gwynedd and Deheubarth, married Angharad verch Maredudd o Deheubarth, daughter of Brenin Deheubarth Maredudd ab Owain o Deheubarth in 994, his third cousin once removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn was King of Gwynedd in Wales between 1005 and 1023. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Chronicle of Ystrad Fflur (1018): "In this year Llywelyn ap Seisyll slew Aeddan ap Blegywryd and his four sons."
2 CONT
2 CONT See "My Lines"
2 CONT
2 CONT ( http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/p336.htm#i7152 )
2 CONT
2 CONT from Compiler: R. B. Stewart, Evans, GA
2 CONT
2 CONT ( http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/index.htm )
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Source: The book, 'The Oxford History of the British Monarchy'
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT #     11078             Welsh Medieval database.
2 CONT
2 CONT Llwelyn was aged 14 at the time of his marriage Source: Lloyd, History of the Princes, the Lords Marcher and the Ancient Nobility of Powys Fadog(Vol 1-page68)
2 CONT
2 CONT MILITARY-Wars & Battles 1015-He asserted his claim to Gwynedd, and led an army against Aeddan ab Blegwryd,and slew him. Source: Lloyd, History of the Princes etc(vol 1-page 68) 1021- An army of Irish Scots under Awlaff, invaded Wa
2 CONC les, and were joined by Hywel & Maredudd, sons of Edwin ab Einion. Llwelyn obtained a decisive victory, but fell by the hand of an assassin. Source: Lloyd, History of the Princes etc.(vol 1-page 68)
2 CONT
2 CONT TITLES Accession to Gwynedd-c1005 Accession to Deheubarth-c1018 Souce: Fryde, Handbook of British Chronology-Pages 50,52. Brenin of Cymru Source: Dwnn's, Heraldic Visitations of Wales(vol2-page107)
2 CONT
2 CONT He became, by usurpation, Prince of all Wales Source: Dunn's Heraldic Visitations etc (Vol 2-page 99-fn-2) He was a wise administrator who provided great prosperity for his country. Source; Lloyd, History of the Princes etc (vol 1
2 CONC -page 68)
2 CONT
