2 CONT biographical and/or anecdotal:
2 CONT
2 CONT notes or source: LDS
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Name 	Cynfyn ap Gwerystan [59, Llewelyn v.2, No 48], 26G Grandfather
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth 	ca 978
2 CONT
2 CONT Death 	1023
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Gwerystan
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 	Angharad ferch Meredudd [59, Llewelyn v.2, No 49], 26G Grandmother
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth 	ca 982
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Meredith ap Owain Prince of Powys (ca938-999)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 	Bleddyn ap Cynfyn (ca1025-1075)
2 CONT
2 CONT   	Rhiwallon (-1069)
2 CONT
2 CONT  by Tim Powys-Lybbe
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Cynfyn ap Gwerstan was a Powys Lord, about whom little is now known. He may have been son of an English Saxon--the name has been postulated as being derived from Werestan.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of Powys
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT # 11126-Welsh Medieval database.
2 CONT
2 CONT TITLES-King of Powys (Derbyshire Feudal History) Twysog Powys (Dwnn, Heraldic Visitations of Wales, Vol 2-page 124) Prince of Powys (Dunn etc)-vol1-page 326
2 CONT
2 CONT PROPERTY Lord of Powys & Lord of Cybwr Lord of Cybwr (Lloyd, History of the Princes, the Lords Marcher and the Ancient Nobility of Powys Fadog, Vol 1-page 68)
2 CONT
2 CONT KINSHIP CONFLICT: Dunns, Heraldic Visitations etc. (vol 2-page 15) -calls him a brother of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, but instead he was married to Gruffydd's mother,Angharad ferch Maredudd. Dunn's Heraldic Visitations etc. Vol 2-page 
2 CONC 26, inserts an extra generation between Cynfyn and Gwerystan called Elystan
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Marriage Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT Cynfyn married Angharad FERCH MAREDYDD, daughter of Maredydd AP OWAIN and Unnamed of Llandilo, in 1023. (Angharad FERCH MAREDYDD was born about 982 in Of, , Deheubarth, Wales. Spouses/Children: Angharad FERCH MAREDYDD Iwerydd FERC
2 CONC H CYNFYN of Powys+ Bleddyn "Sais" AP CYNFYN Prince of Powys+ Rhiwallon AP CYNFYN Prince of Powys+
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://books.google.com/books?id=ZxBJAAAAcAAJ&lpg=PA252&ots=rlh25fKIyW&dq=hywel%20madoc%20trahaern&pg=PA258#v=onepage&q&f=true Heraldic visitations of Wales] and part of the Marches, between the years 1586 and 1613; ed. with no
2 CONC tes by Samuel Rush Meyrick (Google eBook) Lewys Dwnn, Samuel Rush Meyrick William Rees, 1846 page 258
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Courtesy of fantastically full family tree cf.:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hughes of Gwerclas 1/2/3/4:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Descents/hughesofgwerclas_1.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Descents/hughesofgwerclas_2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Descents/hughesofgwerclas_3.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.maximiliangenealogy.co.uk/burke1/Royal%20Descents/hughesofgwerclas_4.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Little is known of Cynfyn. His fame, like that of many of his descendants, was only the reflection of the fame of his distinguished wife. There is no evidence that he ever attempted to rule in the right of his wife as did her firs
2 CONC t husband. The sons of Cynfyn and Angharad were Bleddyn and Rhiwallon. The pedigree of Cynfyn which appears in Vol 1, p 63 of the Historical Manuscripts Commission, London, 1898, extends back through twenty-two generations to Beli
2 CONC  Mawr and Anna through their son Llud. Prof Lloyd thinks that it is mythical. It is certainly incomplete. It would require many more than twenty-two generation to cover the period of more than one thousand years. In the History of
2 CONC  Radnorshire it is stated that Cynfyn was the son of Gwerstan and the grandson of Gwaethfoed Fawr. [The Weaver Genealogy]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia: Cynfyn ap Gwerystan (born c. 990[1]) — sometimes spelled Kynvyn, Kynwyn, Cynvyn, or Kynfyn — was a Welsh nobleman,[citation needed] principally known on account of his son Bleddyn ap Cynfyn, who established the House of
2 CONC  Mathrafal over Powys as a consequence of a Saxon invasion in 1063.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents 1	Interim King of Powys 2	Children 3	See also 4	References Interim King of Powys His father was Gwerystan ap Gwaithfoed and his mother Nest ferch Cadell[2], the daughter of Cadell ap Brochfael, penultimate king of Powys f
2 CONC rom the traditional line). Gwerystan ap Gwaethfoed was a patrilineal descendant of Cynwrig, the younger brother of Iorwerth Hirflawdd who ruled Arwystli. Legendary accounts locate Gwerystan's father Gwaithfoed in Englefield, and d
2 CONC escribe him as a friend of King Edgar (while the latter was in Chester); evidently Cynfyn's grandfather was a significant nobleman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cynfyn married Angharad ferch Maredudd, daughter of Maredudd ab Owain, king of Dyfed, Gwynedd, and Powys;[citation needed] crucially, she was also the widow of Llywelyn ap Seisyll, who had succeeded Maredudd as king of Gwynedd and
2 CONC  Powys. Wolcott argues that Cynfyn acted as caretaker ruler of Gwynedd and Powys, until 1033, when Iago ap Idwal came of age and took the kingship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children With an unnamed Irish princess, daughter of Sitric Silkbeard c. 960 he had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eva ferch Cynfyn, who married Llewelyn ap Coel Nest ferch Cynfyn, who married Ithel ap Coel, brother of Llewelyn Iwerydd ferch Cynfyn After the death of his first wife, possibly in childbirth, he married Angharad (daughter of Mare
2 CONC dudd ab Owain), widow of Llywelyn ap Seisyll and had the following issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhiwallon ap Cynfyn (died 1070), killed at the Battle of Mechain Bleddyn ap Cynfyn (died 1075), King of Gwynedd and Powys Gwerfyl ferch Cynfyn, married Gwrgan ap Ithel Ddu of Glamorgan, and was mother to Iestyn ap Gwrgan. See also
2 CONC  Kings of Wales family trees References
2 CONT  Hen Lwythau Gwynedd a'r Mars, a medieval manuscript containing pedigrees of the chief non-royal families of north Wales, edited by P.C. Bartrum in the National Library of Wales Journal, vol xii, part 3
