2 CONT Page 150,  Line: (177-1):
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.    Griffith ap Llewellyn (176-2), slain 5 Aug. 1063, Prince of North Wales , m. (1) ca 1057 Edith (or Aldgyth) (176 A-4).  (CP I 22; VI 451-453; DNB 23:307; NGSQ 50: 74-78).
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 149 line 176-2:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.   Griffith I ap Llewellyn, slain 5 aug. 1063, Prince of North Wales; m. as 1st wife ca. 1057, Edyth (or Aldgyth) (176A-4) who m. (2) ca. 1064, Harold, Earl of East Anglia (Harold II (1B-23), King of England, dau. of Elgar (Aelf
2 CONC gar), Earl of Mercia 1057 (ASC 1035, 1051, 1053, 1055, 1057, 1058; CCN 444, 604), Son of Leofwine, d. bef 1032, Earl of Mercia (ASC 1017; CCN 604).  (CP VI, 451-453; DNB 23:307; NGSQ 50: 76-77).
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The library of Ken.
1 NOTE He had 2 sons and 1 saughter.
1 NOTE Gruffydd ap Llywelyn or Llewelyn
2 CONT Gruffydd ap Llywelyn or Llewelyn
2 CONT grifith äp hlwelin, looelin , d. 1063, ruler of Wales (1039-63). A seriesof campaigns against other Welsh princes made him the ruler of virtuallyall Wales. Allied with the outlawed Ælfgar of Mercia, he launched aseries of raids in
2 CONC to W England, but his power was broken by Harold in twoinvasions (1062-63). His defeat plunged Wales into confusion and pavedthe way for the conquest of Wales by the Normans. His name also appearsas Griffith ap Llewelyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT REFN: 6396
2 CONT Gruffudd ap Llywelyn, (1007-63) was King of all Wales only from 105 5 to
2 CONT 1063, yet he was the only Welsh ruler to unite the ancient kingdoms of
2 CONT the whole of Wales. Gwynfor Evans points out that though for five
2 CONT centuries the people of Wales had shared a common language, culture,
2 CONT history, religion and for the most part a common law, it was only under
2 CONT Gruffudd ap Llywelyn t hat it had a single sovereign, and thus a measure
2 CONT of political unity. Gruffud d was a grandson of Maredudd ab Owain, King of
2 CONT Deheubarth and the son of Llyw elyn ap Seisyllt, ruler of Gwynedd. By
2 CONT force of arms, he seized Gwynedd and P owys, and overthrew Deheubarth,
2 CONT Gwent and Morgannwg. Walter Map, in "de Nugis Curalium" (c.1180),
2 CONT recorded the kings' account of his military campaigns ag ainst his fellow
2 CONT Welsh: "Speak not of killing. I do but blunt the horns of th e offpsring
2 CONT of Wales, lest they should wound their dam."
2 CONT Gruffudd was even s uccessful in reclaiming lands east of Offa's Dyke from
2 CONT the English settlers. He established his court at Rhuddlan, in the heart
2 CONT of an area settled by Merc ians, and his conquests of areas of Northeast
2 CONT Wales formerly part of the earl dom of Chester meant that they would
2 CONT remain Welsh as parts of the later count ies of Flintshire and
2 CONT Denbighshire. His very successes, however, led to the i nvasion of Wales
2 CONT by Harold, Earl of Wessex. During the fighting, Gruffudd was assassinated
2 CONT by a fellow Welshman. Wales was never again wholly united under a single
2 CONT sovereign though Llywelyn ap Iorweth's successes over a century lat er
2 CONT surpassed those of his predecessor.
1 NOTE The son of Llywelyn Ap Seisyll, King of Deheubarth and Gwynedd, and Angharad, daughter and heiress of Maredudd Ab Owain, King of Deheubarth, who probably still only a child when his father died in 1023 and is said to have spent hi
2 CONC s youth in indolence.  He became king of Gwynedd and Powys after Iago Ab Idwal Foel had been killed by his own men in 1039 and immediately began campaigning against the English, gaining a victory at Rhyd-y-groes in the Severn vall
2 CONC ey that made him a national hero.  He then turned his attention to reconquering Deheubarth, the throne of which had been usurped since his father's death.  After a long struggle against Hywel ab Edwin, he finally defeated and kill
2 CONC ed him in  a battle fought at the mouth of the Towy in 1044.  He then had to contend with Gruffydd ap Rhydderch, who managed to hold power in Deheubarth until he, too, was defeated and slain in 1055, when Gruffydd ap Llywelyn beca
2 CONC me the undisputed master of all Wales.
2 CONT Gruffydd allied himself with Earl Aelfgar of Mercia to attack the Saxons and Normans of Hereford and drove them out, setting fire to the town.  Earl Harold [later Harold II] was sent against him to avenge the incident, but only ma
2 CONC naged to rebuild the town and conclude peace with Aelfgar.  On 16 June 1056 Bishop Leofgar of Hereford led an army against Gruffydd and was defeated by him in a battle fought in the Machawy valley.  Shortly afterwards, an agreemen
2 CONC t was negotiated through the mediation of Harold, Earl Leofric of Mercia and Earl Ealdred of Worcester, and Gruffydd accepted Edward the Confessor as his overlord.  It was probably in the same year that Gruffydd married Ealdgyth, 
2 CONC the daughter of his old ally Aelfgar, whom he helped to regain his lands.
2 CONT Gruffydd was not allowed to end his reign in peace.  After Aelfgar's death in 1062, Harold made a surprise raid on Gruffydd's court at Rhuddlan, but he managed to escape.  He was killed 'through the treachery of his own men' in 10
2 CONC 63 and was eulogized by the Brut y Tywysogion as having been 'the head and shield and defender of the Britons'.  He left two young sons, Maredudd and Idwal, both of whomdied in 1070, and a daughter, Nest, who married Osbern FitzRi
2 CONC chard, Lord of Richard's Castle and Byton, and had issue.  Gruffydd's widow, Ealdgyth, married Earl Harold in 1065.
1 NOTE OR "GRUFFYDD AP LLYWELYN"; THOUGH ACTUALLY ONLY PRINCE OF NORTH WALES,
2 CONT AMBITIOUS ENOUGH TO MAKE HIMSELF "MASTER" OF ALL WALES 1039-1063; HAD 2 SONS
2 CONT BESIDES THOSE SHOWN; DIED FIGHTING HAROLD II
