2 CONT
2 CONT "A wise and gentle ruler" {re: "Royal Ancestors of Magna Charta Barons," Carr P. Collins, Jr., Dallas, 1959, p. 94}. {Bleddyn's wife, Haer, is dau. of Cilin (son of the Lord of Gest).}
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn ap Cynfyn From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn ap Cynfyn (died 1075) was a Prince of the Welsh Kingdoms of Gwynedd and of Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn was the son of Princess Angharad ferch Maredudd (of the Dinefwr dynasty of Deheubarth) with her second husband Cynfyn ap Gwerstan, a Powys Lord, about whom little is now known. He may have been son of an English Saxon - th
2 CONC e name has been postulated as being derived from Werestan.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother Angharad was previously widow of Llywelyn ap Seisyll and also mother of Gruffudd ap Llywelyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn was married to Hear of Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Submission to Harold Godwinson & Reward
2 CONT
2 CONT When Gruffydd ap Llywelyn was killed by his own men after being defeated by the Saxon Harold Godwinson in 1063, his realm was divided among several Welsh Princes. Bleddyn and his brother Rhiwallon ap Cynfyn, as half brothers to Gr
2 CONC uffudd succeeded to his lands but first as vassals and allies of the Saxon King of England, Edward the Confessor and then submitted to Harold and from him received Gwynedd and Powys.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anti-Norman Welsh & Saxon Alliance
2 CONT
2 CONT They continued Gruffudd's policy of allying to the Mercian Saxons to resist the threat from William the Conqueror.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1067 Bleddyn and Rhiwallon joined with the Mercian Eadric the Wild in an attack on the Normans at Hereford, ravaged thelands as far as the River Lugg then in 1068 allied with Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar of Northumbria 
2 CONC in another attack on the Normans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Challenges at Home
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn was challenged by the two sons of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, but defeated them at the battle of Mechain in 1070, one being killed and the other dying of exposure after the battle. Bleddyn's brother Rhiwallon was also killed in 
2 CONC this battle, Bleddyn emerging as the only one of the four to survive the bloody encounter and he ruled Gwynedd and Powys alone until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1073 Robert of Rhuddlan stealthily established his forces on the banks of the River Clwyd and attempted to ambush and capture Bleddyn, narrowly failing but seizing valuable booty from the raids further south.
2 CONT
2 CONT Killed By Deheubarth
2 CONT
2 CONT He was killed in 1075 by Rhys ab Owain of Deheubarth and the nobility of Ystrad Tywi in South Wales, a killing which caused much shock throughout Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Rhys ab Owain was defeated in arms at the Battle of Goodwick and forced to become a fugitive by Bleddyn's cousin and successor as King of Gwynedd, Trahaearn ap Caradog in 1078 and killed by Caradog ap Gruffydd of Gwent shortl
2 CONC y afterwards, this was hailed as "vengeance for the blood of Bleddyn ap Cynfyn".
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn is said in the Brut y Tywysogion to have been a benevolent ruler:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "the most lovable and the most merciful of all kings ... he was civil to his relatives, generous to the poor, merciful to pilgrims and orphans and widows and a defender of the weak ...".
2 CONT
2 CONT and
2 CONT
2 CONT     "the mildest and most clement of kings" and he "did injury to none, save when insulted.... openhanded to all, terrible in war, but in peace beloved." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT He was responsible for a revision of Welsh law in the version used in Gwynedd. After his death Gwynedd was seized by Trahaearn ap Caradog and later recovered for the line of Rhodri the Great by Gruffydd ap Cynan, but in Powys Bled
2 CONC dyn was the founder of a dynasty which lasted until the end of the 13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ K. L. Maund is of the opinion that Bleddyn ruled Gwynedd and Rhiwallon Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * R.R. Davies (1991). The age of conquest: Wales 1063-1415. O.U.P. ISBN 0-19-820198-2. 
2 CONT     * Thomas Jones (ed) (1952) Brut y Tywysogyon: Peniarth MS. 20 version (University of Wales Press)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bleddyn ap Cynfyn (died 1075) was a King of the Welsh Kingdoms of Gwynedd and of Powys.  He was the son of Queen Angharad ferch Maredudd (of the Dinefwr dynasty of Deheubarth) with her second husband Cynfyn ap Gwerstan, a Powys lo
2 CONC rd (about whom little is now known;  he may have been the son of an English Saxon.)  The name has been postulated as being derived from Werestan.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Bleddyn's mother Angharad was the widow of Llywelyn ap Seisyll (and she was also the mother of Gruffudd ap Llywelyn.)  Bleddyn was married to Hear of Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Gruffydd ap Llywelyn was killed by his own men after being defeated by the Saxon Harold Godwinson in 1063, his realm was divided among several Welsh Princes. Bleddyn and his brother Rhiwallon ap Cynfyn, as half brothers to Gr
2 CONC uffudd succeeded to his lands but first as vassals and allies of the Saxon King of England, Edward the Confessor and then submitted to Harold and from him received Gwynedd and Powys.  They continued Gruffudd's policy of allying to
2 CONC  the Mercian Saxons to resist the threat from William the Conqueror.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1067 Bleddyn and Rhiwallon joined with the Mercian Eadric the Wild in an attack on the Normans at Hereford and ravaged the lands as far as the River Lugg.  Then, in 1068, they allied with Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar of
2 CONC  Northumbria in another attack on the Normans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn's rule was challenged by the two sons of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn.  The challenge culminated in the Battle of Mechain, which was fought in 1070 in Montgomeryshire, Wales, for rule of the Welsh kingdoms of Gwynedd and Powys.  A
2 CONC t this battle, both of Gruffyd's sons were defeated, one being killed and the other dying of exposure after the battle.  Bleddyn's brother Rhiwallon was also killed in this battle, leaving Bleddyn to rule Gwynedd and Powys alone.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1073 Robert of Rhuddlan stealthily established his forces on the banks of the River Clwyd and attempted to ambush and capture Bleddyn, narrowly failing but seizing valuable booty from the raids further south.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Bleddyn was killed in 1075 by Rhys ab Owain of Deheubarth and the nobility of Ystrad Tywi in South Wales, a killing which caused much shock throughout Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn's cousin - Trahaearn ap Caradog - succeeded as King of Gwynedd.  He engaged and defeated Bleddyn's killer at the Battle of Goodwick in 1078.  Rhys ab Owain was forced to become a fugitive and he was killed by Caradog ap Gr
2 CONC uffydd of Gwent shortly afterwards.  The killing was hailed as "vengeance for the blood of Bleddyn ap Cynfyn".
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn is said in the Brut y Tywysogion to have been a benevolent ruler.  He was "the most lovable and the most merciful of all kings ... he was civil to his relatives, generous to the poor, merciful to pilgrims and orphans and w
2 CONC idows and a defender of the weak . . .."  He was "the mildest and most clement of kings" who "did injury to none, save when insulted.... [he was] open-handed to all, terrible in war, but in peace beloved." 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Bleddyn was responsible for a revision of the Welsh law implemented in Gwynedd. After his death, Gwynedd was seized by Trahaearn ap Caradog and later recovered for the line of Rhodri the Great by Gruffydd ap Cynan.  However, 
2 CONC in Powys, Bleddyn was the founder of a dynasty which lasted until the end of the 13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT R.R. Davies (1991). The age of conquest: Wales 1063-1415. O.U.P. ISBN 0-19-820198-2.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Jones (ed) (1952) Brut y Tywysogyon: Peniarth MS. 20 version (University of Wales Press) 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bleddyn_ap_Cynfyn http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Mechain
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1063, Blethyn (Bleddyn) rules a mostly united Wales, and Powys is detached from Gwynedd & Deheubarth for, or by, his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn and his brother Rhiwallon were half-brothers of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn. Their mother, Angharad, married Cynfyn ap Gwerstan o the death of Llywelyn ap Seisyll. Cynfyn ap Bwerstan may have been a Powys nobleman; there is also 
2 CONC some suggestion that he may have been half Saxon. Bleddyn remained a strong supporter of the Mercians in their battles against the Normans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn and Rhiwallon also had to contend with internal revolution. The sons of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, Maredudd and Idwal, attempted to reestablish their claim to the throne. However, they were also killed in the Battle of Mechain 
2 CONC in 1070; Bleddyn now ruled without internal challenge.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is reported to having been a generous king, giving to church and populous. He was the first king in over 100 years to revise the laws developed under Hywel Dda.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, in 1073, the Normans began their advance through north Wales, and defeated Bleddyn in a surprise attack. Weakened, he became vurnerable to his southern neighbor, Rhys ab Owain of Deheubarth. In 1075, Rhys betrayed B
2 CONC leddyn, leading to his murder. However, Bleddyn's descendents became the main ruling family of Powys. [The Welsh Kings]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
