2 CONT In 1067 Bleddyn and Rhiwallon joined with the Mercian Eadric the Wild in an attack on the Normans at Hereford, ravaged the lands as far as the River Lugg then in 1068 allied with Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar of Northumbria
2 CONC  in another attack on the Normans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn was challenged by the two sons of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, but defeated them at the battle of Mechain in 1070, one being killed and the other dying of exposure after the battle. Bleddyn's brother Rhiwallon was also killed in 
2 CONC this battle, Bleddyn emerging as the only one of the four to survive the bloody encounter and he ruled Gwynedd and Powys alone until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1073 Robert of Rhuddlan stealthily established his forces on the banks of the River Clwyd and attempted to ambush and capture Bleddyn, narrowly failing but seizing valuable booty from the raids further south.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn was killed in 1075 by Rhys ab Owain of Deheubarth and the nobility of Ystrad Tywi in South Wales, a killing which caused much shock throughout Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Rhys ab Owain was defeated in arms at the Battle of Goodwick and forced to become a fugitive by Bleddyn's cousin and successor as King of Gwynedd, Trahaearn ap Caradog in 1078 and killed by Caradog ap Gruffydd of Gwent shortl
2 CONC y afterwards, this was hailed as "vengeance for the blood of Bleddyn ap Cynfyn."
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn is said in the Brut y Tywysogion to have been a benevolent ruler:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "the most lovable and the most merciful of all kings ... he was civil to his relatives, generous to the poor, merciful to pilgrims and orphans and widows and a defender of the weak ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT and
2 CONT
2 CONT "the mildest and most clement of kings" and he "did injury to none, save when insulted.... openhanded to all, terrible in war, but in peace beloved."
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Died in 1075, assasinated at Powys Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn was Prince of Powys and Gwynedd from 1063 to 1075; he acknowledged the overlordship of King Edward the Confessor and later resisted William the Conqueror. He married  to Haer, daughter and heiress of Cilin ap y Blaidd Rhyd
2 CONC d, Lord of Gestyn-Efionydd. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From: http://www.robertsewell.ca/powys.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT # 11222-Welsh Medieval Database
2 CONT
2 CONT MILITARY-Wars & Battles 1068-Maredudd & Idwel, the Rightful Heirs of Brochwel, King of Powys, led an army against Bleddyn & Rhiwallon, to regain Gwynedd. Bleddyn & Rhiwallon met them at Mechain with a host of Saxons, for the Saxon
2 CONC s had fled from the intrusion of the Normans into Gwynedd , for protection. Rhiwallon was slain, also Idwal ap Gruffydd. Source: Lloyd, History of the Princes, The Lords Marcher and the Ancient Nobility of Powys Fadog, vol 1-page 
2 CONC 71.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1072-Rhys ab Owain ab Edwin ab Einion came against Bleddyn and fought a battle and killed him Source: Lloyd, History of the Princes etc, Vol 1-page 72.
2 CONT
2 CONT TITLES: Accession-Appears to have ruled Gwynedd (after his half-brother Gruffydd ap Llywelyn) as well as Powys from 1063-1075 Source: Fryde, Handbook of British Chronology, Page 54. Prince of Powys Source, Lloyd, History of the Pr
2 CONC inces etc. vol 1-page 109. Brenin of Powys Source 1062-1072 Source, Lloyd, History of the Princes etc, vol1-page 109
2 CONT
