2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gruffydd_ap_Rhydderch
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd ap Rhydderch (d. AD 1055) was a king of Gwent and part of the kingdom of Morgannwg in south Wales and later king of Deheubarth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd was the son of Rhydderch ab Iestyn who had been able to take over the kingdom of Deheubarth from 1023 to 1033. He received the lordship of Caerleon in 1031 and strengthened its fortifications.[1] Already king of part of M
2 CONC organnwg, Gruffydd became involved with Deheubarth when that kingdom was taken over from Hywel ab Edwin by Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, already king of Gwynedd, in 1044. Gruffydd ap Rhydderch was however able to expel him in 1045 and bec
2 CONC ame king of Deheubarth himself. He was said to be a powerful king who stoutly resisted raids by the Danes and attacks by Gruffydd ap Llywelyn. In 1055 however Gruffydd ap Llywelyn killed him in battle and recaptured Deheubarth.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1049 he is reported raiding up the River Severn in alliance with an Irish Viking Fleet.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Caradog ap Gruffydd (who received Caerleon in 1057[1]) also attempted to emulate his father and grandfather by gaining control of Deheubarth but was killed at the Battle of Mynydd Carn.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://welshleigh.org/genealogy/prichardancestry/prichardhistorical4.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT The first in this line was RHYDDERCH ap IESTYN, whose origins are unknown but who came to power in Glamorgan, as we saw in Welsh Princes 1-2. By 1023 he was strong enough to seize control of Deheubarth from the descendants of HYWE
2 CONC L DDA, but he died in 1033, killed by the Irish (Maund pp.61-2, J.Davies p.99). His son GRUFFUDD ap RHYDDERCH was unable to hold on to his lands, and it was not until 1045 that he had sufficient control of Glamorgan and Monmouthsh
2 CONC ire to recapture his father’s place in Deheubarth. He resisted the Danes and attacked the English border, but he was defeated and killed by Gruffudd ap Llywelyn in 1055.
