2 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Cynfyn ap Gwerystan
2 CONT
2 CONT Cynfyn ap Gwerystan, b abt 995. He md Angharad verch Maredudd abt 1024, Wales, daughter of Maredudd ap Owain. He also fathered a child by either another wife or a mistress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Cynfyn ap Gwerstan and Angharad verch Maredudd were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhiwallon ap Cynfyn b abt 1025. See LINE A
2 CONT
2 CONT Bleddyn ap Cynfyn b abt 1027. See LINE B
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Cynfyn ap Gwerystan and unknown wife or mistress was:
2 CONT
2 CONT Iwerydd verch Cynfyn b abt 1030. She md Edwin of Tegeingl abt 1046.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Iwerydd verch Cynfyn and Edwin of Tegeingl was:
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain ap Edwin b abt 1055. The identity of his wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Owain ap Edwin was:
2 CONT
2 CONT Angharad verch Owain b abt 1065. She md Gruffydd ap Cynan, King of Gwynedd, abt 1098.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd ap Cynan, King of Gwynedd, b abt 1070, d 1137. He md Angharad verch Owain abt 1098, daughter of Owain ap Edwin. She was b abt 1084, d 1162.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Gruffydd ap Cynan and Angharad verch Owain were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwenllian verch Gruffydd b abt 1100, Wales. She md Gruffydd ap Rhys, Prince of South Wales, abt 1112, son of Rhys ap Tewdr-Mawr, Prince of South Wales, and Gwladus verch Rhiwallon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain-Gwynedd ap Gruffydd, Prince of North Wales, b abt 1104.
2 CONT
2 CONT Susanna verch Gruffydd b abt 1112, Aberffraw Castle, Anglesey, Wales. She md Madog ap Maredudd abt 1130, prob Wales, son of Maredudd ap Bleddyn, Prince of Powys, and Hunydd verch Einudd.
2 CONT
2 CONT By about 1118 Gruffydd's advancing years meant that most of the fighting which pushed Gwynedd's borders eastward and southwards was done by his three sons by his wife Angharad, daughter of Owain ab Edwin: Cadwallon, Owain Gwynedd 
2 CONC and later Cadwaladr.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT # ID: I4354
2 CONT
2 CONT # Name: Angharat verch Owain
2 CONT
2 CONT # Given Name: Angharat verch
2 CONT
2 CONT # Surname: Owain 1
2 CONT
2 CONT # Sex: F
2 CONT
2 CONT # Birth: Abt 1065 in Tegeingl, Flintshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT # Death: 1162
2 CONT
2 CONT # Change Date: 21 Sep 2005 at 15:23
2 CONT
2 CONT # Note:
2 CONT
2 CONT     [De La Pole.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT     Sources: Young; Kraentzler 1406, 1409; AF; A. Roots 239; History of Morgan Family.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Roots: Angharat of Tegaingl, daughter of Owain ap Edwin.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Young: Angharad II of Tegaingl.
2 CONT
2 CONT     K: Anghared verch Owain (the Golden Hair).
2 CONT
2 CONT     SOURCES:
2 CONT
2 CONT     1. Lloyd, John E., _The History of Wales, Vol. II:
2 CONT
2 CONT     P. 464: "By his wife Angharad, the flaxen-haired daughter of Owain abEdwin,
2 CONT
2 CONT     whom he married about 1095, he had three sons, Cadwallon, Owain, andCadwaladr,
2 CONT
2 CONT     and five daughters...." Footnote 7, same page, names the daughters as
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwenllian (wife of Gruffydd ap Rhys), Marared, Rannillt (named for her paternal grandmother), Susanna(who married Madog ap Maredudd), and Annest.
2 CONT
2 CONT     P. 488: Owain Gwynedd fell into a deep melancholy when his mother died in1162.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Owain Ap Edwin b: 1044 in Llys Edwin, Llaneurguin, Flintshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Morwyl Verch Ednywain b: Abt 1044-1048 in Tegeingl, Flintshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Gruffudd ap Cyan b: 1055 in Dublin, Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Married: Abt 1095 in Wales 2
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Change Date: 21 Sep 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Has Children Margred Verch Gruffudd b: Abt 1070 in Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Has Children Gruffydd ap Rhys b: Abt 1081
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Has Children Gwenlian verch Gruffydd b: Abt 1090 in Caernarvonshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Has Children Susanna Verch Gruffydd b: Abt 1095 in Caernarvonshire, Wales c: in <Gwynedd, Wales>
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Has No Children Cadwaladr Ap Gruffudd b: Abt 1096 in Caernarvonshire, Wales
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Has Children Owain Gwynedd Ap Gruffudd b: Abt 1100 in The Forgotten Monarchy of Scotland"
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Abbrev: Ancestral Roots Of Sixty Colonists Who Came To New Englan d Between 1623 And 1650
2 CONT
2 CONT       Title: Ancestral Roots Of Sixty Colonists Who Came To New England Between 1623And 1650
2 CONT
2 CONT       Author: Weis, Frederick Lewis
2 CONT
2 CONT       Publication: Genealogical Publishing Co. Inc., 1992
2 CONT
2 CONT       Date: 7 Jun 2004
2 CONT
2 CONT       Repository:
2 CONT
2 CONT       Page: 239-5
2 CONT
2 CONT       Quality: 3
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Abbrev: Ancestral Roots Of Sixty Colonists Who Came To New Englan d Between 1623 And 1650
2 CONT
2 CONT       Title: Ancestral Roots Of Sixty Colonists Who Came To New England Between 1623And 1650
2 CONT
2 CONT       Author: Weis, Frederick Lewis
2 CONT
2 CONT       Publication: Genealogical Publishing Co. Inc., 1992
2 CONT
2 CONT       Date: 7 Jun 2004
2 CONT
2 CONT       Repository:
2 CONT
2 CONT       Page: 239-5 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Angharad ferch Owain of Tegeingl was born circa 1072. She was the daughter of Owain ab Edwyn of Tegeingl and Morwyl verch Ydnywain bendew.3 Angharad ferch Owain of Tegeingl married Gryffydd ap Cynan, King of Gwynedd, son of Cynan 
2 CONC ab Iago, Prince of Gwynedd and Radnaillt of Dublin, circa 1082; 6th cousins, 1x removed.4,5
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Gryffydd ap Cynan, King of Gwynedd b. circa 1055, d. 1137
2 CONT
2 CONT Children	
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Susanna ferch Gruffudd ap Cynan o Gwynedd+ b. c 10953
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Owain Gwynedd ap Gruffydd, King of Gwynedd+ b. c 1100, d. Dec 11693,6
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Gwenllian verch Gruffydd o Gwynedd+ b. c 1100?, d. 11367
2 CONT
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/p62.htm#i7995
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hist. W., 464, 469.
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT His (Gruffydd's) wife was Angharad, daughter of Owain, son of Edwin. Her beauties are minutely described by the biographer. By her Gruffydd had three sons: Cadwallon (who in 1124 slew his mother's three brothers and in 1132 was sl
2 CONC ain by his cousin), Cadwaladr, and Owain, afterwards famous a Owain Gwynedd. He also had by her many daughters, one of whom, Gwenllian, was the wife, first of Cadwgan ab Bledyn and then of Gruffydd ab Rhys. Gruffydd was also the f
2 CONC ather of several ilegitimated children. [The Dictionary of National Biography; George Smith, Fd., Sir Leslie Stephen & Sir Sidney Lee, Ed., 1953; Vol VIII, pp 744-747, Gruffydd ab Cynan]
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT His (Owain ap Edwin) daughter Angharad was the wife of Gruffydd ap Cynan. [The Dictionary of National Biography; George Smith, Fd., Sir Leslie Stephen and Sir Sidney Lee, Ed., 1953, Vol XIV, p 1284, Owain ap Edwin]
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT Angharad (d 1162), wife of Gruffudd ap Cynan, was a daughter of Owain ab Edwin, a chieftain of eastern Gwynedd. She m. Gruffudd about 1095, during his early struggle for power, and survived her husban many years, dying in 1162. Th
2 CONC eir children were Cadwallon (d 1132), Owain (Gwynedd), and Cadwaladr, and five daughters, named Gwenllian, Marared (Margaret) , Rainillt, Susanna, and Annest. Of these, Gwenllian m. Gruffydd ap Rhys, and Sussanna m. Madog ap Mared
2 CONC udd. Angharad is singled out for lavish praise by her husband's biographer, as a handsome blonde, gentle, eloquent, generous, discreet, good to her people and charitable to the poor. Gruffydd left her, in addition to the half of h
2 CONC is goods as provided by Welsh law, two shares of land (rhandir) and the profits of the port of Abermenai. [Dictionary of Welsh Biography p9]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Angharad married Gruffudd ap Cynan in 1082 in the Kingdom of Gwynedd when she was seventeen. She was queen of Gwynedd for forty years with her husband and queen mother for twenty-five years into the time period when her son Owain 
2 CONC ap Gruffyd was king of Gwynedd in north Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personality[edit] Angharad was a tall blonde and labeled "Golden Haired".[14][15] She was considered attractive and eloquent.[16] Angharad had large eyes, strong limbs, long fingers with well groomed nails, a nice figure and fine 
2 CONC feet.[16] She had a queenly personality, was good-natured, and was known as being kindhearted and sympathetic towards the poor.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings[edit] Her siblings were Meilyr ab Owain, Rhiryd ab Owain, Gronw ab Owain and Einion ab Owain.[A][B][C]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children[edit] Angharad and Gruffudd had the following children at Aberffraw Castle, Aberffraw, on Anglesey (now within Isle of Anglesey County), in the Kingdom of Gwynedd in north-west Wales; Rhanullt verch Gruffydd (b. 1083), Ow
2 CONC ain Gwynedd (b. 1087), Gwenllian verch Gruffydd (b. 1085), Elen verch Gruffydd (b. 1089), Merinedd verch Gruffydd (b. 1091), Susanna verch Gruffydd (b. 1095), Cadwaladr ap Gruffydd (b. 1096), Cadwallon ap Gruffydd (b. 1097), Yslan
2 CONC i verch Gruffudd (b. 1104), Membyr "Ddu" ap Gruffydd (b. 1114), Rhael verch Gruffydd (b. 1116), Annes verch Gruffydd (b. 1118), Margred verch Gruffydd (b. 1120), and Tudwal ap Gruffydd (b. 1122).[13][16][20][21]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later life and death[edit] Angharad was 72 years old in 1137 when her hushand died. He left her half of his assets as was customary by Welsh law of the time.[10] In addition she received two shares of land (rhandir).[9] She also r
2 CONC eceived in her inheritance the profits of the ferry service of the time at the port of Abermenai.[9][10] This ferry is documented in the twelfth-century Welsh reference work Life of Guffudd ap Cynan and believed to be the earliest
2 CONC  such service at this strait.[22][23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Angharad died in 1162 at the age of 97.[24][25]
2 CONT
2 CONT References Notes ^ "In 1124 Cadwallon, who was clearly as unscrupulous as he was energetic, slew the three rulers of the cantref, Gronw, Rhiryd, and Meilyr ab Owain, notwithstanding that they were his mother's brothers."[17]
2 CONT  ^ "CADWALLON, one of the sons of Gruffydd ab Cynan, prince of North Wales, whose name is recorded in the military history of that age, as being the leader of his father's armies. In 1125, he slew his three uncles, Gronw, Rhiryd, 
2 CONC and Meilyr, sons of Owain ab Edwin; but about the year 1130, he was himself put to death in Nanheudwy by another uncle, Einion ab Owain ab Edwin." [18]
2 CONT  ^ "And in the year 1132 died Robert Curthose the Kings brother in the castell of Caerdyffe. And the year following Cadwallon, son to Gruffudd ap Cynan was slain at Namheudwy by Einion, son to Owain ab Edwin, his uncle (whose thre
2 CONC e brothers he had slain)."[19] Citations
