2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ynir lived in the middle of the 12th century and descended through this father from Ynir, the second son of Cadwalader, King of the Britons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cadwalader built Abergavenny, in the County of Monmouth and its castle, which was afterward rebuilt by Hamlet ap Hamlet, ap Sir Druce of Balladon, in France, and portions of the walls still remain.  This according to a pedigree re
2 CONC corded in the College of Arms [RI Genealogical Register, p. 1401].
2 CONT
2 CONT "His lineage has been traced through 96 generations of ancestors to Adam, but perhaps that is too far for accuracy" [One Hundred and Sixty Allied Families, p. 16].
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT See Darrell Wolcott, "The Herbert Family Pedigree," http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id40.html (Anne Brannen, curator, January 2, 2019)
2 CONT
2 CONT See Darrell Wolcott: The Herbert Family Pedigrees; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id40.html. (Steven Ferry, April 15, 2017.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Location of Gwent:
2 CONT
2 CONT East Wales Gwent is a preserved county and a former local government county in south-east Wales. It was formed on 1 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1974, under the Local Government Act 1972, and was named after the ancient Kingdom of Gwent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Monmouthshire
2 CONT
2 CONT See also: Monmouthshire (historic)
2 CONT
2 CONT The territory which became Monmouthshire was part of the Welsh kingdoms of Gwent and Glywysing and later, after the Norman conquest of southern Wales, of the Welsh Marches. Although the original Laws in Wales Act of 1535 specifica
2 CONC lly stated the lands making up Monmouthshire were from the 'Country or Dominion of Wales', the Laws in Wales Act 1542 added Monmouthshire to the Oxford circuit of the English Assizes rather than falling under the Court of Great Se
2 CONC ssions in Wales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to historian John Davies, this arrangement was the cause of the erroneous belief that the county had been annexed by England rather than remaining part of Wales.[6] In later centuries, some English historians, map-makers
2 CONC , landowners and politicians took the view that Monmouthshire was an English rather than a Welsh county, and references were often made in legislation to "Wales and Monmouthshire". The position was finally resolved by the Local Go
2 CONC vernment Act 1972, which confirmed Monmouthshire's place within Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Wikipedia
