2 CONT Nest or Nesta flourished 1106 as mistress of Henry I. She was the daughter of Rhys ap Tewdwr, who died 1093, King of Dehenbarth, by Gwladys, daughter of Rhywallon. Rhys was made King of South Wales by England in 1063. Nesta receiv
2 CONC ed as her marriage portion the lordship of Carew, and about 1095, or soon afterwards, married Gerald de Windsor, Constable of Pembroke Castle, a loyal and prudent man. She was clever and beautiful. About 1106 her cousin Owen, son 
2 CONC of Cadwgan, visited the castle by night, and in order to get to the room where she and her husband were set fire to the castle. Nesta pulled up a board and let her husband into the drain, by which he escaped. She was carried to Po
2 CONC ys, together with two of her sons by Gerald and two of his children by another woman. Cadwgan was angry at his son's act for he feared the wrath of the English, and begged him to send Nesta back, but he would not. However, she per
2 CONC suaded him to send her husband's children back to him. Her abduction led to a war, in which Gerald took a conspicuous part. After a time she rejoined her husband, who appears to have died before 1136. She was also the wife of Step
2 CONC hen, Constable of Cardigan. In the list of her children, given by her descendant, Giraldus Cambrenses, the names of the three fathers, to whom the greater number of them are assigned, stand in order as Gerald, Stephen and King Hen
2 CONC ry. Indeed, it seems that her eldest son was by Gerald. It is probable that her connection with Stephen did not begin before 1110, and that she bore a son to Henry in 1114. Seven of her sons became lords of Cantreds in South Wales
2 CONC , and from her descended some of the most famous of the conquerors of Ireland. Her children by Gerald of Windsor were William FitzGerald, her eldest son, Maurice, David, and a daughter, Angharad, and two other sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference Dictionary of National Biography, Vol. 40, pp. 228/9. See Generation No. 4. Gerald of Windsor or Gerald FitzWalter, who married Nesta.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/cgi-bin/gedlkup/n=royal?royal05170 '''From Tompsett'''] : "Known as the most beautiful woman in Wales. She had many lovers. In Christmas 1108 Owain ap Cadwgan of Cardigan came to visit Gerald and Nesta. 
2 CONC He so lusted after her that he, that night, attacked the castle and carried her off and had his way with her. This upset Henry I so much that the incident started a war."[JohnFaye (8 Jun 05).FTW]
2 CONT
