2 CONT biographical and/or anecdotal:
2 CONT
2 CONT notes or source:
2 CONT
2 CONT LDS
2 CONT
2 CONT ancestry.com
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT NOTE: Maurice FITZ GERALD de Windsor, Lord of Llanstephen (child of Gerald Fitz Walter de Windsor, born 1100 at Windsor; married Alice de Montgomery daughter of Arnulf de Montgomery and Lafracoth Ua Briain; died 1 Sep 1176 at Co. 
2 CONC Wexford, Ireland Children ofMaurice FitzGerald de WINDSOR `Invader of Ireland'; Maurice "the Invader" FitzGerald; Lord of Lanstephen, Wales (1100 in Windsor, Berkshire County, England. died 1 Sep 1176 in Wexford, Ireland or he pos
2 CONC sibly died at the Abbey Grey Friar, Welford, Berkshire County.buried Friary of the Grey Friars Cem,Wexford, Wales) Steward of St. Davids., Baron of the Nass & MAYNOOTH Wexford,Wales md Alice de Montgomerie (b. 1105 Pembrokeshire, 
2 CONC Wales ) dau of Arnulph (Arnulf) de MONTGOMERY and Lafracoth (Lafracota) O'BRIEN of MUNSTER ** --- dapifer of St. Edmund's. Under Henry I he held lands in Bedfordshire, Berkshire, Dorset, Essex, Middlesex, Northamptonshire, Norfolk
2 CONC , and Suffolk in 1130. Confirmed in his land and offices by Stephen. Succeeded by his nephew Ralph De Hastings as his heir in land and office. --- He started building the The Black Castle. The castle was begun by Maurice Fitzgeral
2 CONC d when he was granted the district in 1176, but his death a year later delayed its completion. ** **In April 1172, Henry II., on his departure for England, appointed FitzGerald and FitzStephen Wardens of Dublin, under Hugh de Lacy
2 CONC . It was FitzGerald who saved De Lacy's life in the encounter with O'Rourke at the Hill of Ward. On the recall of De Lacy in 1173, FitzGerald retired to Wales, in consequence of misunderstandings with Strongbow. In 1176 matters we
2 CONC re arranged between them, and he was made a grant of the barony of Offaly, and the territory of Offelan, comprising the present towns of Maynooth and Naas. He was given the castle of Wicklow in return for his share of Wexford, app
2 CONC ropriated with other towns by the King. Llanstephen was in the County of Radnor, Wales. Maurice was the Steward of St. Davids. Maurice participated in organizing the invasion of Leinster, Ireland. He was granted lands in Wexford b
2 CONC y King Dermot MacMurrogh, and Maurice landed there in in the third wave of invaders in late 1169 with a force of two ships. ** In September 1177, he died at Wexford, and was buried in the Abbey of Grey Friars, without the walls of
2 CONC  the own. According to Lodge, his death was "not without much sorrow of all his friends, and much harm and loss to the English interest in Ireland. He was a man witty and manful; a truer man, nor steadfaster for constancy, fidelit
2 CONC y and love, left he none in Ireland." Cambrensis thus describes him: "Maurice was indeed an honourable and modest man, with a face sun-burnt and well-looking, of middle height; a man well modelled in mind and bodyl and of innate g
2 CONC oodness; desiring rather to be than to seem good. A man of few words, but full of weight, having more of the heart than of the mouth, more of reason than of volubility, more wisdom than eloquence; and yet, when it was required, ea
2 CONC rnest to the purpose. In military affairs valiant, and second to few in activity; neither impetuous nor rash, but circumspect in attack, and resolute in defence; a sober, modest, and chaste man; constant, trusty, and faithful; a m
2 CONC an not altogether without fault, yet not spotted with any notorious or great crime."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Maurice Fitzgerald helped STRONGBOW secure Dublin for the English and in 1172 was appointed by Henry II, warden of the city under Hugh de Lacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1176 he was made a grant of the barony of Offaly and the territory of Offelan, comprising the present towns of Maynooth and Naas. He died 1177, the following Year. (From "The Noble Dwellings of Ireland" by John FitzMaurice Mill
2 CONC s. p.131 - GLIN CASTLE, GLIN, co. LIMERICK
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.fitzmaurice.info/ances.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice fitzGerald de Windsor
2 CONT
2 CONT (Born A.D. 1100; died 1 Sep 1176 at Wexford, Ireland)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord of Maynooth and Baron of Naas.
2 CONT
2 CONT The photo at the right is a drawing of Maurice that his nephew, Giraldus Cambrensis, inserted in the Latin MS of his Expugnatio Hibernica (Conquest of Ireland). [Note the red hair on top of his head and the dark mini-beard on his 
2 CONC lower lip.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT In A.D. 1168, Dermot MacMorrogh, King of Leinster, driven from his territory by Roderick O'Conor, High King if Ireland, sought assistance from the Norman-Welsh and succeeded in enlisting in his cause Richard de Clare, Earl of Pemb
2 CONC roke (called Strongbow). Having reached St. David's, on his way back to Ireland, Dermot was hospitably received by David fitzGerald (son of Gerald of Windsor), Bishop of the diocese. According to Burke's Peerage, the Bishop persua
2 CONC ded his brother, Maurice fitzGerald, and his half-brother, Robert fitzStephen to assist MacMorrough with their forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime after achieving success in the invasion of Ireland, Strongbow granted to Maurice fitzGerald the middle cantred of Offelan, in which Naas was situated. Offelan was a district quite distinct from Offaly.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice married Alice de Montgomery and had six sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT William fitzMaurice, Baron of Naas. Confirmed in his lands in Offelan in A.D. 1185. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerald fitzMaurice, 1st Baron of Offaly, ancestor of the Dukes of Leinster. d. A.D. 1203 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas fitzMaurice, Lord of Connello, Co. Limerick. d. A.D. 1213. Ancestor of the FitzGeralds, the Earls of Desmond, the White Knight, the Knight of Glin, the Knight of Kerry, the FitzMaurice Lords of Kerry, etc. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice fitzMaurice of Kiltrany (now Burntchurch), Co. Kilkenny. Ancestor of the Barons of Burntchurch. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander fitzMaurice, d.s.p. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert fitzMaurice. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The information above was all obtained from the 1970 edition of Burke's Peerage. I am inclined to accept that source for the present.
2 CONT
2 CONT From at least the end of the 16th century to at least the mid19th century, English and Irish genealogists wrote that William fitzMaurice (the eldest son of Maurice fitzGerald of Windsor) was the ancestor of the FitzMaurices of Ker
2 CONC ry via his son Raymond le Gros.16 There is now general agreement that Raymond le Gros had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT One modern Irish genealogist102 argues that Thomas fitzMaurice (the 1st Lord of Kerry) was the son of Maurice fitzThomas of Molahiffe (d. 1306), son of Thomas fitzRobert (d. after 1261), son of Robert fitzMaurice, the youngest son
2 CONC  of Maurice fitzGerald (Lord of Maynooth and Baron of Naas, d. 1176).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Maurice Fitz Gerald: Ancestors of the Earls of Kildare, now Dukes of Leinster. One of the soldiers of fortune who accompanied Strongbow into Ireland, in 1168. He died in the year 1177 and was burried in the Abbey of Grey Friers, a
2 CONC t Wexford. Maurice Fitz-Gerald, the eldest son, one of the first invaders of Ireland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Maurice FitzGerald==
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord of Maynooth, Naas, and Llanstephan) was a major figure in the Norman invasion of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice was the second son of Gerald de Windsor, Constable of Pembroke Castle by his wife, Nest ferch Rhys, a Welsh princess. He fought under Robert FitzMartin at the Battle of Crug Mawr in 1136. Diarmait Mac Murchada (Dermot MacM
2 CONC urrough), the deposed King of Leinster, who had been exiled by the High King of Ireland, sought FitzGerald's assistance to regain his throne. He assisted his younger half-brother Robert Fitz-Stephen in the Siege of Wexford (1169).
2 CONC  His nephew, Raymond FitzGerald, was Strongbow's second in command and had the chief share both in the capture of Waterford and in the successful assault on Dublin.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He gained the title of Lord of Llanstephan, Wales [feudal barony]. In 1167 he was pledged Wexford by Dermot MacMurrogh, King of Leinster, if he would help restore him to his kingdom. In 1169 at Ireland he went to Ireland and helpe
2 CONC d secure Wexford and Dublin. He held the office of Joint Keeper of Dublin after 1171, by King Henry II. He gained the title of Baron of Naas [feudal barony]. He gained the title of Lord of Maynooth [feudal barony].
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurice FitzGerald, Lord of Maynooth, Naas, and Llanstephan (c. 1105 – September 1176) was a medieval Cambro-Norman baron and a major figure in the Norman invasion of Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wars in Wales and Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT A Welsh Marcher Lord, Lanstephan fought under Robert FitzMartin at the Battle of Crug Mawr in Wales 1136.
2 CONT
2 CONT Diarmait Mac Murchada (Dermot MacMurrough), the deposed King of Leinster who had been exiled by the High King of Ireland, sought Cambro-Norman assistance to regain his throne. Lanstephan participated in the resulting 1169 Norman i
2 CONC nvasion of Ireland. He assisted his younger half-brother Robert Fitz-Stephen in the Siege of Wexford (1169). His nephew Raymond was Strongbow's second-in-command and had the chief share both in the capture of Waterford and in the 
2 CONC successful assault on Dublin in 1171. Lanstephan and his son also fought in this battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT FitzGerald is sometimes said to have married Alice, a supposed daughter of Arnulf de Montgomery. There is no evidence that Arnulf left any descendants, however, and the claim that a daughter of his married FitzGerald dates no earl
2 CONC ier than the 19th century.[3] FitzGerald's children were:
2 CONT  Gerald FitzMaurice, 1st Lord of Offaly (b. c. 1150, d. before 15 Jan 1204)
2 CONT  Alexander
2 CONT  William, Lord of Naas (d. c.1199)
2 CONT  Maurice, Lord of Kiltrany
2 CONT  Thomas FitzMaurice FitzGerald (d. c.1213)
2 CONT  Robert
2 CONT  Nest (m. Hervey de Montmorenci, Constable of England)
2 CONT
2 CONT Through his oldest son, Sir Gerald, FitzGerald was the ancestor of the FitzGerald Earls of Kildare and Dukes of Leinster.
2 CONT
2 CONT The original Earldom of Desmond in the province of Munster was based on landholdings belonging to the descendants of Maurice's son Thomas FitzMaurice FitzGerald, Lord of O'Connelloe. Thomas's son John FitzGerald, who was killed in
2 CONC  the Battle of Callann, became the first Baron Desmond. Others from this line include the Knights of Glin and Knights of Kerry.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.mathematical.com/fitzgeraldmaurice1100.html
