2 CONT 1160-1195 OWAIN CYFEILIOG, Prince of Southern Powys, received Cyfeiliog from his uncle Madog ap Maredudd 1149, abdicated in favour of his son Gwenwynwyn 1155 and retired to the monastery of Strata Marcella, born about 1125, marrie
2 CONC d firstly Gwenllian. daughter of Owain Gwynedd, King of Gwynedd, and had issue,
2 CONT
2 CONT     1. Gwnwynwyn, succeeded his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT     2. Caswallon.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain ap Gruffydd (c. 1130 - 1197) was a prince of the southern part of Powys and a poet. He is usually known as Owain Cyfeiliog to distinguish him from other rulers named Owain, particularly his contemporary, Owain ap Gruffydd of
2 CONC  Gwynedd known as Owain Gwynedd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain was the son of Gruffydd ap Maredudd and nephew of Madog ap Maredudd, the last prince of the whole of Powys. Madog gave his nephew the commote of Cyfeiliog to rule in 1147. On Madog's death in 1160 Owain became the ruler of m
2 CONC ost of southern Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is recorded as having been in alliance with the other Welsh princes to withstand the invasion of 1165 by king Henry II of England. Thereafter he usually followed a policy of supporting the English crown. In 1170 he gave land fo
2 CONC r the founding of the abbey of Strata Marcella. In 1188 however he refused to meet or support Baldwin, Archbishop of Canterbury and Giraldus Cambrensis when they journeyed around Wales to raise men for a crusade, and was excommuni
2 CONC cated as a result.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1195 Owain handed the rule of his realm to his son Gwenwynwyn ab Owain and retired to the abbey of Strata Marcella, where he died and was buried two years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain was also a notable poet. Although only one of his poems has been preserved, this one, Hirlas Owain is commonly rated as one of the finest Welsh poems of this period. In the poem, Owain's bodyguard are gathered at his court f
2 CONC ollowing a raid in 1155 to free his brother Meurig from prison in Maelor. The mission accomplished, Owain calls for the drinking horn to be passed to each member of his bodyguard in turn, with words of praise for each one. There i
2 CONC s a more sombre note when he remembers two of his men who fell in the fighting and grieves for their loss.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain also appears in the romance of Fulke FitzWarin as a knight who strikes Fulk with a spear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hirlas Owain
2 CONT
2 CONT Oddi ar Wicitestun
2 CONT
2 CONT Neidio i: llywio, chwilio
2 CONT
2 CONT gan Owain Cyfeiliog
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwawr pan ddwyreai, gawr a ddoded -
2 CONT
2 CONT     Galon yn anfon anfud dynged.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Geleurudd ein gwyr gwedi lludded - trwm
2 CONT
2 CONT     Tremid gofwy mur Maelawr drefred.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Deon a yrrais dygyhused,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Diarswyd ar frwydr, arfau goched.
2 CONT
2 CONT     A rygoddwy glew, gogeled - rhagdaw:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gnawd yw o'i ddygnaw ddefnydd codded.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dywallaw di, fenestr, gan foddhäed
2 CONT
2 CONT     Y corn yn llaw Rys yn llys llyw ced -
2 CONT
2 CONT     Llys Owain ar brain yd ry borthed,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Porth mil a glywy, pyrth egored.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Menestr a'm gorthaw, na'm adawed,
2 CONT
2 CONT     As deuy â'r corn, er cydyfed,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hiraethlawn, amliw, lliw ton nawfed,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hirlas ei arwydd, aur ei duded.
2 CONT
2 CONT     A dyddwg o fragawd wirawd worgred
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ar llaw Wgawn draws dros ei weithred.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Canawon Goromwy gwrdd gynired - gwyth,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwyr a obryn tâl ym mhob called;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwyr ynd ngawr gwerthfawr gwrdd ymwared,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Bugelydd Hafren, balch eu clywed,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Bugunad cyrn medd, mawr afneud.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dywallaw di'r cprm ar Gynfelyn
2 CONT
2 CONT     Anrhydeddus feddw o fedd gorewyn;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ac or mynny hoedl hyd un flwyddyn,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Na didawl ei barch, can yd berthyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT     A dyddwg i Ruffudd, waywrudd elyn,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwin â gwydr golau yn ei gylchyn;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dragon Arwystli, arwystl terfyn,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dragon Owain hael o hil Cynfyn,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dragon a'I dechrau, ac niw dychryn - cad,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cyflafan argrad, cymwy erlyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cydwyr ydd aethant er clod edwyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Talasant eu medd mal gwyr Belyn - gynt
2 CONT
2 CONT     Teg eu hydrefynt tra fo undyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dywallaw di'r corn, can y'm puchant,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hirlas yn llawen yn llaw Forgant,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwr a ddyly gwawd, gwahan foliant,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwenwyn ei adwyn, gwân edrywant,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Areglydd defnydd dioddeifiant,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Llafn llyfn ei ddeitu, llym ei amgant.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dywallaw di, fenestr, o lestr ariant
2 CONT
2 CONT     Celennig edmig can ardduniant;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ar llawr Gwestun Fawr gwelais irdant;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ardwy Goronwy oedd gwaith I gant.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cedwyr cyfafaeth ydd ymwnaethant;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cad ymerbyniaid, enaid ddichwant.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cyfarfu ysgwyn ac ysgarant - aer,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Llas maer, llosged caer cer môr lliant.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Mwynfawr garchaeawr a gyrchasant -
2 CONT
2 CONT     Meurig fab Gruffudd, frym darogant.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Neud oedd gochwys pawb pan atgorsant;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Neud oedd lawn o haul hirfryn a phant.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dywallaw di'r corn i'r cynifiaid,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Canawon Owain, cyngrain cydnaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Wynt a ddyrllyddant, yn lle honnaid,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Glud, men ydd ânt, gloyw hëyrn ar naid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Madawg a Meilyr, gwyr gorddyfnaid
2 CONT
2 CONT     Trais, tros gyferwyr gyferbyniaid;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Taranogion torf, terfysg ddysgaid;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Trinheion faon, traws arddwyaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ciglau am dâl medd myned haid - Gatraeth,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cywir eu harfaeth, arfau llifaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cosgordd Fynyddawg, am ei cysgaid
2 CONT
2 CONT     Cawsant eu hadrawdd, casflawdd flaeniaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ni waeth wnaeth fy nghydwyr yng ngraid - Faelawr
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ddillwng carcharawr, ddyllest folaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dywallaw di, fenestr, fedd hidlaid - melys,
2 CONT
2 CONT     O gyrn buelin balch oreuraid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ergyrayw gwrys gochwys yn rhaid,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Er gobryn bobrwy gwerth eu henaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     A'r gnifer anun a borth cuniaid
2 CONT
2 CONT     Nis gwyr namyn Duw, ac a'i dywaid.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwr ni dwng, ni dâl, ni bydd wrthwir,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Daniel, draig cannerth, mor ferth hywir.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Menestr, mawr o waith ydd oleithir
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwyr ni olaith llaith oni llochir.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Menstr, medd ancwyn a'm cydroddir,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gwrdd dân gloyw golau, gwrddleu babir.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Menestr, gwelud galchöed - gyngrain,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Yng nghichyn Owain gylchwy enwir.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Pan breiddwyd Cawres, taerwres trwy ddir,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Praidd ostwng orflwng a orfolir.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Menestr, na'm diddawl, ni'm diddolir;
2 CONT
2 CONT     Poed ym mharadwys y'n cynhwysir.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Gan ben tëyrnedd boed hir - ein trwydded,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Yn y mae gweled gwaradred gwir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wedi dod o "http://cy.wikisource.org/wiki/Hirlas_Owain"
2 CONT
2 CONT Categorïau: Owain Cyfeiliog | Cerddi Caeth
