1 NAME William "le Belward" /ap Dafydd ap William/
2 GIVN William "le Belward"
2 SURN ap Dafydd ap William
2 _MARNM de Malpas
2 NICK William de Malpas
1 NAME William "le Belward" de Malpas
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1120
2 PLAC Whitchurch
2 ADDR
3 CITY Malpas
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1165
2 ADDR
3 CITY Malpas
3 STAE England
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Please see Darrell Wolcott: The "Malpas" Family in Cheshire; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id152.html. (Steven Ferry, April 19, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT William (son of David ap William ll ap William b abt 1090 & Margaret ferch Ralph) was born about 1120. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of his wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son was David, born about 1150 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT ===notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id152.html THE "MALPAS" FAMILY IN CHESHIRE] By Darrell Wolcott
2 CONT
2 CONT there were 3 sons of the c. 1030 Sir William of Malpas: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * b.  Ralph of Malpas, father of Margaret.  She married her 1st cousin, David b 1090, son of William ll.
2 CONT
2 CONT With this marriage, the line of Ralph became extinct and his one-third of Malpas passed to his son-in-law and nephew, David, and thence to his daughter's son, the earliest of the "William ap David ap William" men in the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/sitebuildercontent/sitebuilderpictures/.pond/malpaspedigreex.jpg.w560h549.jpg     1.  Visitation of Cheshire, 1580]:
2 CONT
2 CONT The William Belward (1) is a composite man.  When combined with David (4), William (6), Beatrix (7), David (8) and Beatrix (10), he was born c. 1120, the son of David ap William II ap Sir William of 1030.  But when combined with D
2 CONC avid (4) who married Margaret (5) daughter of Ralph (2) and Beatrix (3), he was born c. 1060 and is William II ap Sir William. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/sitebuildercontent/sitebuilderpictures/malpaspedigreeo.jpg 2.  Pedigree from Ormerod's History of Cheshire, vol 2, 598:]
2 CONT
2 CONT David (4) who married Margaret (5) was not "le clerk" but the son of William II and father of William born c. 1120 whose son David of c. 1150 was the father of William (6) who married Beatrix (7).  
1 FAMC @F6000000038923436686@
1 FAMS @F6000000038924460863@
1 RFN geni:6000000038923815406
1 SUBM @S5079474084160022074@
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/20/86/4a/98/5344483efdd5e28f/barons_of_malpas_original.jpg?hash=2d707bdf2f0ff4d613be0771dac9c92fa55ef721bc9793aa101f08ebf6064a54.1794124799
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Barons of Malpas 
2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE From http://www.theheraldrysociety.com/articles/heraldic_devices/crosses_by_HSL.htm
3 CONT
3 CONT The term cross patonce in the 13th century indicated that the ends of the cross terminated in three prongs somewhat like a paw, and patonce was perhaps derived from patte=paw. Confusingly at the same time there arose the similar t
3 CONC erm of cross paty which perhaps derived from the Old French patu, meaning the base of a cup ( the word itself derived from the latin patens meaning opening or spreading) used to describe a cross diverging at the ends but with a fl
3 CONC at end. An alternative label for the latter type of cross is formy ie “shapely”. Because of the similarity of the words paty and patonce heraldists over the centuries have frequently conflated the two terms. This led to Oswald Bar
3 CONC ron’s suggestion that the term cross patonce be abandoned. Here Norfolk Herald elucidates the full history of the usage of these terms, and makes the counter suggestion that the usage of cross paty should be abandoned! 
2 NOTE Arms, generally attributed to the barons of Malpas, Argent, a cross patonce Azure.
