1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT See Darrell Wolcott, http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id187.html, "The Children of Lord Rhys," for untangling of these lines. (April 21, 2016, Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd ap Rhys II (died 25 July 1201) was a prince of Deheubarth in south-west Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Lineage 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Norman Intermarriage 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Family Feud 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Succession By Interview 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Prisoner of His Brother & the English 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Released to Mediate 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 Lands Regained 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 Death & Burial 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9 References 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Rhys ap Gruffydd (The Lord Rhys) and grandson of Gruffydd ap Rhys.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd was the eldest son of Rhys ap Gruffydd by his wife Gwenllian, daughter of Madog ap Maredudd prince of Powys.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Norman Intermarriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys intended Gruffydd to be his main heir, and in 1189 he was married to Matilda, daughter of William de Braose, by whom he had two sons, Rhys and Owain.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family Feud
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rhys' last years a feud developed between Gruffydd and his brother Maelgwn ap Rhys, both supported by some of their other brothers. In 1189 Rhys was persuaded to imprison Maelgwn, and he was given into Gruffydd's keeping at Din
2 CONC efwr. Gruffydd handed him over to his father in law, William de Braose. In 1192 Rhys secured Maelgwn's release, but by now he and Gruffydd were bitter enemies. In 1194 Maelgwn and another brother Hywel defeated their father and im
2 CONC prisoned him, though he was later released by Hywel.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Succession By Interview
2 CONT
2 CONT Rhys ap Gruffydd died in 1197. Gruffydd was recognised as his successor after an interview with Archbishop Hubert the justiciar.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Prisoner of His Brother & the English
2 CONT
2 CONT But Maelgwn used troops supplied by Gwenwynwyn ab Owain of Powys to attack Aberystwyth. He captured the town and the castle, and took Gruffydd himself prisoner, later handing him over to Gwenwynwyn who in turn transferred him to t
2 CONC he English who imprisoned him in Corfe Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Released to Mediate
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1198 Gwenwynwyn threatened the English holdings at Painscastle and Elfael, and Gruffydd was released from captivity to try to mediate in the dispute. His efforts failed, and in the ensuing battle Gwenwynwyn was defeated.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Lands Regained
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd retained his liberty and by the end of the year had captured all of Ceredigion from Maelgwn except for the castles of Cardigan and Ystrad Meurig. In 1199 he took Cilgerran Castle. Maelgwn made an agreement with King John 
2 CONC of England, selling Cardigan castle to him in exchange for the possession of the remainder of Ceredigion.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1201 another brother, Maredudd ap Rhys, was killed, and Gruffydd took over his lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death & Burial
2 CONT
2 CONT However on 25 July Gruffydd himself died of an illness and was buried in Strata Florida Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
