1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Please see Darrell Wolcott: The 'Next Heir' of Morgan of Caerleon; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id214.html. (Steven Ferry, September 18, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>-----------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.oxforddnb.com/view/printable/48555
2 CONT
2 CONT "Late in June 1175 Iorwerth came to an agreement with the Lord Rhys, with whose support he regained Caerleon from King Henry. The recovery of Caerleon may also have had something to do with the sexual liaison between the king and 
2 CONC Iorwerth's daughter, Nest Bloet, with whom the king had an illegitimate son, Morgan (surnamed Bloet, from his being brought up in the family of Nest's husband, Ralph Bloet (d. 1199) of Silchester)."
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>-----------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ====en.Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nest_Bloet Nest Bloet]
2 CONT * “Nest Bloet (died 1224/5), sometimes called "Nest of Wales", was a Welsh noblewoman, best known for her many romantic liaisons, including an extramarital affair with King Henry II of England. She was the daughter of Angharad, da
2 CONC ughter of Uthred, Bishop of Llandaff, and Iorwerth ab Owain, the lord of Caerleon.[1]…
2 CONT * “Nest is most famous for her affair with King Henry II.[5] This affair probably took place in the early or mid 1170s.[5] She may have met the king when he came to meet with a number of Welsh lords at Gloucester in 1175 and resto
2 CONC red Caerleon to her father,[6] although Henry and Iorweth also met one another three years earlier.[5] 
2 CONT * “This affair produced a son, Morgan, who was raised by Ralph and Nest but acknowledged by Henry II.[7]”
2 CONT ** '''CITATIONS'''
2 CONT ** [1] Trett, Robert (2006). “A History of Caerleon Castle” @[https://www.caerleon.net/mynde/castle/page9.html Caerleon.net]. '''NOTE: ''' This webpage '''does not''' mention Nest. Therefore, the author of this Wikipedia paragraph
2 CONC  isn’t entirely reliable. One must access the next three footnotes to determine their veracity.
2 CONT ** [5] Crouch, David (2008). [http://www.oxforddnb.com/view/article/47222 "Nest Bloet"]. ''Oxford Dictionary of National Biography'' (online ed.). Oxford University Press. doi:[ https://doi.org/10.1093%2Fref%3Aodnb%2F47222 10.1093
2 CONC /ref:odnb/47222].. (Subscription or UK [https://www.oxforddnb.com/help/subscribe#public public library membership] required.)
2 CONT ** [6] Gillingham, John. 2000. “Henry II, Richard I and the Lord Rhys.” In ''The English in the Twelfth Century: Imperialism, National Identity, and Political Values'', 59–68. Woodbridge, England: Boydell Press. [https://search.wo
2 CONC rldcat.org/title/40738905 WorldCat.org], p. 61.
2 CONT ** [7] Barlow, F (1945). ''Annales Dunelmenses in Durham Annals and Documents of the Thirteenth century''. Surtees Society. [https://archive.org/details/annalesdunelmenses/page/3/mode/1up?view=theater Archive.org], pp. 3-4.
2 CONT <nowiki>-----------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20Kings%201066-1603.htm#HenriIIdied1189B
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRI d’Anjou, son of GEOFFROY "le Bel/Plantagenet" Comte d'Anjou et de Maine & his wife [Empress] Matilda [Maud] of England (Le Mans, Anjou 5 Mar 1133-Château de Chinon 6 Jul 1189, bur Abbaye de Fontevrault[370]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (5): NESTA, wife of RALPH Bloet, daughter of ---. 
2 CONT King Henry II had one illegitimate child by Mistress (5):  
2 CONT 12. MORGAN ([1180/89]-after 1213).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert de Graystane´s early 14th century History of the Church of Durham records the election as bishop of Durham in 1213 of “Morganus frater Regis Johannis et Galfridi archiepiscopi Eboracensis, præpositusque Beverlacensis”, that
2 CONC  his appointment was blocked by Rome because he was born “spurius...Henricus pater eius” to “uxore...militis...Radulphi Bloeth”, and that the Pope offered to confirm the election if he declared that the king was not his father, wh
2 CONC ich he refused to do[545].  Provost of Berkeley, Yorkshire 1201.  Elected Bishop of Durham in 1213.
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>-----------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ====en.Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morgan_(bishop) Morgan (bishop)]
2 CONT * "Morgan was an illegitimate son of King Henry II of England and Nesta, daughter of Iorwerth ab Owain, Lord of Caerleon. Nesta was married to Sir Ralph Bloet, who raised Morgan as his son. The date of his birth is unknown but pre
2 CONC sumed to be toward the end of King Henry's life.[1]
2 CONT * "Morgan was the provost of Beverley before being nominated to the see of Durham between 7 March and 7 May 1215. He was never consecrated because Pope Innocent III refused consecration on the grounds of his illegitimacy.[2] Innoc
2 CONC ent offered to confirm the election and allow the episcopal consecration if Morgan would swear that he was the son of Nesta and her husband Ralph and not King Henry. Morgan, after deliberation, replied that it was unthinkable for 
2 CONC him to deny his father the king.[1]"
2 CONT
2 CONT ** [1] Given-Wilson, Chris, and Alice Curteis. 2023. ''The Royal Bastards of Medieval England''. Milton Park, Abingdon-on-Thames, Oxfordshire, UK: Routledge. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Royal_Bastards_of_Medieval_Eng
2 CONC land/WlfgEAAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=Morgan Google Books], ''Snippet view''. '''In Libraries:''' [https://search.worldcat.org/title/10145237 WorldCat.org]. p. 99
2 CONT ** [2] Greenway, Diana E. 1971. “Durham 1066-1300.” In ''Fasti Ecclesiae Anglicanae 1066-1300:Monastic Cathedrals (Northern and Southern Provinces)'', 2:29–32. London, England: Institute of Historical Research. British History Onl
2 CONC ine, Archived @[https://web.archive.org/web/20110719101948/http://british-history.ac.uk/report.aspx?compid=33859 Archive.org Wayback Machine]. 
2 CONT *** “'''[Morgan'''
2 CONT *** “Morgan, provost of Beverley, illegitimate son of Henry II, el. between 7 March and 7 May 1215 on king's suggestion (Rot. Litt. Claus. 1 190b, 204; cf. ''ibid''. p. 268''b''; ''Durham Annals'' pp. 1-2; ''Script. Tres'' p. 35).
2 CONC  But cons. refused by pope on grounds of illegitimacy (''Script. Tres'' p. 35).]”
2 CONT ** ''See also'' – Turner, James. 2023. ''The Royal Bastards of Twelfth Century England: Power and Blood''. Barnsley, England: Pen & Sword Books. [https://search.worldcat.org/title/1369290165 WorldCat.org].
