2 CONT =William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber=
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Braose,_4th_Lord_of_Bramber
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Braose, (or William de Briouze), 4th Lord of Bramber (1144/1153 – 9 August 1211), court favourite of King John of England, at the peak of his power, was also Lord of Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth, Radnor, Kingto
2 CONC n, Limerick, Glamorgan, Skenfrith, Briouze in Normandy, Grosmont, and White Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Lineage===
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the most notable member of the de Braose dynasty and his steady rise and sudden fall at the hands of King John is often taken as an example of that king's arbitrary and capricious behaviour towards his barons.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the son of William de Braose, 3rd Lord of Bramber and his wife Bertha of Hereford, also known as Bertha de Pitres, (born 1130) daughter of Miles Fitz Walter, Earl of Hereford and his wife, Sibyl, daughter of Bernard de
2 CONC  Neufmarche. From his father he inherited the Rape of Bramber, in Sussex, and through his mother he inherited a large estate in the Welsh Marches area of modern day Monmouthshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Abergavenny Massacre===
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1175, William de Braose carried out the Abergavenny Massacre, luring three Welsh princes and other Welsh leaders to their deaths. His principal antagonist was a Seisyll ap Dyfnwal, of Castell Arnallt near Llanover in the valley
2 CONC  of the River Usk near Abergavenny, whom he blamed for the death of his uncle Henry. After having invited the Welsh leaders to a Christmas feast at Abergavenny Castle under the pretence of peace and the start of a new era at the e
2 CONC nd of the year (a traditional time for settling outstanding differences amongst the Welsh), he had them murdered by his men. This resulted in great hostility against him among the Welsh, who named him the "Ogre of Abergavenny". Ge
2 CONC rald of Wales exonerates him and emphasises the religious piety of de Braose and his wife and de Braose generosity to the priories of Abergavenny and Brecon. William de Braose did however reputedly hunt down and kill Seisyll ap Dy
2 CONC fnwal's surviving son, Cadwaladr, a boy of seven.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1192 William de Braose was made Sheriff of Hereford, a post he held until 1199. In 1196 he was made Justice Itinerant for Staffordshire. In 1195 he accompanied King Richard I of England to Normandy and in 1199, William de Braos
2 CONC e fought beside Richard at Chalus, where the king was mortally wounded.
2 CONT
2 CONT He then supported King John's claim to the throne of England, supported the new king in making various royal grants and was in attendance with John in Normandy at the time of Arthur of Brittany's death in 1203. Arthur was John's n
2 CONC ephew and was seen by many as the rightful heir to the English throne.
2 CONT De Braose served in the war of 1204 against King Philip II of France in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Royal favourite===
2 CONT
2 CONT He was greatly favoured by King John early in his reign. John granted him all that he might conquer from the Welsh in Radnorshire, gave him lordship over Limerick in Ireland (save for the city itself), possession of Glamorgan cast
2 CONC le, and the Lordship of Gower with its several castles.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1203, William de Braose was put in charge of Arthur of Brittany, whom he had personally captured the previous year at the Battle of Mirebeau. William was suspected of involvement in Arthur's disappearance and death, although no
2 CONC  concrete evidence ever came to light. There is somewhat better evidence that he at least knew the truth of the matter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1206 King John gave William de Braose the three great neighbouring trilateral castles of Gwent (Skenfrith Castle, Grosmont Castle, and White Castle). These have been interpreted as bribes encouraging silence on the demise of Ar
2 CONC thur, seen by many as a rightful heir to the throne occupied by John of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT At this point only an earldom separated him from the greatest in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Royal persecution and exiled death===
2 CONT
2 CONT But soon after this William de Braose fell out of favour with King John of England. The precise reasons remain obscure. King John cited overdue monies that de Braose owed the Crown from his estates. But the King's actions went far
2 CONC  beyond what would be necessary to recover the debt. He distrained de Braose's English estates in Sussex and Devon and sent a force to invade Wales to seize the de Braose domains there. Beyond that, he sought de Braose's wife, Mau
2 CONC d de St. Valery, who, the story goes, had made no secret of her belief that King John had murdered Arthur of Brittany. Gerald of Wales describes Maud as a 'prudent and chaste woman' who bore her husband three sons William, Giles a
2 CONC nd Reginald de Braose.
2 CONT
2 CONT De Braose fled to Ireland, then returned to Wales as King John had him hunted in Ireland. In Wales, William allied himself to the Welsh Prince Llywelyn the Great and helped him in rebellion against King John.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1210, William de Braose fled Wales in disguise as a beggar, to France. His wife and eldest son were captured, and he died the following year in August 1211 at Corbeil, France. He is buried in the Abbey of St. Victor in Paris by
2 CONC  a fellow exile and vociferous opponent of King John, Stephen Langton, the Archbishop of Canterbury. His hopes to return alive to Wales and a burial in Brecon were to be unfulfilled. William's wife, Maud, and eldest son, William, 
2 CONC once captured were murdered by King John, possibly starved to death incarcerated in Windsor Castle and Corfe Castle in 1210.
2 CONT
2 CONT While William had aroused the jealousy of the other barons during his rise, the arbitrary and violent manner of his fall very likely discomfited them and played a role in the Baronial uprisings of the next decade. The historian Si
2 CONC dney Painter, in his biography of King John, called it "the greatest mistake John made during his reign, as the King revealed to his Barons once and for all his capacity for cruelty".
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Junior branch of the de Braose dynasty===
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Braose's eldest son, William, although captured in hiding with his mother and starved to death in 1210, had fathered four sons. They were John, Giles, Phillip and Walter and although they were also held imprisoned they 
2 CONC were released in 1218. John, the eldest, was said to have been brought up in secret, on Gower, by a Welsh ally or retainer. On release he came under the care of his uncle Giles de Braose. John made a claim to being rightful heir o
2 CONC f the de Braose lands and titles and although the courts did not find for him, his other uncle Reginald de Braose was able to cede by a legal convention the Baronies of both Gower and Bramber to him for a fee. This established the
2 CONC  junior branch of the dynasty in its own right and further positioned the dynasty for survival at worst and at best opportunity, continued future power and influence.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Later dynasty===
2 CONT
2 CONT The middle son, Giles de Braose, exiled in France until 1213, was Bishop of Hereford from 1200 until his death in 1215. He made peace with King John and agreed terms for regaining de Braose lands in 1215 but had also made alliance
2 CONC s with the Welsh leader Llywelyn the Great. He died in 1215 before he could come into the lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's third son, Reginald de Braose reacquired his father's lands and titles for himself through simply seizing them back by force following the death of Giles. Reginald did not actually come to terms with the Crown until 1217
2 CONC  and the new, young King Henry III of England, after the death of King John. This in turn aroused the anger of Llywelyn the Great who had an understanding with Giles de Braose and the seeming duplicity caused the Welsh to attack d
2 CONC e Braose lands in Brecon and Abergavenny and Gower. Abergavenny Castle had to be rebuilt as a result. Reginald de Braose died in 1228.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's eldest daughter Matilda (also called Maud) married a prominent Welsh prince, Gruffydd ap Rhys II of Deheubarth. Another daughter, Margaret, married Walter de Lacy, Lord of Meath in Ireland and himself another powerful Ma
2 CONC rcher Lord.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Fiction===
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of the death of Maud de St. Valery and the conflict of her family with John Lackland is covered in several novels, notably Lady of Hay by Barbara Erskine. Erskine describes the life of Maud by sending a woman of the 20th
2 CONC  century by psychological regression back into the 12th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===External links===
2 CONT
2 CONT * FMG on the first son of William de Braose/Briouse
2 CONT * Holden, Brock W., "King John, the Braoses, and the Celtic Fringe, 1207-1216", Albion: Journal of British Studies v.33 (2001)
2 CONT
2 CONT from thePeerage.com:
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Briouze1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #102562, d. 9 August 1211
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=30 Apr 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT      William de Briouze was the son of William de Briouze and Bertha of Hereford.1 He married Maud de St. Valery.1 He died on 9 August 1211.1 He was buried on 10 August 1211 at Abbey of St. Victor, Paris, France.1
2 CONT
2 CONT      William de Briouze succeeded to the title of Lord of Bramber, Sussex [Feudal].1 He gained the title of Lord of Brecon [Feudal].1 He succeeded to the title of Lord de Briouze, Normandie.1 He succeeded to the title of Lord Aber
2 CONC gavenny [Feudal] after 1179.1 In 1208 as a result of his well-known quarrel with King John, his lands were forfeited.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of William de Briouze and Maud de St. Valery
2 CONT
