2 CONT William de Braose, 7th Baron Abergavenny and 4th Lord of Bramber (1144/1153 – 9 August 1211), court favourite of King John, at the peak of his power was also Lord of Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth, Radnor, Kington, Limerick
2 CONC , Glamorgan,Skenfrith, Briouze in Normandy, Grosmont, and White Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT William is the most famous member of the de Braose dynasty and his steady rise and sudden fall at the hands of King John is often taken as an example of that king's arbitrary and capricious behaviour towards his Barons.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the son of William de Braose, Third Lord of Bramber and his wife Bertha of Hereford, also known as Bertha de Pitres, (born 1130) daughter of Miles Fitz Walter, Earl of Hereford and his wife, formerly Sibyl de Neufmarch
2 CONC e, daughter of Bernard de Neufmarche. From his father he inherited the Rape of Bramber, in Sussex, and through his mother he inherited a large estate in the Welsh Marches area of modern day Monmouthshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was also called 4th Lord of Bramber. William "the Ogre of Abergavenny."
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Maud de St. Valéry, daughter of Bernard IV de St. Valéry, before 1175. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the strongest of the Marcher Lords involved in constant war with the Welsh and other lords circa 1175. He was called the "Ogre of Abergavenny" by the Welsh for the massacre of three Welsh princes, their families, and t
2 CONC heir men, which took place during a feast at his castle of Abergavenny in 1175 at Monmouthshire, Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT William inherited Bramber, in Sussex, and Builth and Radnor, in Wales, from his father in 1192. He inherited Brecknock and Abergavenny through his mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was made Sheriff of Hereford by King Richard I between 1192 and 1199. He was 4th Lord of Bramber at West Sussex, England, between 1192 and 9 August 1211.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the Normans' foremost warriors, and fought alongside King Richard (when Richard received his fatal wound) on 6 April 1199 at Châlus, Haute-Vienne, Aquitaine, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT William received from King John the lordship of Limerick in 1201 at Ireland. He was also given custody of Glamorgan, Monmouth, and Gwynllwg in Wales in return for large payments.
2 CONT
2 CONT William captured Arthur, Count of Brittany, and in charge of his imprisonment, for King John, but did not kill him, in 1202 at Mirebeau, Vienne, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was granted custody, by King John, of Glamorgan castle in Wales in October 1202. He was rewarded by King John, who gave him the grant of Gower in Wales in February 1203.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was possibly bribed by King John to keep silent about Arthur of Brittany's death by being given the city of Limerick in Ireland in July 1203.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was released from the debt to King John (which he owed for the marriage of Amabil de Limesi to his son, John on 26 March 1205). He received the "three castles"--Whitecastle, Grosmont, and Skenfrith--albeit for a high fee, 
2 CONC and was also re-appointed Sheriff of Hereford and justice itinerant of Gloucester in 1206.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was stripped, for some reason, of his office as bailiff of Glamorgan and other custodies by King John in 1207. He was deprived of all his lands and sought by King John in Ireland after 1207.
2 CONT
2 CONT William returned to Wales and joined the Welsh Prince, Llewelyn Fawr, in rebellion circa 1208. According to the Annals of Monte Fernando (1208): "Willelmus de Breaus ejicitur de Anglia et venit in Hiberniam."
2 CONT
2 CONT William fled to France via Shoreham "in the habit of a beggar" in 1210. He died on 9 August 1211 at Corbeil, Marne, France, in exile, a year after his wife and son William were starved to death by King John in their dispute with J
2 CONC ohn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his intention to be interred at St. John's, Brecon, William "the Ogre of Abergavenny" was buried in the Abbey of St. Victoire, Paris by Stephen Langton, the Archbishop of Canterbury, another of John's chief opponents, who 
2 CONC was also taking refuge there.
2 CONT
