2 CONT 1.  Sheriff of Hereford 1192-1199.  Lord of Briouze, Basse-Normandie, France; Bramber, Sussex, England; Breconshire, England; and Over Ghent. Unknown GEDCOM info: MH:N162 Unknown GEDCOM info: E57CF4B2-8913-4082-A496-A09A78C78437
1 NOTE He had been a supporter of John but  in 1208 they had a falling out.  John
2 CONT ordered William to surrender his goods and lands.  William was later declared
2 CONT an outlaw and forced to flee England leaving his family behind.  Williams parents William and  Bertha Fitzwalter
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY: Occupation: Bramber, Brecon, Over Gwent; Sheriffof Hereford 
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY: Occupation: Bramber, Brecon, Over Gwent; Sheriffof Hereford
1 NOTE  Source: Frederick Lewis Weis & Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., Ancestral Roots
2 CONT of certain American colonists..., (Edition 7, Genealogical Publishing
2 CONT Company, Baltimore, 1992), 177-6.
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Braose 4th Lord of Bramber. At his peak Lord of Bramber,
2 CONT Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth, Radnor, Kington, Limerick and the
2 CONT three castles of Skenfrith, Grosmont and Whitecastle.
2 CONT
2 CONT William inherited Bramber, Builth and Radnor from his father; Brecknock
2 CONT and Abergavenny through his mother. He was the strongest of the Marcher
2 CONT Lords involved in constant war with the Welsh and other lords. He was
2 CONT particularly hated by the Welsh for the massacre of three Welsh princes,
2 CONT their families and their men which took place during a feast at his
2 CONT castle of Abergavenny in 1175. He was sometimes known as the "Ogre of
2 CONT Abergavenny".
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the Normans' foremost warriors, he fought alongside King Richard
2 CONT at Chalus in 1199 (when Richard received his fatal wound). William
2 CONT received Limerick in 1201 from King John. He was also given custody of
2 CONT Glamorgan, Monmouth and Gwynllwg in return for large payments.
2 CONT
2 CONT William captured Arthur, Count of Brittany at Mirebeau in 1202 and was in
2 CONT charge of his imprisonment for King John. He was well rewarded in
2 CONT February 1203 with the grant of Gower. He may have had knowledge of the
2 CONT murder of Arthur and been bribed to silence by John with the city of
2 CONT Limerick in July. His honours reached their peak when he was made Sheriff
2 CONT of Herefordshire by John for 1206-7. He had held this office under
2 CONT Richard from 1192 to 1199.
2 CONT
2 CONT His fall began almost immediately. William was stripped of his office as
2 CONT bailiff of Glamorgan and other custodies by King John in 1206/7. Later he
2 CONT was deprived of all his lands and, sought by KingJohn in Ireland, he
2 CONT returned to Wales and joined the Welsh Prince Llewelyn in rebellion.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fled to France in 1210 via Shoreham "in the habit of a beggar" and
2 CONT died in exile near Paris. Despite
2 CONT intending to be interred at St. John's, Brecon, he was buried in the
2 CONT Abbey of St. Victoire, Paris by Stephen Langton, the Archbishop of
2 CONT Canterbury, another of John's chief opponents who was also taking refuge
2 CONT there.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife and son William were murdered by John, possibly starved to death
2 CONT at Windsor Castle.
