1 NOTE William de Braose 4th Lord of Bramber. At his peak Lord of Bramber, Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth, Radnor, Kington, Limerick and the three castles of Skenfrith, Grosmont and Whitecastle.
2 CONT
2 CONT William inherited Bramber, Builth and Radnor from his father; Brecknock and Abergavenny through his mother. He was the strongest of the Marcher Lords involved in constant war with the Welsh and other lords. He was particularly hat
2 CONC ed by the Welsh for the massacre of three Welsh princes, their families and their men which took place during a feast at his castle of Abergavenny in 1175. He was sometimes known as the "Ogre of Abergavenny".
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the Normans' foremost warriors, he fought alongside King Richard at Chalus in 1199 (when Richard received his fatal wound). William received Limerick in 1201 from King John. He was also given custody of Glamorgan, Monmouth 
2 CONC and Gwynllwg in return for large payments.
2 CONT
2 CONT William captured Arthur, Count of Brittany at Mirebeau in 1202 and was in charge of his imprisonment for King John. He was well rewarded in February 1203 with the grant of Gower. He may have had knowledge of the murder of Arthur a
2 CONC nd been bribed to silence by John with the city of Limerick in July. His honours reached their peak when he was made Sheriff of Herefordshire by John for 1206-7. He had held this office under Richard from 1192 to 1199.
2 CONT
2 CONT His fall began almost immediately. William was stripped of his office as bailiff of Glamorgan and other custodies by King John in 1206/7. Later he was deprived of all his lands and, sought by KingJohn in Ireland, he returned to Wa
2 CONC les and joined the Welsh Prince Llewelyn in rebellion.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fled to France in 1210 via Shoreham "in the habit of a beggar" and died in exile near Paris. Despite
2 CONT intending to be interred at St. John's, Brecon, he was buried in the Abbey of St. Victoire, Paris by Stephen Langton, the Archbishop of Canterbury, another of John's chief opponents who was also taking refuge there.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife and son William were murdered by John, possibly starved to death at Windsor Castle.
1 NOTE LORD OF BRAMBER, ETC., IN RIGHT OF HIS MOTHER, SUCCEEDED TO VAST ESTATES ON THE
2 CONT WELSH MARCHES; MURDERED A CROWD OF WELSHMEN AT ABERGAVENNY IN 1176 IN REVENGE
2 CONT FOR THE DEATH OF HIS UNCLE, HENRY OF HEREFORD - FOR THIS, THE WELSH HELD A
2 CONT HATRED OF THE BRAIOSE'S UNTIL THEY WERE ABLE TO TAKE REVENGE ON WILLIAM III'S
2 CONT GRANDSON IN 1230; SHERIFF OF HEREFORD 1192-1199; GRANTED THE LORDSHIP OF
2 CONT LIMERICK BY KING JOHN 1/10/1201, AS WELL AS OTHER HOLDINGS; AFTER REFUSING TO
2 CONT HAND OVER HOSTAGES TO KING JOHN TO DEMAND FEALTY, WILLIAM AND MAUD FLED TO
2 CONT IRELAND - THEIR LANDS WERE SEIZED BY THE KING - AFTER A FAILED ATTEMPT TO
2 CONT RETAKE HIS HOLDINGS BY FORCE, WILLIAM AND HIS SONS WERE FORCED TO RETURN TO
2 CONT IRELAND; AFTER REFUSING PROMISES TO COMPENSATE THE KING, WILLIAM III WAS
2 CONT DECLARED AN OUTLAW, AND MAUD AND HER SONS WERE TAKEN PRISONER IN WINDSOR
2 CONT CASTLE, WHERE SHE AND HER SON WILLIAM WERE STARVED TO DEATH; 5TH BARON DE
2 CONT BRAIOSE
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY: William de BRAOSE Lord Brecknock 5th Baron de Braiose (1144-1211) [Pedigree] 
2 CONT Son of William de BRAOSE 1st Baron of Gwentland (1112-1179) and Berta of Hereford (1123-) 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY:     b. ABT 1144, Bramber, Sussex, Eng.
2 CONT     d. 9 Aug 1211, Corbeil
2 CONT     d. 9 Aug 1211, Corbeil, near Paris, France
2 CONT     bur. 10 Aug 1211, St. Victor's Abbey, Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Married Matilda (Maud) de ST. VALERIE (1148-1210) 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: William de BRAOS (1200-1210) m(1) Maud (Matilda) de CLARE (1175-1213) 
2 CONT Reginald de BRAOSE (1171-1228) m(1) Grace de BRIWERE (1186-1251) 
2 CONT Margaret de BRAOSE (1177-) m(2) Walter de LACY Lord of Meath, Ireland (1172-1241) 
2 CONT References: [AR7],[EnglishP],[ConverseA],[PlantagenetA], [GENSERV],[MCS4],[MC],[RD500]
