1 OCCU Prince of Lower Powys; Lord Bromfield aka Gruffydd Maelor II, Prince of Northern Powys. Became ally of Llewellyn ap Gruffyd., Last Prince of Powys-Vadog, Lord of Bromfield and the Castle of Dinas Bran, Prince of pwys
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} See Peter Bartrum, https://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/5573/Bleddyn%20ap%20Cynfyn%204.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (December 26, 2016; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: The Ancestry of Owain Glyndwr; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id200.html. (Steven Ferry, September 13, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Towering high above the Dee Valley and the bustling town of Llangollen, home of the International Eisteddfod, Castell Dinas Bran occupies one of Britain's most spectacular sites. A rugged, foreboding pinnacle, the hillock was the 
2 CONC ideal spot to erect a castle. It seemed completely impenetrable, commanded views for miles around, and offered quick recognition of an approaching visitor, whether friend or foe. Yet, the native Welsh princes of Powys occupied the
2 CONC  hilltop for only a few decades.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, that same site is open to exploration by the public. Forced to climb to the summit, modern visitors experience the struggle and the exhilaration that the castle's medieval inhabitants - and their Edwardian attackers - must 
2 CONC have felt. Without a doubt, the walk is a breathtaking challenge. However, that climb heightens the allure of Dinas Bran. And, it demonstrates the stark reality of medieval castle life.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Dinas Bran" is variously translated as "Crow Castle," "Crow City," "Hill of the Crow," or "Bran's Stronghold." The castle first appears in 12th century historical documents as part of a medieval piece entitled "Fouke le Fitz Wary
2 CONC n,"or "The Romance of Fulk Fitzwarine." While this work claimed that the castle, known as "Chastiel Bran," was in ruin as early as 1073, the remains we see today date to the occupation of the princes of Powys Fadog in the mid 13th
2 CONC  century. Possibly, the Chastiel Bran mentioned in the romance was a Norman timber castle, but nothing of substance supports this conjecture. However, the encompassing ditch and earthen embankments, which enclose the southern and 
2 CONC eastern portions of the stone fortress, do date to the Iron Age. They remind us that this hilltop had strategic value long before the princes of Powys, or the Normans, ventured into the region. Interestingly, the word, "Dinas," ha
2 CONC s its origins in the Iron Age as well, and is found in the names of Iron Age hillforts throughout Wales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Reid (1973) speculated that the hill at Dinas Bran was occupied in the 8th century by a man named Eliseg. The same Eliseg also gave his name to an ancient pillar that stands just north of Valle Crucis Abbey, near Llangollen. The m
2 CONC ystery man may have been an ancestor of the princes of Powys who later dominated the area, but there is no real proof to support this assertion.
2 CONT
2 CONT The historical record also conflicts over whom really built the remains at Dinas Bran. The most reliable sources state that Gruffydd Maelor II, son of Madog ap Gruffydd Maelor I, began the castle in the late 1260's. The elder Mado
2 CONC g founded nearby Valle Crucis Abbey, where both men were buried. Some references offer an earlier date for the castle, placing it in the 1230's, when Madog would have been alive and, therefore, its builder. Regardless of whom actu
2 CONC ally erected the buildings that now survive in ruin, they were part of the last building effort at Dinas Bran. Incredibly, assuming the later date to be most accurate, the Welsh princes only occupied the site for two decades.
2 CONT
2 CONT During those final two decades, the castle on the hilltop became a prized possession of the princes of Powys Fadog. Dinas Bran's power did not go unnoticed by English forces. In 1277, during Edward I's initial foray into Wales, th
2 CONC e Earl of Lincoln, Henry de Lacy, besieged the castle. The Welsh lord of Dinas Bran was forced to submit to the invading army, which promptly set the site afire, completely destroying it. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Even after its devastation, Henry de Lacy marveled at the vigor of the forlorn castle, and recommended that the king make repairs. Lacy noted that the exterior masonry was still intact and that the restored castle could be used by
2 CONC  the English, who faced more battles with the Welsh. The Earl of Lincoln exclaimed to his king that there was no greater Welsh castle, nor one in England that could compare with the might of Castell Dinas Bran. Edward I was not im
2 CONC pressed, and the castle was never restored.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after that, however, Castell Dinas Bran and the lands of Bromfield were granted to John de Warenne, Earl of Surrey, a formidable Marcher lord. However, Warenne was more interested fortifying his estates at Holt than in rep
2 CONC airing his decaying Welsh stronghold. In 1282, during Edward's second campaign into Wales, Dafydd ap Gruffydd, Llywelyn the Last's rebellious brother, held Dinas Bran. But, Edward's armies captured Dafydd soon after Llywelyn's dea
2 CONC th at Cilmeri, and took him to a painful execution in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT As late as the 15th century, Castell Dinas Bran still had value. Then the property of Thomas Fitzalan, Earl of Arundel, the castle was targeted by the legendary folkhero, Owain Glyndwr, who unsuccessfully attempted to capture the 
2 CONC site in 1402. During the reign of King Henry VIII, John Leland, the king's well-traveled chronicler, described Dinas Bran as a total ruin. He claimed that the only living being willing to inhabit the castle ruins was the eagle who
2 CONC  returned each year to breed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In more recent centuries, Castell Dinas Bran was recognized for its dramatic placement high above the Welsh valleys. Painters, like Turner and Wilson, captured the essence of the place on canvas. Wordsworth also visited Dinas Bran
2 CONC , lamenting the castle's fate as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "Relics of kings, wreck of forgotten wars, To the winds abandoned and the prying stars." 
2 CONT
2 CONT From afar, we can readily spot the tumbledown ruins of Castell Dinas Bran. In many ways, it was a typical Welsh-built castle. Surprisingly simple in design, its stone curtain wall traced the outline of the hilltop upon which it st
2 CONC ood. Essentially rectangular, the wall enclosed an area about 300 feet long and 130 feet wide. At the eastern end, a plain square keep connected to the curtain wall. Like many other keeps, access was at the first level above the g
2 CONC round floor, using a movable wooden ladder.
2 CONT
2 CONT Immediately to the right (north) of the keep stood the great twin-towered gatehouse, a primitive structure when compared to its Edwardian counterparts under construction at the same time. This native Welsh gatehouse was rib-vaulte
2 CONC d and had a narrow passage into the inner bailey. Little remains except the foundations.
2 CONT
2 CONT Midway along the southern wall, a characteristically-Welsh apsidal tower protrudes outward from the castle walls. The intriguing D-shaped tower still offers panoramic views of the less-sloping western approach to the castle, the s
2 CONC ector which would have been most vulnerable to attack. Adjacent to this tower are the scanty remains of the hall block, once the castle's focal point, where the lord would have entertained his guests. The castle's priest may have 
2 CONC offered religious services on an upper level. 
2 CONT
2 CONT TALES OF DINAS BRAN
2 CONT
2 CONT Legends associate Castell Dinas Bran with the king of Britain mentioned in the "Mabinogion," whose story dates to Arthurian times and whose name (Bran) translates to "raven." The king was killed after invading Ireland, and his hea
2 CONC d was buried in what may have been London's Tower Hill. Stories then added that, as long as Bran's head remained buried, Britain would be safeguarded. Perhaps, the idea that the head is linked with the Tower of London derives from
2 CONC  the ongoing presence of real ravens, creatures also said to have the magical power to protect the kingdom from disaster. Tales also state, however, that King Arthur retrieved the head, choosing to protect Britain himself rather t
2 CONC han resorting to the power of a buried body part. Claims have also been made that the Holy Grail or a golden harp are hidden in the hillock at Dinas Bran and that fairies dwell there.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to "The Romance of Fulk Fitzwarine," the Normans pushed their way into the eastern borderlands of Wales and stopped just beneath the ruins of Dinas Bran. An arrogant knight, Pain Peveril, noticed the crumbling walls and 
2 CONC learned that the site was once the home of King Bran. Since Bran's demise, no one had enough courage to stay overnight inside the remains, for fear of evil spirits. To prove their mettle, Pain and some of his cohorts climbed up to
2 CONC  the ruins, determined to endure the night. During that night, a storm arose and forced the men to seek shelter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Suddenly, an evil, mace-wielding giant appeared. This giant was the notorious Gogmagog, a man possessed by an evil spirit who had terrorized the countryside for years. Pain defended his men with his shield, protected with a cross,
2 CONC  a shield so sturdy that it withstood the penetration of the giant's mace. His brazen defiance startled the giant and Pain swiftly stabbed him with his sword. As Gogmagog died, the evil spirit recounted KingBran's bravery against 
2 CONC the giant's attacks. Bran had even built the palace atop the hillock to thwart the giant and enraged the evil spirit inside Gogmagog. Then, the giant forced Bran and his followers to flee. The dying spirit also claimed that a grea
2 CONC t treasure, including a golden ox, was buried beneath the hill. The next day, King William learned of Pain's adventure, disposed of Gogmagog's body, and kept the mace as a spoil of battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT (This tale was paraphrased from Oman's account in the book, "Castles," written originally in the 1920's. It is a classic text on British castles.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, Castell Dinas Bran overlooks the Welsh valleys, as it has done for centuries. It is now in the care of the local authority, and is open freely any reasonable time. One can only wonder if the golden ox is still embedded some
2 CONC where inside the hill! 
2 CONT
2 CONT There’s nothing like a medieval castle to exhume one’s sense of romance however deeply it may be buried beneath the mundane. And Wales, that piece of geography that bulges from British soil into the Irish Sea like a full belly, ha
2 CONC s close to 400 of them in various states of restoration and ruin. But only one claims legendary ties to the Holy Grail: Castell Dinas Bran, which translated into English means ‘castle of the fort of the crow (or raven)’. Some say 
2 CONC it is the Arthurian Corbenic Castle itself and the Grail is still there buried deep beneath the rocky remains.  The word in old French for raven or crow is Corbenic and the word for raven in Welsh is Bran; same castle name, differ
2 CONC ent tongue. It’s all in the name and based upon French Grail romance manuscripts from the 12th and 13th centuries that tell the story of how the Holy Grail came to rest in a castle in North Wales. Dinas Bran was the physical inspi
2 CONC ration for these romances.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gruffydd II ap Madog, Lord of Dinas Bran
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd Maelor II, Prince of Powys Fadog 1236 - 1269.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lineage
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Madog ap Gruffydd Maelor and inherited his father's lands and title in partible succession along with his four brothers Gruffydd Ial, Maredudd, Hywel and Madog Fychan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kingdom of Powys Fadog had previously been unified under one leader but now had five and was subjected to outside forces as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Emma, the widow of Henry Touchet, and daughter of Henry Audley and Bertred Mainwaring.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Death & Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1269 (or 1270) leaving issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Madog II, succeeded his father and was killed in battle with the English in 1277.
2 CONT
2 CONT Llywelyn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Owain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffydd Fychan I succeeded his eldest brother in 1277 and died in 1289.
2 CONT
2 CONT Angharad 1308. m (after 1261) William Le Botiler of Wem, Shropshire (-1283).
2 CONT
2 CONT Powys Fadog was divided in accordance with Welsh custom between his sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Cynllaith was divided between Llywellyn and Owain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gruffudd had Iâl and Edeirnion, which included Glyn Dyfrdwy.[1]
2 CONT
