1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''The Wikipedia article about his father, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walter_de_Riddlesford Walter de Riddlesford], cites the following paragraph with three sources.''' 
2 CONT >"He died in 1226 and was succeeded by his son Walter. His granddaughter, Emmeline, married Hugh de Lacy, 1st Earl of Ulster (as his second wife) and, then, Stephen Longespée, son of William Longespée, 3rd Earl of Salisbury and gr
2 CONC andson of Henry II of England (one of their daughters was Ela Longespee).[4][5][6]"
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4.''' Eds, (1850) ''Proceedings of the Royal Irish Academy'', Vol. 4 Royal Irish Academy, [https://books.google.com/books?id=D3lYw61ES4wC&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q=485&f=false Google Books], 
2 CONC p. 485. — '''Note:''' This book mentions "Walter de Riddelsford" whose daughter Emmeline married "Stephen de Longspey" – she being "heiress" of her father a "relict of Hugh de Lacy, first Earl of Ulster." It's assumed that this un
2 CONC sourced passage is referring to the current Geni relationship arrangement.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''5.''' Adams, Arthur, et al (1964) ''The Magna Charta Sureties, 1215: The Barons Named in the Magna Charta, 1215, and Some of Their Descendants who Settled in America, 1607-1650'', p. 116. — No offense to the author and editors 
2 CONC of this book which doesn't begin to cite sufficient primary and secondary sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''6.''' '''Note:''' The Wikisource biography about  "Lacy, Hugh de (d.1242?)" does not mention Walter or surname Riddlesford.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
