1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT MAY 1215
1 OCCU bishop-elect of Durham, provost of Beverley
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Annales Dunelmenses: "Henricus rex pater Iohannis regis genuerat   eum de uxoram cuisdam militis Angli dict Radulfi Bluet"
2 CONT
2 CONT {King Henry, father of King John, engendered him upon the wife of a certain English knight called Ralph Bluet"
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.oxforddnb.com/view/printable/48555
2 CONT
2 CONT Late in June 1175 Iorwerth came to an agreement with the Lord Rhys, with whose support he regained Caerleon from King Henry. The recovery of Caerleon may also have had something to do with the sexual liaison between the king and I
2 CONC orwerth's daughter, Nest Bloet, with whom the king had an illegitimate son, Morgan (surnamed Bloet, from his being brought up in the family of Nest's husband, Ralph Bloet (d. 1199) of Silchester).
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>--------------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ====en.Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morgan_(bishop) Morgan (bishop)]
2 CONT * Morgan was a medieval Bishop-elect of Durham.
2 CONT * "Morgan was an illegitimate son of King Henry II of England and Nesta, daughter of Iorwerth ab Owain, Lord of Caerleon. Nesta was married to Sir Ralph Bloet, who raised Morgan as his son. The date of his birth is unknown but pre
2 CONC sumed to be toward the end of King Henry's life.[1]
2 CONT * "Morgan was the provost of Beverley before being nominated to the see of Durham between 7 March and 7 May 1215. He was never consecrated because Pope Innocent III refused consecration on the grounds of his illegitimacy.[2] Innoc
2 CONC ent offered to confirm the election and allow the episcopal consecration if Morgan would swear that he was the son of Nesta and her husband Ralph and not King Henry. Morgan, after deliberation, replied that it was unthinkable for 
2 CONC him to deny his father the king.[1]
2 CONT * "After his accession to the throne of England, John, Morgan's half-brother, was known to be generous to the entire Bloet family.[1]"
2 CONT ** '''[1]''' Given-Wilson, Chris, and Alice Curteis. 2023. ''The Royal Bastards of Medieval England''. Milton Park, Abingdon-on-Thames, Oxfordshire, UK: Routledge. [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Royal_Bastards_of_Mediev
2 CONC al_England/WlfgEAAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=Morgan Google Books], ''Snippet view''. '''In Libraries:''' [https://search.worldcat.org/title/10145237 WorldCat.org]. p. 99
2 CONT ** '''[2]''' Greenway, Diana E. 1971. “Durham 1066-1300.” In ''Fasti Ecclesiae Anglicanae 1066-1300:Monastic Cathedrals (Northern and Southern Provinces)'', 2:29–32. London, England: Institute of Historical Research. British Histo
2 CONC ry Online, Archived @[https://web.archive.org/web/20110719101948/http://british-history.ac.uk/report.aspx?compid=33859 Archive.org Wayback Machine]. 
2 CONT *** “'''[Morgan'''
2 CONT *** “Morgan, provost of Beverley, illegitimate son of Henry II, el. between 7 March and 7 May 1215 on king's suggestion (Rot. Litt. Claus. 1 190b, 204; cf. ''ibid''. p. 268''b''; ''Durham Annals'' pp. 1-2; ''Script. Tres'' p. 35).
2 CONC  But cons. refused by pope on grounds of illegitimacy (''Script. Tres'' p. 35).]”
2 CONT ** '''''See also''''' - Turner, James. 2023. ''The Royal Bastards of Twelfth Century England: Power and Blood''. Barnsley, England: Pen & Sword Books. [https://search.worldcat.org/title/1369290165 WorldCat.org].
2 CONT <nowiki>--------------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20Kings%201066-1603.htm#HenriIIdied1189B
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRI d’Anjou, son of GEOFFROY "le Bel/Plantagenet" Comte d'Anjou et de Maine & his wife [Empress] Matilda [Maud] of England (Le Mans, Anjou 5 Mar 1133-Château de Chinon 6 Jul 1189, bur Abbaye de Fontevrault[370]).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress (5): NESTA, wife of RALPH Bloet, daughter of ---.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Henry II had one illegitimate child by Mistress (5): 12. MORGAN ([1180/89]-after 1213).
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert de Graystane´s early 14th century History of the Church of Durham records the election as bishop of Durham in 1213 of “Morganus frater Regis Johannis et Galfridi archiepiscopi Eboracensis, præpositusque Beverlacensis”, that
2 CONC  his appointment was blocked by Rome because he was born “spurius...Henricus pater eius” to “uxore...militis...Radulphi Bloeth”, and that the Pope offered to confirm the election if he declared that the king was not his father, wh
2 CONC ich he refused to do[545]. Provost of Berkeley, Yorkshire 1201. Elected Bishop of Durham in 1213.
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>----------------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ====The Henry Project: [https://fasg.org/projects/henryproject/data/henry002.htm The Ancestors of King Henry II of England]
2 CONT * "...Morgan, d. 1216×7, provost of Beverley 1201-1216×7, bishop-elect of Durham in 1213.  Roger de Hoveden [iv, 174] states that he was made provost by his brother archbishop Geoffrey in 1201, and that it was said ("ut dicebatur"
2 CONC ) that he was a son of king Henry. Hist. Dunelm. [pp. 31, 35] and Ann. Dunelm. [s.a. 1213], evidently relying on a common source, state that Morgan was a brother of king John [and also of archbishop Geoffrey, Hist. Dunelm.], an il
2 CONC legitimate son of king Henry by the wife of Ralph Bloet, a knight, who, elected as bishop of Durham in 1213, would have been installed in that office if he had been willing to name Ralph Bloet as his father, but that he refused to
2 CONC  disavow his relationship to the king. Morgan was living in Feb.×March 1216, when the pope granted him a dispensation [Cheney (1967), 177, #1067], and deceased by 6 November 1217, when Pope Honorius III wrote a letter to Archbisho
2 CONC p Walter Gray of York regarding the goods of the recently deceased "M.", provost of Beverley [Reg. Gray 130]...."
2 CONT
2 CONT <nowiki>----------------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.kingrichard3.com/genealogy/Blewett%20Family%20History.pdf Page 3-4 of 9
2 CONT
2 CONT >“Given-Wilson and Curteis (p. 9, 99 , 179) add Morgan to their list of “identifiable” bastards of Henry II, suggesting that the reason this illegitimate son of Henry II is so little known is that the king died before Morgan was o
2 CONC ld enough to profit from his father’s assistance and influence in his career. Having been elected to the see of Durham, Morgan in 1213 requested and was refused papal confirmation to the see because of his papal illegitimacy. Give
2 CONC n-Wilson and Curteis (p. 99) state that Pope Innocent offered to confirm Morgan as Bishop of Durham if Morgan would swear that he was the son of Nest and her husband, Ralph Bloet, rather than the son of Nest and Henry II. Morgan r
2 CONC efused to deny his parentage.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Comments
2 CONT
2 CONT >Removed death data of "1216 on pilgrimage."  The last known references is to the quashing of his nomination as bishop of Durham by the pope, undated, but which was after his nomination of between 7 March and 7 May 1215.
