1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Elen-ferch-Llywelyn/6000000002754410308?through=6000000002184297964, the younger
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT updated 7 November 2024
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Elen ferch Llywelyn''' was born about 1208. She was the daughter of Llywelyn Fawr ap Iorwerth and Joan, natural daughter of King John of England. She is sometimes called Elen, or Helen, the Elder, because another Helen is attri
2 CONC buted to Llywelyn, born a couple of decades later, and referred to as Helen the Younger. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elen the Elder, also called Elen of Wales, died before 5 August 1253 when royal escheators were ordered to restore to her husband Robert de Quincy "lands that his deceased wife had held, as he had a child by her." [7] An inquest p
2 CONC ost mortem was held for her on 10 November 1253.
2 CONT
2 CONT A writ after the death of "Eleanor, sometime the wife of John Earl of Chester", dated "the eve of St Martin 38 Hen III", records the "partition of her lands between Si J. de Bayllol, Robert de Brus, and Henry de Hasting, the heirs
2 CONC  of the said earl"[291]. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Scotland,_Earl_of_Huntingdon
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John of Scotland (or John de Scotia or John le Scot), 9th Earl of Huntingdon and 7th Earl of Chester (c. 1207 – 6 June 1237), sometimes known as "the Scot", was an Anglo-Scottish magnate, the son of David of Scotland, Earl of Hunt
2 CONC ingdon by his wife Matilda of Chester, daughter of Hugh de Kevelioc.
2 CONT
2 CONT John married Elen ferch Llywelyn, daughter of Llywelyn the Great, in about 1222.
2 CONT
2 CONT John died childless on 6 June 1237, aged 30. He too, like his uncle Ranulph before him, left three sisters as his co-heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Ferch_Llewelyn-1
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew of Paris records that it was suspected that his wife "filia Leolini" poisoned John “the Scot”[290]. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was forced by King Henry III (her mother's half-brother) to marry Sir Robert de Quincy, before  5 December 1237. Robert de Quincy, Lord of Ware, was the son of Saher de Quency, 1st Earl of Winchester and Margaret de Beaumont. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Helen ferch Llywelyn and Robert de Quincy, Lord of Ware, born after their marriage in 1237:
2 CONT # Anne, a nun[6]
2 CONT # Joan de Quincy (-25 Nov 1284), married Humphrey de Bohun, son of Humphrey de Bohun, Earl of Hereford and Essex, & his first wife Maud de Lusignan[6]
2 CONT # Hawise ([1250]-before 27 Mar 1285), married (before 5 Feb 1268) Baldwin Wake, son of Hugh Wake & his wife Joan de Stuteville[6] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Robert de Quincy died at a tournament at Blie in August 1257, without male issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT Their daughter, Hawise, was married to Baldwin Wake, Lord Wake of Lidel. Hawise and Baldwin’s granddaughter, Margaret Wake, was the mother of Joan of Kent, later Princess of Wales. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of Kent was the wife of Edward, the Black Prince and the mother of Richard II of England, who was deposed and died without heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess of Huntingdon and Chester
2 CONT House of Aberffraw (by birth)
2 CONT House of Dunkeld (by marriage)
2 CONT The arms of the Aberffraw House of Gwynedd were traditionally first used by Elen's grandfather, Iorwerth Drwyndwn.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Disambiguation
2 CONT
2 CONT There is also a record of a "Helen" daughter of "Llywelyn of Wales" who married Mormaer Maol Choluim II, Earl of Fife and later married Domhnall I, Earl of Mar. The dates appear to rule out this being Elen, since Maol Chaluim II d
2 CONC id not die until 1266 while Elen's death is recorded in 1253. Some genealogists propose the existence of another Elen, an illegitimate daughter born towards the end of Llywelyn's life, but there is no clear evidence for this. Anot
2 CONC her possibility is that this Helen might have been an illegitimate daughter of Llywelyn the Last born when he was a young man, but there is also no evidence of the theory being true. More likely this lady was Susannah ferch Llywel
2 CONC yn ab Iorwerth, who was sent to England in 1228 and married the earl of Fife in the summer of 1237.
2 CONT
2 CONT See more at https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/WALES.htm#Llywellyndied1240B
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Llywelyn had four illegitimate children by Mistresses (3) - (7):
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.   [HELEN] (before [1230]-after 16 Feb 1295).   ...    m [firstly] [as his first wife,] MALCOLM Earl of Fife, son of DUNCAN Macduff of Fife & his wife Alice Corbet (-1266).  [m secondly (after 1266) DONALD Earl of Mar, son of WI
2 CONC LLIAM Earl of Mar & his first wife Elizabeth Comyn of Buchan (-after 25 Jul 1297).]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Genealogy
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/WALES.htm#Llywellyndied1240B
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Prince Llywelyn''' m secondly/thirdly (after 16 Apr 1205) '''JOAN''' [of England], illegitimate daughter of JOHN King of England & his mistress Clementia Pinel (-30 Mar 1237).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Llywelyn and his second/third wife had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         '''HELEN''' (-1253 before 24 Oct).  The Annales Cestrienses record in 1222 that “Johannes filius comitis David” married “filiam Lewelini” in accordance with the agreement between him and “comitem Cestrie”[307].  The Ann
2 CONC als of Dunstable record that “Johannes comes Cestriæ” died in 1237 and “eius uxor…filia Lewelini” married “Roberto [de Quinci]” against her father´s wishes[308].  The primary source which confirms her name has not yet been identif
2 CONC ied.  Matthew Paris records that it was suspected that his wife "filia Leolini" poisoned John “the Scot”[309].  A writ after the death of "Eleanor, sometime the wife of John Earl of Chester", dated "the eve of St Martin 38 Hen III
2 CONC ", records the "partition of her lands between Si J. de Bayllol, Robert de Brus, and Henry de Hasting, the heirs of the said earl"[310].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (1222) '''JOHN "the Scot" Earl of Huntingdon''', son of JOHN of Scotland Earl of Huntingdon & his wife Matilda of Chester ([1207]-Darnal [5/7] Jun 1237, bur Chester St Werburg).  He was created Earl of Chester 21 Nov 123
2 CONC 2.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (1237 before 5 Dec) '''ROBERT de Quincy''', son of SAHER Earl of Winchester & his wife Margaret of Leicester (-Aug 1257). 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
