2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: Abt 1175, Bramber, Sussex, England & Gower, Wales 808,3672,3674 Married (1): Questionable marriage 1107,9110 Married (2): Before 1196 803,808 Died: 9 Aug 1211, Corbeil, Marne, Champagne, France 803,808,3672,3674,3675,3676 Bu
2 CONC ried: 10 Aug 1211, Abbey of St. Victor, Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT    Another name for William was William DE BRAIOSE.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Ancestral File Number: 84ZZ-P5.
2 CONT
2 CONT    General Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Braose, born perhaps c1175, died Corfe or Windsor Castle 1210, of starvation by order of King John, son of William de Braose, died 1211, Lord of Bramber, Sussex, by his wife Maud de St Valery. [Magna Charta Sureties]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William did not accompany King Richard on Crusade but fought with King John against Philip in Normandy (1203/4). King John demanded William as a hostage for his father's loyalty in 1208. His mother Maud refused and the fled to Ire
2 CONC land. In 1210 John prepared an expedition to Ireland. Maud and William escaped Ireland, but were apprehended in Scotland. William the father was in Wales at the time. It is believed that Maud and William were starved to death at W
2 CONC indsor Castle (Some say Corfe).
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William, who perished by starvation with his mother at Windsor m. Maud, dau. of the Earl of Clare, with whom he had the town of Buckingham, in frank marriage, and left a son, John. [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited a
2 CONC nd Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, England, 1883, p. 72, Braose, Baron Braose, of Gower]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT At his peak Lord of Bramber, Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth, Radnor, Kington, Limerick and the three castles of Skenfrith, Grosmont (right) and Whitecastle.
2 CONT
2 CONT William inherited Bramber, Builth and Radnor from his father; Brecknock and Abergavenny through his mother. He was the strongest of the Marcher Lords involved in constant war with the Welsh and other lords. He was particularly hat
2 CONC ed by the Welsh for the massacre of three Welsh princes, their families and their men which took place during a feast at his castle of Abergavenny in 1175. He was sometimes known as the "Ogre of Abergavenny". One of the Normans' f
2 CONC oremost warriors, he fought alongside King Richard at Chalus in 1199 (when Richard received his fatal wound). 
2 CONT
2 CONT William received Limerick in 1201 from King John. He was also given custody of Glamorgan, Monmouth and Gwynllwg in return for large payments. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William captured Arthur, Count of Brittany at Mirebeau in 1202 and was in charge of his imprisonment for King John. He was well rewarded in February 1203 with the grant of Gower. He may have had knowledge of the murder of Arthur a
2 CONC nd been bribed to silence by John with the city of Limerick in July. His honours reached their peak when he was made Sheriff of Herefordshire by John for 1206-7. He had held this office under Richard from 1192 to 1199. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His fall began almost immediately. William was stripped of his office as bailiff of Glamorgan and other custodies by King John in 1206/7. Later he was deprived of all his lands and, sought by John in Ireland, he returned to Wales 
2 CONC and joined the Welsh Prince Llewelyn in rebellion. He fled to France in 1210 via Shoreham "in the habit of a beggar" and died in exile near Paris. Despite intending to be interred at St. John's, Brecon, he was buried in the Abbey 
2 CONC of St. Victoire, Paris by Stephen Langton, the Archbishop of Canterbury, another of John's chief opponents who was also taking refuge there. His wife and son William were murdered by John, possibly starved to death at Windsor Cast
2 CONC le. 594,808,8925,9110,9111 http://www.celtic-casimir.com/webtree/3/32743.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT    Marriage Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT William married Maud DE FAY, daughter of Ralph DE FAY of Bromley and Beatrice DE TURNHAM, in Questionable marriage 1107,9110. The marriage ended in divorce. (Maud DE FAY was born about 1205 in Bromley, Poplar, Surrey, England and 
2 CONC died in 1249 9112.)
2 CONT
2 CONT     Marriage Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT William also married Matilda "Maud" DE CLARE, daughter of Richard DE CLARE MCS, 3rd Earl of Hertford and Amice Fitzrobert DE MELLENT Countess of Gloucester (1183-1224), before 1196 803,808. The marriage ended in divorce. (Matilda 
2 CONC "Maud" DE CLARE was born in 1182 in Lincoln, Lincolnshire, England 803,808 and died in 1249 in England www.celtic-casimir.com
2 CONT
2 CONT William IV "The Younger" DE BRAOSE 4th Lord of Bramber, 11 Lord Abergavenny 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: Abt 1175, Bramber, Sussex, England & Gower, Wales  Married (1): Questionable marriage    Married (2): Before 1196  Died: 9 Aug 1211, Corbeil, Marne, Champagne, France Buried: 10 Aug 1211, Abbey of St. Victor, Paris, France  
2 CONT
2 CONT Another name for William was William DE BRAIOSE.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestral File Number: 84ZZ-P5.   
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: William de Braose, born perhaps c1175, died Corfe or Windsor Castle 1210, of starvation by order of King John, son of William de Braose, died 1211, Lord of Bramber, Sussex, by his wife Maud de St Valery. [Magna Char
2 CONC ta Sureties]    William did not accompany King Richard on Crusade but fought with King John against Philip in Normandy (1203/4). King John demanded William as a hostage for his father's loyalty in 1208. His mother Maud refused and
2 CONC  the fled to Ireland. In 1210 John prepared an expedition to Ireland. Maud and William escaped Ireland, but were apprehended in Scotland. William the father was in Wales at the time. It is believed that Maud and William were starv
2 CONC ed to death at Windsor Castle (Some say Corfe).   William, who perished by starvation with his mother at Windsor m. Maud, dau. of the Earl of Clare, with whom he had the town of Buckingham, in frank marriage, and left a son, John.
2 CONC  [Sir Bernard Burke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Burke's Peerage, Ltd., London, England, 1883, p. 72, Braose, Baron Braose, of Gower]    At his peak Lord of Bramber, Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth, Rad
2 CONC nor, Kington, Limerick and the three castles of Skenfrith, Grosmont (right) and Whitecastle.  William inherited Bramber, Builth and Radnor from his father; Brecknock and Abergavenny through his mother. He was the strongest of the 
2 CONC Marcher Lords involved in constant war with the Welsh and other lords. He was particularly hated by the Welsh for the massacre of three Welsh princes, their families and their men which took place during a feast at his castle of A
2 CONC bergavenny in 1175. He was sometimes known as the "Ogre of Abergavenny". One of the Normans' foremost warriors, he fought alongside King Richard at Chalus in 1199 (when Richard received his fatal wound).   William received Limeric
2 CONC k in 1201 from King John. He was also given custody of Glamorgan, Monmouth and Gwynllwg in return for large payments.   William captured Arthur, Count of Brittany at Mirebeau in 1202 and was in charge of his imprisonment for King 
2 CONC John. He was well rewarded in February 1203 with the grant of Gower. He may have had knowledge of the murder of Arthur and been bribed to silence by John with the city of Limerick in July. His honours reached their peak when he wa
2 CONC s made Sheriff of Herefordshire by John for 1206-7. He had held this office under Richard from 1192 to 1199.   His fall began almost immediately. William was stripped of his office as bailiff of Glamorgan and other custodies by Ki
2 CONC ng John in 1206/7. Later he was deprived of all his lands and, sought by John in Ireland, he returned to Wales and joined the Welsh Prince Llewelyn in rebellion. He fled to France in 1210 via Shoreham "in the habit of a beggar" an
2 CONC d died in exile near Paris. Despite intending to be interred at St. John's, Brecon, he was buried in the Abbey of St. Victoire, Paris by Stephen Langton, the Archbishop of Canterbury, another of John's chief opponents who was also
2 CONC  taking refuge there. His wife and son William were murdered by John, possibly starved to death at Windsor Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information: William married Maud DE FAY, daughter of Ralph DE FAY of Bromley and Beatrice DE TURNHAM, in Questionable marriage. The marriage ended in divorce. (Maud DE FAY was born about 1205 in Bromley, Poplar, Surrey, 
2 CONC England and died in 1249.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information: William also married Matilda "Maud" DE CLARE, daughter of Richard DE CLARE MCS, 3rd Earl of Hertford and Amice Fitzrobert DE MELLENT Countess of Gloucester (1183-1224), before 1196. The marriage ended in divo
2 CONC rce. (Matilda "Maud" DE CLARE was born in 1182 in Lincoln, Lincolnshire, England and died in 1249 in England.)
