2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SPANISH%20NOBILITY%20LATER%20MEDIEVAL.htm#PedroManriquedied1202
2 CONT
2 CONT PEDRO Manrique de Lara, son of conde MANRIQUE Pérez de Lara & his wife Ermesinde Ctss de Narbonne (-Jan 1202, bur Santa María de Huerta).  The Nobiliario of Pedro Conde de Barcelos names "Conde D. Pedro de Molina, D. Mafalda Manri
2 CONC que, D. Maria Manrique" as the children of "D. Manrique de Lara" and his wife "D. Hermesenda, hija de D. Almerique el primer señor de Narbonna"[1446]. …
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly MARGARET, consanguinea of HENRY II King of England, daughter of --- (-[17 Nov 1189/1195]).  A charter dated "X Kal Feb 1221" (Spanish Era = A. D. 23 Jan 1183) at Anjou records a donation by "P…comes de Lara…comitisse Ma
2 CONC rger´ uxori mee consanguinee H...angl´ regis" of "Molmera et Handaluz et Agusinu et Eles et Pulucranke…in Hyspania" to Llanthony abbey witnessed by "comes Gaufredus Britannie, J. sine terra, J. comes J, Mauricius de Creon senescal
2 CONC lus Andeg, J. Didaci comitis"[1467].  Evans suggests that she was Margaret of Scotland, widow firstly of Conan IV "le Petit" Duke of Brittany, Earl of Richmond, and secondly of Humphrey [IV] de Bohun, daughter of Henry of Scotland
2 CONC  Earl of Huntingdon and Northumberland & his wife Ada de Warenne of Surrey ([1143/44]-1201, bur Sawtrey Abbey, Herts).  He points out that Llanthony has been founded in 1136 by Miles of Gloucester, whose eldest daughter married Hu
2 CONC mphrey de Bohun, who was the father of Margaret´s second husband[1468].  If this origin is correct, Pedro’s marriage to Margaret was presumably terminated before her death, given his third marriage.  The difficulty with Evans´s pr
2 CONC oposal is the obvious age difference between Pedro de Lara and Margaret of Scotland.  Another possibility, which also justifies the connection with Llanthony through the Bohun family and is more satisfactory from a chronological p
2 CONC oint of view, is that Pedro´s second wife was an otherwise unrecorded daughter of Margaret’s, so Margaret de Bohun, daughter of Humphrey [IV] de Bohun & his wife Margaret of Scotland.  "P…comes…cum uxore mea Marg. cometissa" donat
2 CONC ed their property "in Asbaladejo et in Cannet et in termino Conchæ" to the Order of Calatrava by charter dated 30 Dec 1187[1469].  "Comes Petrus…cum uxore mea Margarita" donated "hæreditatem de Grudis" to found a monastery, confir
2 CONC med by "sororum Mariæ et…Santia Marric", by charter dated 11 Mar 1187[1470].  "Petrus…comes…cum uxore mea comitissa domna Margerina" donated vines at Madrid to the Order of Calatrava by charter dated 17 Nov 1189[1471]. 
