1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Raoul  II of Fougères (died 1194) was a 12th - century Baron of Fougères  who remained famous for his opposition to King Henry  II of England .
2 CONT
2 CONT Origin
2 CONT Raoul  II de Fougères, born around 1128, was the 4th  child but the eldest of the two sons of Henri de Fougères and Olive de Penthièvre (died in 1175). Having become a widow in 1150, her mother had remarried Guillaume de Saint-Jea
2 CONC n, lord of Saint-Jean-le-Thomas 1 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Family ties
2 CONT Raoul  II had a family network that made him one of the great regional lords of his time. He was related to the ducal families of Brittany and Normandy. Through his father, he descended from Richard I  of Normandy 2 . Through his 
2 CONC mother, Olive, from the house of Rennes which controlled the north of Brittany and important English fiefs. Olive was in fact the daughter of Stephen  I of Penthièvre , who had the great fiefs of Penthièvre and Trégor . Olive's br
2 CONC others and sisters were among the most important figures in the Duchy of Brittany  : Geoffrey  II Boterel dominated all of Penthièvre  ; Alain le Noir , held the English honour of the county of Richmond and married the Duchess Ber
2 CONC the of Brittany  ; Henry of Penthièvre was lord of Tréguier and Agnorie was married to the powerful Olivier  II of Dinan . Raoul  II of Fougères is therefore the first cousin of Conan  IV of Brittany , son of Alain of Richmond, bu
2 CONC t also of Count Alain  I of Avaugour , who received Trégor and Goëlo . This position therefore allows Raoul to play a leading role in the early days of the reign of Conan  IV . He helps the duke fight against his father-in-law and
2 CONC  guardian, Eudes of Porhoët , who refused to give Conan his duchy back. By capturing Eudes, Raoul allowed the young duke to accede to the ducal throne in 1155. Raoul is named as a reward by his cousin "Grand Forester of the County
2 CONC  of Rennes", which allows him to administer the vast forest of Rennes , bordering that of Fougères. The union of Raoul  II completes this network of alliances: according to Frédéric Morvan, he marries his cousin, Jeanne de Dol 3 .
2 CONC  As a result of this alliance, when his brother-in-law Jean  II of Dol -Combourg died , he was appointed guardian of his only daughter, Iseult of Dol. Raoul therefore received custody of the orphan's castles: Dol and Combourg 4 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Territorial power
2 CONT Raoul  II had inherited the major castle of Fougères, which had most certainly existed since the first half of the 11th century  . He also owned, as other secondary castles, the feudal mound of Marcillé , mentioned for the first t
2 CONC ime around 1204, but also probably the castles of Antrain and Bazouges , the castle of Landéan or La Forestie where Hugues de Lusignan signed a deed in 1183, and perhaps even the mound of Chastel in Chauvigné . These fortresses th
2 CONC us crisscrossed the Breton lands of Raoul de Fougères, who also held other lands in Normandy and England. The latter were rewards received for the participation of Raoul  I of Fougères in the military expeditions of William the Co
2 CONC nqueror . In Normandy, these lands were located around Saint-Hilaire-du-Harcouët , near the country of Louvigné , namely in Savigny, Loges, Moulines, Virey, Romagny and Appenty. Others were located in the region of Mortain , in La
2 CONC  Mancellerie, Brecey and Mesnilthoué. To the north of Coutances , he owned the lands of Egouvillet and Creteville. He still had some in Vattigny and Verdun and finally near Mont Saint-Michel , namely the lands of Bouillon, Moisdre
2 CONC y and Courtils. An act of 1188 shows that the lord of Fougères, Raoul  II , paid homage for his fiefs in Normandy to the abbey of Mont-Saint-Michel . Although these domains seem quite scattered, the fact remains that the most impo
2 CONC rtant were located in the north-east of the bishopric of Rennes and to the south of the bishoprics of Avranches and Coutances and formed a territorial entity, straddling Brittany and Normandy . These multiple domains allowed the l
2 CONC ords of Fougères to control, through vassalage, in their fief of Fougères alone, a network of 45 motte-and-bailey manors , or as many knights. According to Michel Brand'honneur, the lord of Fougères then had, around the middle of 
2 CONC the 12th century  , the most important network of fortified sites in the region after that of the Count of Rennes and Duke of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT In England, the bulk of Raoul's estates were in Devon and Cornwall, but he also held the lands of Bellingtone in Somerset , Winchester in Hampshire , Kingstown in Surrey , Plymouth in Devonshire , and Thetford in Norfolk . As with
2 CONC  other great lords of England, these lands were scattered throughout the kingdom. William the Conqueror and his immediate successors had refused to create territorial entities that were too powerful and too dangerous, such as thos
2 CONC e that had been established in the kingdom of France. In addition, Raoul  II had received from his mother, Olive, the manor of Berrington, in the honour of Richmond , in the north of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Conflict with Henry  II
2 CONT The guardianship of Iseult of Dol and the custody of the castles of Dol and Combourg were the source of significant difficulties for Raoul  II . The King of England, Henry  II Plantagenet , Duke of Normandy, Count of Anjou and Mai
2 CONC ne, immediately opposed this significant increase in his power. Henry  II dominated Brittany at this time, having obtained from Duke Conan  IV of Brittany his renunciation of the throne and governed in the name of Conan IV 's only
2 CONC  young daughter  , Constance of Brittany , betrothed to his younger son Geoffrey Plantagenet . The King of England could not accept the increase in power of the Lord of Fougères, who would have united under his authority three maj
2 CONC or castles: Dol, Combourg and Fougères, plus around twenty knightly fiefs and more than sixty motte-and-bailey castles. Henry  II took the lead. As soon as Jean de Dol died in 1162, he had his agents seize the castle of Dol. Raoul
2 CONC   II did not react, apparently contenting himself with acknowledging the law of the strongest. Henry  II took advantage of this to send his constable of Normandy, Robert du Hommet , to take possession of the castle of Combourg. He
2 CONC  seems to have chosen, in order to spare the susceptibilities of the lord of Fougères, as guardian of the castle of Dol the first cousin of Raoul, Étienne, son of Count Geoffroy  II Boterel, and appointed as guardian of the castle
2 CONC  of Combourg, Raoul du Hommet, the father-in-law of Guillaume de Fougères, eldest son and heir of Raoul  II . In order to prevent Henry  II and his agents from going any further and to protect his property, Raoul  II took the Cros
2 CONC s in 1163; the property of a crusader became inviolable for the entire duration of the pilgrimage. On his return in 1166, Raoul  II attempted to negotiate with the King of England and resided at that time in one of Conan  IV 's En
2 CONC glish castles . However, this attempt was in vain . 5
2 CONT
2 CONT Raoul II 's opposition  to Henry  II Plantagenet was not without consequences since it contributed to a weakening of his position in the Vendelais . Indeed, the English king probably favored Robert  III of Vitré whose lordship was
2 CONC  then experiencing a spectacular boom. Thus, it is remarkable to note that vassals, although loyal to Raoul in the years1150, swore loyalty to the Lord of Vitré from the beginning of the years1160. Combourtillé and Javené , althou
2 CONC gh in the immediate vicinity of Fougères itself, then came under the influence of Robert the Younger, before the knights of Luitré also changed their loyalty to André  II de Vitré in the following decade 6 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolts against Henry  II of England
2 CONT Raoul  II then decided to seek support from the Plantagenet's rival, the King of France. He entered into conflict with Henry  II , organized the revolt and defended his castle of Fougères. Despite his efforts and probably abandone
2 CONC d by the King of France who did not intervene, Raoul  II was defeated by the forces of Henry  II . His castle of Fougères was destroyed and he had to submit toJuly 14, 1166to keep his other property and give up custody of his niec
2 CONC e, Iseult de Dol, who then married Harsculf de Subligny , a loyal follower of the King of England. In compensation, Raoul obtained the recovery, in 1167, of his English estate of Twyfort.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, Raoul II 's resentment  towards Henry  II remained strong because he actively participated in the great uprising of 1173 led by the king's own son, Richard the Lionheart , then Duke of Aquitaine, and his brothers. Th
2 CONC e latter had formed a coalition of great lords against his father who were unhappy with the policies of the King of England. If the first army sent by the King of England was routed in the region of Fougères, between Fougères and 
2 CONC Saint-James-de-Beuvron, the second ride of the Plantagenet was more effective. Composed of the famous roaders of Henry  II , it forced theAugust 20, 1173, Raoul  II and 82 other Breton lords and knights to lock themselves in the t
2 CONC ower of Dol. Their surrender was inevitable and the lord of Fougères was taken prisoner. His castle, which was being rebuilt, was taken and razed again. The punishment inflicted on Raoul  II , probably because of his family origin
2 CONC s, was quite light. As a price for his release, the King of England only demanded that he hand over his two eldest sons as hostages and in 1175, Raoul then recovered his fief of Fougères 7 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Faithful to Geoffrey  II of Brittany
2 CONT From 1175 to 1181, Henry  II no longer intervened directly in Brittany, leaving it to his son Geoffrey  II of Brittany , who had come of age, to take charge of the government of his wife's duchy. Geoffrey  II did not hesitate to r
2 CONC ely on great Breton lords who had been adversaries of his father, such as Rolland de Dinan . Having officially become duke upon his marriage in 1181, he attached Raoul  II of Fougères to himself by entrusting him with the high off
2 CONC ice of the duchy, that of seneschal of Brittany in 1184. The lord of Fougères then became one of the main supporters of Geoffrey  II Plantagenet. The latter's accidental death did not interrupt his activity in 1187; he was still s
2 CONC eneschal of Brittany and from 1189 he supported the claims of Geoffrey's posthumous son, Arthur of Brittany , against Richard the Lionheart , who had become King of England. Raoul  II had reconstituted his power because he had his
2 CONC  castle of Fougères rebuilt by equipping it with a powerful stone keep 8 .
2 CONT
2 CONT Recent years
2 CONT Despite the prestigious position he held at the end of his life, Raoul  II did not emerge unscathed from his participation in the great revolts against Henry  II . From 1166 onwards, he lost his influence over the Vendelais, a reg
2 CONC ion located south of Fougères, to the benefit of the Lord of Vitré, Andrew  II , who was much more conciliatory towards the King of England and who had the castle of Châtillon-en-Vendelais built . Several vassals of Raoul  II, suc
2 CONC h as the Combourtillés, paid homage to the Lords of Vitré. Thanks to Henry  II , who granted them fractions of the ducal domain in the County of Rennes, the Lords of Vitré became more powerful than the Lords of Fougères and the nu
2 CONC mber of fortified sites they controlled increased from 24 to 55, making them the most important Lords of the County of Rennes, just after the Duke of Brittany. After returning to the Holy Land to participate in the Third Crusade w
2 CONC ith Raoul d'Aubigné, younger brother of the Lord d'Aubigné 9 , Raoul  II died onMay 16, 1194, at the abbey of Savigny which he had showered with benefits and where he had taken the monastic habit.
2 CONT
2 CONT Union posterity and succession
2 CONT From the union of Raoul  II with Jeanne de Dol, a large posterity came 10  :
2 CONT
2 CONT Geoffroy ;
2 CONT Juhel miles  ;
2 CONT William of Fougères (died in 1187) married Agathe du Hommet;
2 CONT Henry dom  ;
2 CONT Marguerite (died in 1209) married Guillaume Bertran in first marriage, and Galéran  V of Meulan in second marriage (1189)  ;
2 CONT Mabille married Alain  IV of Rohan .
2 CONT His son and heir William having predeceased him in 1187 , it was his grandson Geoffroy de Fougères who succeeded him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes and references
2 CONT Michel Brand'Honneur Manors and castles in the county of Rennes ( 11th – 12th centuries  )  PUR  Rennes (2001) ( ISBN  2 86847 5612 ) table 17 p.  276.
2 CONT Through his paternal grandmother Havoise FitzRichard (died around 1138) great-granddaughter of Godefroi de Brionne , Count of Eu .
2 CONT of Gelduin  I and granddaughter of Godehilde de Fougères.
2 CONT Frédéric Morvan The Breton Knights. Between Plantagenets and Capetians from the mid- 12th to the mid-  13th century Coop Breizh editions, Spézet 2014 ( ISBN  9782843466700 ) "Genealogy of Fougères" p.  272 ??and "The Eudonides (ca
2 CONC det branch of the house of Rennes" p.  253 and Michel Brand'honneur op. cit. table 12 p.  273.
2 CONT André Chédeville and Noël-Yves Tonnerre Feudal Brittany 11th  –  13th century Ouest-France Université Rennes (1987 )  ( ISBN 9782737300141 ) p . 87-88 .    
