1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The English branch of the Dinhams (or Dynhams), long time owners of Hemyock Manor and Castle. Their main English base was at Hartland, Devon.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Geoffrey de Dinan, sieur de Dinan [Brittany], d. after 1122
2 CONT **Oliver de Dinan, sieur de Dinan, d. 1150
2 CONT ***Oliver de Dinant, d. after 1172
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Quoting the Dinan section of the Bretagne page of the Medieval Lands Project: (page moved?)
2 CONT
2 CONT OLIVIER (-1209). "Gauffredum Oliverii filium et fratres suos" donated property to the priory of Jugon by undated charter, signed by "Goffredus, Alanus, Oliverius fratres et dominus hujus castri"[587]. "Oliverius de Dinanno, Oliver
2 CONC ii filius" donated "terras in Anglia" to Saint-Malo de Dinan by charter dated 18 Jun 1182, witnessed by "Alano filio Brient…"[588]. A charter of Richard I King of England confirms the foundation of Hertland Abbey by “Gaufridi de D
2 CONC inam senioris et Oliveri fratris sui, et Gaufridi filii Oliveri” to Hertland Abbey[589]. "Hawis de Dinan et Sibill soror eius" paid a fine for "de Bokeland, Corfton in Sumerset et de Hertilande in Devon…vs Oliverum de Dinan", date
2 CONC d 1204[590]. The Chronicon Britannicum records the death in 1209 of "Oliverius de Dinano, filius Gaufredi"[591]. m ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Olivier´s wife is not known. Olivier & his wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT (a) GEOFFROY [III] de Dinan . A charter of Richard I King of England confirms the foundation of Hertland Abbey by “Gaufridi de Dinam senioris et Oliveri fratris sui, et Gaufridi filii Oliveri” to Hertland Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Dinham-14
2 CONT
2 CONT >Olivier of Dinham, having received some of his cousin Roland’s possessions which escheated during 1169 (for which see below, p. 176), between 1189 and 1199 concluded an agreement with his nephew, Olivier III de Dinan (himself the
2 CONC  son of Geoffrey II, and thus lord of Dinan nord), which separated the lands of Olivier III’s father Geoffrey II into ‘all lands… which my father held in Brittany’ to be retained by the son (and nephew) Olivier III, and ‘all lands
2 CONC … which my father held in England’ to be held by the cousin (and uncle) Olivier of Dinham. This agreement… must have significantly contributed to the establishment of the family of Olivier of Dinham as an English (and specifically
2 CONC  west-country) family. The family of the second son of the Breton Olivier II de Dinan, Olivier of Dinham, was well-entrenched among the Cornish and Devonshire gentry to 1501.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"It seems at least possible that the ‘English’ family of Olivier of Dinham kept some of their Breton rights… Certainly, at least for practical reasons, the involvement of the British Dinham family with the French priory of Marmou
2 CONC tier concerning the manors of Harpford and Nutwell (themselves used to support the priory of Saint-Malo at Dinan) extended well into the thirteenth century. [NOTE 98: In 1268 Oliver of Dinham (the great-great-grandson of the first
2 CONC  Olivier of Dinham) purchased the interest in Harpford and Nutwell from Marmoutier.]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.familysearch.org/Eng/search/AF/individual_record.asp?recid=7886309&lds=0&frompage=99
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Norman spelling for Oliver de Dinham b.1121 is Oliver DE DINAN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Different sources give Oliver III of Dinan two possible mothers or two different names for his mother if Agnorie and Gunnor are one and the same person. If they are separate people Oliver's mother is presumed to be Agnorie.
