2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Goronwy ap Tudur Hen (d.1338), Lord of Penmynydd was a member of the noble Tudor family of Penmynydd, Anglesey, North Wales and a descendant of Marchudd ap Cynan, Lord of Rhos, Lord Protector of Rhodri Mawr, King of Gwynedd and th
2 CONC e ancestor of Owen Tudor and thereby of the Tudor dynasty.[1] An elegy was written to him by Bleddyn Ddu.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Tudur ap Goronwy (d. 1311) also known as Tudur Hen (Tudur the Elder), and Angharad ferch Ithel Fychan.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as a soldier in Scotland and may well have fought at Bannockburn in 1314. In 1318 he followed Sir Gruffudd Llwyd as forester of Snowdon. http://www.thepeerage.com/p10282.htm#i102817  He married Gwerfyl ferch Madog, daugh
2 CONC ter of Madog ap Dafydd, Baron of Hendwr. They had two sons: Hywel, who gained position as a canon of Bangor Cathedral (and later as Archdeacon of Anglesey); the other son was Tudur, who was influential in North Wales and was the g
2 CONC randfather of Owen Tudor.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was burried at Bangor
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT See Peter Bartrum, http://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/5128/bleddyn%20ap%20cynfyn%2026.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y (May 4, 2017; Anne Brannen, curator)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Goronwy ap Tudur Hen From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Goronwy ap Tudur Hen Born	c. early 14th century Died	11 December 1331 Resting place	Bangor, Gwynedd Other names	Goronwy ap Tudur Goronwy Fychan Occupation	Welsh noble Titl
2 CONC e	Lord of Penmynydd Children	 Hywel ap Goronwy Tudur ap Goronwy Gruffydd ap Goronwy Goronwy ap Tudur Hen (died 1331), also known as Goronwy ap Tudur or Goronwy Fychan, was a Welsh aristocrat and Lord of Penmynydd. He was a member 
2 CONC of the Tudor family of Penmynydd, Anglesey, North Wales, and a direct ancestor of Owen Tudor and thereby the Royal House of Tudor. He was a soldier for the English crown, who fought in the First War of Scottish Independence, inclu
2 CONC ding in the English invasion which led to the Battle of Bannockburn. He remained loyal to King Edward II of England until the king's death, and was both a yeoman and forester of Snowdon. After his death in 1331, his body was inter
2 CONC ned in Llanfaes Friary, near Bangor, Gwynedd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents  [hide]  1	Ancestry and family 2	Service to the English crown 3	Legacy 3.1	Lineage 4	Notes 5	References Ancestry and family[edit] Goronwy ap Tudur Hen was one of three sons of Welsh noble Tudur Hen (English: Tudur the Eld
2 CONC er) and Anghared fil Ithel Fychan,[1][2] alongside Madog ap Tudur Hen and Hywel ap Tudur Hen.[1] Tudor Hen had first been in service to Llywelyn ap Gruffudd (also known as Llywelyn the Last), the King of Gwynedd, but retained his 
2 CONC titles under the conquest of Wales by King Edward I of England. But during the rebellion of Madog ap Llywelyn, Tudur acted as his steward. After the rebellion failed, Tudur pledged his loyalty to Edward.[3] Utilising the more adva
2 CONC ntagious property laws of England, Tudur saw to it that his lands were passed seamlessly upon his death in 1311 into the hands of Goronwy.[1][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Service to the English crown[edit] Goronwy ap Tudur Hen was Lord of Penmynydd and a soldier in the service of the English crown.[2][5] He joined King Edward II of England for his invasion of Scotland in 1314 as part of the First W
2 CONC ar of Scottish Independence, which included service at the Battle of Bannockburn in June of that year.[5] In 1316, a rebellion took place in Glamorgan under the leadership of Llywelyn Bren, at the same time Edward was campaigning 
2 CONC in Scotland.[6] Despite this there was little concern that it would spread to North Wales, with the royal officials instead concerned with the threat of invasion from Ireland.[7] Some 1,500 troops were summoned from the area to su
2 CONC pport the King, and among those leading those troops were Iorweth ap Gruffudd, Gruffudd Llwyd and his cousin Goronwy ap Tudur Hen.[8] After these troops, under the command of Gruffudd Llwyd, moved to Chester, they were dismissed o
2 CONC n 4 August 1316 to return to North Wales in order to protect the area from the Irish threat.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Goronwy and his troops were again summoned into the King's service to retake Berwick in 1319, after Scottish forces had taken it a year earlier. The majority of the forces from Wales came from the southern Marcher Lords, but Goron
2 CONC wy, Gruffudd Llwyd and Hywel ap Gruffudd ap Geruath led those from the northern part of the country.[10] Around the same time, as a yeoman of the king, Goronwy was made forester of Snowdon. He remained loyal to Edward II, but foll
2 CONC owing the king's death, he resumed service to Edward III.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Goronwy married Gwerful fil Madog, daughter of Madog ap Iorwerth, Baron Hendwr.[2] Goronwy and Gwerful had three sons, Hywel ap Goronwy, Tudur ap Goronwy and Gruffydd ap Goronwy.[3][11] Goronwy was interned after his death in the 
2 CONC west wall of the Franciscan Llanfaes Friary near Bangor, Gwynedd on 11 December 1331. His father had overseen the reconstruction efforts of the friary, and in 1311 was the first of the family to be placed there following his death
2 CONC .[1][5] Other members of the family were placed there following death,[12] until some were moved following the Dissolution of the Monasteries under the Church of England imposed by King Henry VIII of England, with Goronwy's being 
2 CONC moved to St Gredifael's Church, Penmynydd.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy[edit] Goronwy's eldest son Hywel ap Goronwy joined the priesthood, and became a canon of Bangor Cathedral. In 1357, Hywel became the Archdeacon of Anglesey.[5] Gruffydd drowned in 1344.[11] Goronwy ap Tudur Hen was one of t
2 CONC he direct ancestors of the Royal House of Tudor.[14] His son Tudur ap Goronwy became a knight in the service of Edward III,[15] and was the grandfather of Owain ap Maredudd ap Tudur (through his son Maredudd ap Tudur, grandson to 
2 CONC Goronwy ap Tudur Hen).[15] Owain Anglicised his name, becoming Owen Tudor, and was himself the grandfather of King Henry VII of England, the founder of the House of Tudor.[16]
2 CONT
