1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Please see Peter Bartrum: https://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/4857/Iestyn%204.png?sequence=1&isAllowed=y & https://cadair.aber.ac.uk/dspace/bitstream/handle/2160/4852/Iestyn%203.png?sequence=1&is
2 CONC Allowed=y (Steven Ferry, November 27, 2020.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Family of Drymbenog ap Maenyrch of Brycheiniog; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id312.html (Steven Ferry, May 21, 2023.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Ifor Bach, Lord of Senghenydd; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id316.html (Steven Ferry, August 5, 2023.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: How Bartrum 'Lost' Hywel ap Gruffudd ap Ifor Bach; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id317.html (Steven Ferry, August 9, 2023.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Darrell Wolcott: Three Conflated Brecknock Families; http://www.ancientwalesstudies.org/id324.html [Fam3: # 5] (Steven Ferry, October 6, 2024.)
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Archaeologia Cambrensis : a record of the antiquities of Wales and its Marches and the journal of the Cambrian Archaeological Association (1846)
2 CONT *https://archive.org/details/archaelolgiacam00unkngoog
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/archaelolgiacam00unkngoog#page/n15/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.7
2 CONT *   Morgan was alive in 1206, and dead in 1209, so that he probably died in 1207-8 ; in which case, if only of age at his father's presumed death, he would have been seventy-nine years old. The Welsh pedigrees marry Morgan to Wenl
2 CONC lian, daughter of Ivor Bach, and give him, besides Lleisan and Morgan-Gam, five sons and two daughters. 
2 CONT *   V. Lleisan ap Morgan, lord of Avene, was no doubt of age in 1204, when he was in command of two hundred Welshmen : ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/archaelolgiacam00unkngoog#page/n16/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.8
2 CONT * .... Lleisan seems to have died, without issue, before 1228, leaving his brother Morgan his heir. 
2 CONT *   V. 2. Morgan ap Morgan, the next lord of Avene, was no doubt the well known Morgan Gam (cam or gam meaning "the crooked," — camus, and applied either to bandy legs, as in the hero of Agincourt ; or, as in the present instance,
2 CONC  to a squint caused by the loss of an eye). When a son bore his father's name, it was the universal custom in Wales to give him a to-name by way of distinction ; and this was usually derived from some personal peculiarity, as "Vac
2 CONC han" or "Bach" (the less), "Tew" (the fat), "Coch" (the red), "Ddu" (the black) ; all of which, and many others, were in frequent use. This practice makes it almost certain that Morgan ap Morgan bore some distinguishing epithet, a
2 CONC nd highly probable that he was the Morgan Gam of the Margam charters. ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/archaelolgiacam00unkngoog#page/n17/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.9
2 CONT *   In his confirmation charter to Margam, reprinted here, Morgan ap Morgan, with his brothers Lleisan and Owen, alludes to his claims on the new castle at Newcastle, as possibly to be decided by .... 
2 CONT *   It is not known when Morgan Gam was born, but this event may safely be placed as early as 1180, since in 1204 his elder brother, Lleisan, was in military command. Morgan does not appear to have adopted his brother's political 
2 CONC views, for in 1227 he was in arms against the Earl of Gloucester ; and being taken, was sent to England, and kept there till the next year.   ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/archaelolgiacam00unkngoog#page/n18/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.10
2 CONT *   Morgan Gam affixes his seal, as lord of Avene, to Rose Coh's charter (75, B. 40), which, though without date, cites a deed of 1234. Bishop Elias tests the charter, and he died May 1240 ; but, in truth, Morgan Gam himself died,
2 CONC  say the Annals of Tewkesbury, in February 1240, and was buried at Margam. (Ann. de Theok. in loco.) Hence the mandate of Henry III to the Welsh chieftains in 1245 is addressed "filio Morgan Gam." 
2 CONT *   The patent roll of 5 Edward III (1331) adds some further information about Morgan Gam. It states that Matilda, daughter of Morgan Gam, married Gilbert de Turbervill, and had from her father in frank marriage the manor of Landi
2 CONC mor in Gower, which was afterwards claimed by another Gilbert de Turbervill, their descendant and heir, whose elder male line ended in two co-heirs, whose representatives became, through the elder, Scurlage and Mansel ; and throug
2 CONC h the younger, De la Bere and Basset. (Rot. Pat. 5 Ed. Ill, p. 8.) This connexion with Gower is further supported by an Inquisitio p. m. of 13 Ed. II (No. 62), which shews that John de Braose had given the vills of Leisaneston and
2 CONC  Kettehill (in Gower) to Morgan Gam. 
2 CONT *   Thomas de Avene's borough charter of 1349 mentions '''Morgan Vychan and Sir Lleisan as sons of Morgan Gam'''. 
2 CONT *   VI. Sir Lleisan ap Morgan Gam, lord of Avene. The repetition of this name in (probably) three succeeding generations, in each as Lleisan ap Morgan, and in the first two dying childless, has created considerable confusion in th
2 CONC e genealogy. It was exceedingly popular in the family ; and its occurrence in the neighbourhood of Neath, renders very probable the claim of some of the older families in that valley to descend from the house of Avene. 
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/archaelolgiacam00unkngoog#page/n19/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.11
2 CONT *'''   VI. 2. MORGAN VACHAN ap Morgan Gam, lord of Avene, appears to have been next brother to, and heir of, Sir Lleisan. He is mentioned as a son of Morgan Gam in the borough charter of 1249 ; and in an extent cited by Mr. Floyd 
2 CONC as taken on the death of Richard de Clare, about 1263-4, is said to hold half a commote in Walshery by the tenure of a heriot of his horse and arms at his decease. He was father, as it would seem, of another Lleisan. The Welsh ped
2 CONC igrees marry him to the heiress of Kilvey, and give him also a son called Rhys ; whence claimed descent the Lleisans of Baglan, and through them the Pryces of Briton Ferry and other families now mostly extinct. According to the Ch
2 CONC ronicle in the Archaeol Camb. (p. 282), Morgan the son of Morgan Gam, married the daughter of Walter de Sully in 1276. He seems to have died in 1288, according to the Chronicle printed in the Archceol. Camb. for 1862 (p.281), "ann
2 CONC o 1288 obiit Margan Dominus de Avene viij idus Augusti" (6th August). ....
2 CONT *   VII. LLEISAN ap '''Morgan Vachan, or D'Avene, lord of Avene, described as the son of Morgan Vachan in a borough charter. According to the Chronicle, Morgan, son of Morgan Gam''', married, in 1276, a daughter of Walter de Sully
2 CONC . (Arch. Camb., 1862, p. 282.) '''This date is scarcely consistent with the known period of Morgan Gam''', and perhaps relates to this Lleisan. ....
2 CONT *https://archive.org/stream/archaelolgiacam00unkngoog#page/n20/mode/1up
2 CONT *Pg.12
2 CONT *   This Lleisan is regarded by Mr. Jones as the grantor of the borough charter, with seal of arms, given afterwards. Lleisan was father of John and Thomas de Avene, both mentioned in Penrice agreement of 1340. He died before 1328
2 CONC , when his son was lord. 
2 CONT *   VIII. Sir John de Avene, knight, lord of Avene, Sully, and Kilvey, so styled as witness to a local charter (75, C. 25) of 28 June, 1328, and at the head of a charter by himself on the Wednesday in Epiphany, 6 Edw. Ill, January
2 CONC  1333. He was therefore in possession as early as 1328. Sully, no doubt, came by Maud, daughter of Walter de Sully, either Sir John's mother or his grandmother. This manor must have come to the Avenes between 1315 and 1328, since 
2 CONC at the former date it was held by William de Briwes, probably by marriage with Elizabeth, daughter of Raymond de Sully, How the acquisition was made, whether by inheritance on Elizabeth dying childless, or by purchase from her and
2 CONC  De Briwes, is not known.
2 CONT *Pg.19
2 CONT *CHART
2 CONT *_____________________________________
