0 @N6000000002187592011@ NOTE Aedan of Dalriada
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT For the 7th century saint, see Aidan of Lindisfarne. 
1 CONT
1 CONT .Áedán (OIr: Áedán mac Gabráin) was king of Dál Riata from c. 574 to his death, c. 609, when he was said to be over 70.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT The sources for Áedán's life include Bede's Historia ecclesiastica gentis Anglorum; Irish annals, principally the Annals of Ulster and the Annals of Tigernach; and Adomnán of Iona's Life of Saint Columba. Áedán also appears in the
1 CONC  poems 'Gein Branduib maic Echach ocus Aedáin maic Gabráin and Compert Mongán.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Background 
1 CONT 2 Reign 
1 CONT 3 Notes 
1 CONT 4 References 
1 CONT 5 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Background
1 CONT The historical Áedán was a son of Gabrán mac Domangairt (died c. 555–560), and had at least one brother, Eógan, who died c. 593.[2] The Senchus fer n-Alban lists five sons of Gabrán: Cuildach, Domnall and Domangart, as well as Áed
1 CONC án and Eógan.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, in the Rawlinson B 502 manuscript, dated to c. 1130, is the poem Gein Branduib maic Echach ocus Aedáin maic Gabráin (The Birth of Brandub son of Eochu and of Aedán son of Gabrán). This tells how Áedán was a son of Echu ma
1 CONC c Muiredaig mac Óengusa of the Uí Cheinnselaig of Laigin, and thus the twin brother of Brandub mac Echach (died c. 605–608). Áedán was exchanged at birth for one of the twin daughters of Gabrán, born the very same night, so that e
1 CONC ach family might have a son. Whether the tale is entirely fabricated, or whether it echoes a foster-relationship between Áedán and Brandub, can only be conjectured.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Footprint used in king-making ceremonies, DunaddÁedán became king after the death of his cousin, Conall mac Comgaill c. 572–574.[4] Adomnán's Life of Saint Columba recounts that the Saint had a vision of an angel on three successi
1 CONC ve nights: the angel commanded Columba to ordain Áedán as king rather than Eógan his brother.[5] If accurate, this is one of the first ordinations known, although Gildas refers to the anointing of kings in De excidio.[6]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reign
1 CONT A convention of many Irish kings was convoked at Druim Cett (probably near Limavady). Among the matters settled there was an agreement fostered by Columba between Áedán and Áed mac Ainmuirech of the Cenél Conaill, a branch of the 
1 CONC northern Uí Néill to which Columba himself belonged, possibly in 575. This settled the relationship of Dál Riata and the Uí Néill high kings.[7] The apparent purpose of the alliance between the Cenél Conaill and the Dál Riata was 
1 CONC to contain the ambitious king of Ulaid, Báetán mac Cairill.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the first year of Áedán's rule, the Irish annals record a battle at Teloch or Delgenn (the location is unknown) in Kintyre. The annalist records the death of Conall's son Dúnchad "and many others of the followers of Gabrán's so
1 CONC ns". Their opponent is not recorded, but if not a civil war fought over the succession, then it may be that this represented an effort by Báetán mac Cairill to extend his control over Dál Riata.[8] The reporting of the battle of T
1 CONC eloch suggests a Dál Riatan defeat, as does the expedition by the Ulaid to the Isle of Man the following year. The Ulaid did not remain long in control of Man, as they retired the following year. [9]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 580, according to the Annals of Ulster, Áedán undertook an expedition to Orkney. This may have been followed by a second the following year. Orkney had been under the authority of the Pictish king Bridei mac Maelchon (died c. 5
1 CONC 81–584), but it may not have been under the control of his successor Gartnait.[10] At the same time, Áedán's ally Áed mac Ainmuirech won the battle of Druim Meic Erce over the Cenél nEógan.[11] As well as Orkney, it has been sugge
1 CONC sted that Áedán also campagned in the Isle of Man in the early 580s, although the evidence for this is slim. It is more likely that Áedán campaigned against the Britons of Gododdin at this time.[12] The annals report that Áedán wo
1 CONC n the battle of Lethreid, but against whom, and where this was, are unknown.[13]
1 CONT
1 CONT The Gein Brandub says that Áedán tried to impose himself as High King of Ireland, supposedly as as a great-grandson of legendary king Conaire Már mac Eterscélae, but if Conaire Már existed it must have been far distant from Áedán'
1 CONC s time and he cannot have been a great-grandson of Conaire. The Compert Mongán begins with the statement
1 CONT
1 CONT Fiachnae Lurgan [mac Báetáin], the father of Mongán, was sole king of the province [of Ulster]. He had a friend in Scotland, to wit, Áedán, the son of Gabrán.[14]
1 CONT
1 CONT There is no evidence of an alliance between Fiachnae and Áedán other than the tale of Mongán, but the claim that Fiachnae fought with Áedán against Æthelfrith and the Bernicians is more credible than the tale of the High Kingship 
1 CONC in the Gein Brandub.
1 CONT
1 CONT The matter of how many sons Áedán had, and what they were called, is unclear. The Senchus says that he had seven sons, naming them as Eochaid Buide and Eochaid Find, Tuathal, Bran, Baithéne, Conaing and Gartnait. This list is doub
1 CONC tful as it contradicts sources closer to the time and contains improbable names.[15]Adomnán, in the Life of Saint Columba, states that Áedán had three grown sons: Artuir, Eochaid Find and Domangart, and asked Columba which of thes
1 CONC e would succeed him. He reports that Columba prophesied that all three would be killed before Áedán and that Eochaid Buide, then only a child, would succeed him.[16]
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of Áedán's sons Bran and Domangart is recorded for 596.[17] The Annals of Tigernach record additional details, including that there was a battle in Circinn - in Pictland - so it may be assumed that the battle was fought 
1 CONC against the Picts. Tigernach also recorded, or some later copyist added, the death of four of Áedán's sons in this year: Bran, Domangart, Eochaid Fínd and Artuir.[18]
1 CONT
1 CONT Earlier victories turned to defeat when Áedán led a large force ("an immense and mighty army", as Bede calls it) against Æthelfrith of Bernicia, who had been successfully expanding his territory at the expense of the British. Áedá
1 CONC n was decisively defeated at the battle of Degsastan, perhaps in Liddesdale c. 600–603 (Bede and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle say 603). According to Bede, most of Áedán's army was slain by Æthelfrith's smaller force, and Áedán himsel
1 CONC f fled; after this defeat, Bede reports, the Dalriadan kings would not make war against the English again, right up to Bede's own time, 130 years later.[19] The Bernician exile Hering son of Hussa and the Cenél nEógan prince Máel 
1 CONC Umai mac Báetáin fought on Áedán's side at the battle, and Máel Umai is said to have slain "Eanfrith brother of Æthelfrith".[20]
1 CONT
1 CONT It should be said that the war between Áedán and Æthelfrith, as with the wars between Áedán and Picts, and with his fellow Gaels, were not life and death struggles between one "race" and another. The presence of Hering with Áedán'
1 CONC s army makes that plain. War was the normal state of affairs, and if not Æthelfrith, then Áedán would have fought another of his neighbours. Indeed, on Æthelfrith's death his sons would find refuge at the court of Áedán's son Eoch
1 CONC aid Buide, and relations between the Gaels and Bernicia, and later Northumbria, were friendly as Bede himself notes.[21]
1 CONT
1 CONT After the defeat of Degsastan the annalists report nothing of Áedán until his death six years after the battle, which is to say 609 if Bede's dating is accepted.[22]
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT ? Áedán's age is given as 74 and his reign as 38 years by the Annals of Tigernach; age 88 and reign 37 years by the Chronicon Scotorum; and a reign 24 years in the Duan Albanach. 
1 CONT ? Death of Eógan, Annals of Ulster, s.a. 593. Cf. Adomnán, III, 5. 
1 CONT ? See Gein Branduib (at CELT) under External links 
1 CONT ? Death of Conall: Annals of Ulster, s.a. 574; Annals of Tigernach, s.a. 573; etc. 
1 CONT ? Adomnán, Life, III, 5. 
1 CONT ? Broun, "Aedán mac Gabráin"; Snyder, Age of Tyrants, p. 86; Gildas, De excidio, chapter 21; Adomnán, Life, note 358; Byrne, Irish Kings and High-Kings, p. 159. 
1 CONT ? Columba was a grandson of Fergus Cendfota, and Áed a great-grandson, see Adomnán, Life, Table 1. The actual date of the meeting at Druim Cett is debated. Sharpe, in the editor's notes to Adomnán's Life, note 204, favours a much 
1 CONC later date, circa 590. Sharpe is followed by Meckler (The Annals of Ulster and the date of the meeting at Druim Cett, Peritia, vol. 11, 1997) but this is challenged by Jaski (Druim Cett revisited (Peritia, vol. 12, 1998). 
1 CONT ? Annals of Ulster, s.a. 576; Annals of Tigernach, s.a. 574. Both entries are for the year following Conall's death. 
1 CONT ? Ó Cróinín, Early Medieval Ireland, pp. 50–51; Annals of Ulster, s.a. 577, 578; Annals of Tigernach, s.a. 575, 576. 
1 CONT ? Annals of Ulster, s.a. 580, 581; Adomnán, Life, II, 42, and note 324. 
1 CONT ? Annals of Ulster, s.a. 580. 
1 CONT ? Annals of Ulster, s.a. 582: "The battle of Manu, in which Aedán son of Gabrán son of Domangart was victor", also s.a. 583. Cf. Annales Cambriae, s.a. 584: "Battle against the Isle of Man ..." The location of Manu is discussed by
1 CONC  Sharpe in Adomnán, Life, note 81, where Manaw, the British kingdom or tribe in the area of Stirling and Clackmannan is proposed. 
1 CONT ? Annals of Ulster, s.a. 590. "Lethreid" is not listed in the Onomasticon Goedelicum, even as an unknown location. 
1 CONT ? Translation by Mary Jones, see External links. 
1 CONT ? See the discussion of the name Conaing in Byrne, Irish Kings and High-Kings, pp. 111–112. Baithéne would appear most likely to be post-600 and the death of Abbot Baithéne of Iona. 
1 CONT ? Adomnán, Life, I, 9. 
1 CONT ? Annals of Ulster, s.a. 596 
1 CONT ? Annals of Tigernach, s.a. 594. 
1 CONT ? Bede, H. E., I, 34. 
1 CONT ? Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, Ms. E., s.a. 603; Annals of Tigernach, s.a. 59
1 CONT ? Bede, HE, IV, 26. 
1 CONT ? Annals of Ulster, s.a. 606 (Degsastan s.a. 600); Annals of Tigernach, s.a. 604 (Degsastan s.a. 598); Annales Cambriae, s.a. 607. 
