0 @N6000000002187905485@ NOTE Cadwaladr
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cadwaladr ap Cadwallon (c. 633–682, reigned from c. 655) (Latin: Catuvelladurus; English: Cadwallader), also known as Cadwaladr Fendigaid ('the Blessed') was a king of Gwynedd. Welsh chroniclers consider him to be one the greatest
1 CONC  British kings to have ever lived, Geoffrey of Monmouth included him in his Historia Regum Britanniae (vii,3) as the last in the line of legendary Kings of the Britons. His standard, the red dragon, was later adopted by Henry VII 
1 CONC of England, founder of the Tudor dynasty , who claimed descent from Cadwaladr.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Cadwallon ap Cadfan, Cadwaladr was only a child when his father was killed by the army of Oswald of Bernicia at the Battle of Heavenfield, and Cadafael Cadomedd took over in Gwynedd. Raised abroad, either in Ireland, Br
1 CONC ittany or in a neighboring Welsh kingdom, Cadwaladr eventually reclaimed his family's throne from Cadafael, and went on to challenge the West Saxons in Somerset in 658, unsuccessfully. Cadwaladr was arguably the last Welsh ruler t
1 CONC o mount a serious counteroffensive against the Anglo-Saxon forces that had overrun Southern Britain since the fall of the Western Roman Empire. It may be for this reason that Geoffrey of Monmouth chose to end his narrative of Brit
1 CONC ish kings with Cadwaladr.
1 CONT
1 CONT After these initial military escapades, Cadwaladr seemingly settled down and focused on the domestic situation, establishing several religious foundations in Gwynedd and gaining a reputation as a devout, pious leader; so much so t
1 CONC hat, after his death, the Welsh church came to regard him as a saint.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Annales Cambriae, he died of plague in 682. Other sources suggest he may have been the victim of an earlier plague, in 663/664, but such an early death would seem to extend the reign of his successor, Idwal to an 
1 CONC improbable length.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Cadfael Cadomedd Kings of Gwynedd Succeeded by:
1 CONT Idwal Iwrch
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Cadwalladon Mythical British Kings Succeeded by:
1 CONT --
0 @N6000000003319708319@ NOTE
1 CONT AFN: HPGD-02
0 @N6000000003949916861@ NOTE Was still a boy when his father was killed in battle.  Because the natives of Gwynedd did not like the usurper, Cadfael, they hailed him as the promised savior.  Unfortunately he did not live up to their hopes.  In 658 he led an a
1 CONC rmy against the West Saxons at Peonne in Somerset and was roundly defeated.  Thereafter he spent his years establishing pease and prosperity in Gwynedd.  He established at least 3 religious foundations and became highly regarded a
1 CONC s a good and pious ruler and is remembered as a saint in the Welsh church.  All histories agree that he died of the plague but whether it was the one of 663/4 or 682 is unclear but the latter is most likely.
0 @N6000000003051146545@ NOTE ["After the Flood", Bill Cooper]
1 CONT But he [Cadwallo] was succeeded by Cadwallader (Kydwaladr vendigaid) whose reign was divided into two periods from ca AD 633-643 and 654-664. Twelve years after he inherited the crown, Cadwallader was struck down with an unspecifi
1 CONC ed illness, and during his incapacity the Britons fell to warring amongst themselves. Due to the civil war and due no doubt to the consequent neglect and destruction of the crops, the country was ravaged by a long-remembered famin
1 CONC e that was followed by the plague. For safety's sake, Cadwallader sought refuge on the continent, entering Brittany where he was received with much kindness by king Alan II. Eleven years later, Alan persuaded Cadwallader to return
1 CONC  to Britain and resume his reign. His stay in Brittany would thus have fallen between the years AD 643-654.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Wikipedia, "Cadwaladr", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT Cadwaladr ap Cadwallon (c. 633-682, reigned from about 655) (Latin: Catuvelladurus; English: Cadwallader), also known as Cadwaladr Fendigaid ('the Blessed') was a king of Gwynedd. According to the Historia Brittonum he "reigned am
1 CONC ong the Britons" as high king. Later Welsh legend and prophecy (including the 10th Century prophetic poem Armes Prydain) regarded him as the promised deliverer, who would one day return to lead the Brythons to victory against the 
1 CONC Saxons (a role which Arthur and especially Owain would later assume, although Cadwaladr never entirely lost his place in the Welsh prophetic tradition of the later Middle Ages). Geoffrey of Monmouth included him in his Historia Re
1 CONC gum Britanniae (xii,14) as the last in the line of legendary Kings of the Britons. His supposed standard, the red dragon, was later adopted by Henry VII of England, founder of the Tudor dynasty , who claimed descent from Cadwaladr
1 CONC , and today forms the basis for y Ddraig Goch.
1 CONT
1 CONT The son of Cadwallon ap Cadfan, Cadwaladr was only a child when his father was killed by the army of Oswald of Bernicia at the Battle of Heavenfield, and Cadafael Cadomedd took over in Gwynedd. Raised abroad, either in Ireland, Br
1 CONC ittany or in a neighboring Welsh kingdom, Cadwaladr eventually reclaimed his family's throne from Cadafael. He went on to challenge the West Saxons in Somerset in 658, unsuccessfully[citation needed]. Cadwaladr was arguably the la
1 CONC st Welsh ruler to mount a serious counteroffensive against the Anglo-Saxon forces that had overrun much of Southern Britain since the fall of the Western Roman Empire. It may be for this reason that Geoffrey of Monmouth chose to e
1 CONC nd his narrative of British kings with Cadwaladr.
1 CONT
1 CONT After these initial military escapades, Cadwaladr seemingly settled down and focused on the domestic situation, establishing several religious foundations in Gwynedd and gaining a reputation as a devout, pious leader; so much so t
1 CONC hat, after his death, the Welsh church came to regard him as a saint (he was not canonized).
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Annales Cambriae, he died of plague in 682. The earlier Historia Brittonum suggest he was the victim of an earlier plague, in 663/664, but such an early death would seem to extend the reign of his successor, Idwal
1 CONC  Iwrch, to an improbable length.
