0 @N6000000002187590879@ NOTE Eochaid mac Áeda Find
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eochaid mac Áeda Find is a spurious King of Dál Riata found in some High Medieval king-lists and in older history books.
1 CONT
1 CONT Supposedly a son of Áed Find (died 778) and successor to Áed's brother Fergus mac Echdach, Eochaid is now thought to represent a misplacing of the reign of Eochaid mac Echdach. The reigns of Selbach mac Ferchair, Dúngal mac Selbai
1 CONC g and, perhaps, Alpín mac Echdach, are similarly misplaced in the sources which contain this Eochaid.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Broun, Dauvit, The Irish Identity of the Kingdom of the Scots in the Twelfth and Thirteenth Centuries. Boydell, Woodbridge, 1999. ISBN 0-85115-375-5 
1 CONT Broun, Dauvit, "Pictish Kings 761–839: Integration with Dál Riata or Separate Development" in Sally M. Foster (ed.), The St Andrews Sarcophagus: A Pictish masterpiece and its international connections. Four Courts, Dublin, 1998. I
1 CONC SBN 0-85182-414-6 
1 CONT
1 CONT Also King of Daldria. Source: RoyaList, Brian Tompsett 
0 @N6000000002315775376@ NOTE King of Scots 814-827 Possibly he m. the only dau. of Alpin, son of Wroid, King of the Picts, 775- 779, which would account for the name of Alpin among the names of the kings of the Scots. "814. Thirteen to Eochy-annuine (or the P
1 CONC oisonous)." All these Eochys, a favorite Irish name, were so-called, after Eochy-Munrevar, the father of Erc (above). O'Flaherty also gives the name of Alpin in Irish as "Alpin mac Eocoid." (The Cro. regum Scot. and the Nomina reg
1 CONC um. make "Tre deg" mean thirty years instead of thirteen as was meant; both however call him the son of Ed-fin (Adalbi, Hethfin), as do other sources; Cronica de Mailros gives his death as 834. 
0 @N6000000002315775480@ NOTE King of Scots 697-700 Said to have given orders that the actions of the kings should be preserved in the records of the monasteries. " Eochy-hook-(or wry)-nose, the son of Domangart, the son of Donald Breck, reigned 3 years." 
