0 @N6000000003051115915@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Elisedd ap Gwylog", retrieved 22 Dec 07]
1 CONT Elisedd ap Gwylog (died c. 755), also known as Elise was king of Powys in eastern Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Little has been preserved in the historical records about Elisedd, who was a descendant of Brochwel Ysgithrog. He appears to have reclaimed the territory of Powys after it had been overrun by the English. His great-grandson, Cynge
1 CONC n ap Cadell erected a column in his memory which stands not far from the later abbey of Valle Crucis. This is known as the Pillar of Eliseg, but the form Eliseg which appears on the column is thought to be a mistake by the carver 
1 CONC of the inscription.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Latin inscription on the pillar is now very hard to read, but was apparently clearer in the time of Edward Lhuyd who transcribed it. The translation of the part of the inscription referring to Elisedd is as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT + Concenn son of Catell, Catell son of Brochmail, Brochmail son of Eliseg, Eliseg son of Guoillauc.
1 CONT + And that Concenn, great-grandson of Eliseg, erected this stone for his great-grandfather Eliseg.
1 CONT + The same Eliseg, who joined together the inheritance of Powys . . . out of the power of the Angles with his sword and with fire.
1 CONT + Whosoever repeats the writing, let him give a blessing on the soul of Eliseg.
1 CONT Some old poems refer to Elisedd and assert he had a "special crown, a chain of twisted gold links, and armlets and anklets of gold which were the badges of sovereignty of Powys". [1] These artifacts have disappeared from history b
1 CONC ut perhaps resurfaced briefly during the coronation of Owain Glyndwr in 1400.
1 CONT
1 CONT Elisedd was succeeded by his son Brochfael.
