0 @N6000000002187763708@ NOTE Owain ap Hywel
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain ap Hywel (died 987) was king of Deheubarth in south Wales and probably also controlled Powys.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain was the son of Howell the Good, originally king of Deheubarth but by the end of his life king of most of Wales. On Howell's death in 950 Deheubarth was shared between Owain and his two brothers, Rhodri and Edwin. The sons of
1 CONC  Hywel were not able to keep hold of Gwynedd, which was reclaimed for the traditional dynasty by Iago ab Idwal and Ieuaf ab Idwal, the sons of Idwal Foel.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 952 Iago and Ieuaf invaded the south, penetrating as far as Dyfed. The sons of Hywel retaliated by invading the north in 954, reaching as far north as the Conwy valley before being defeated in a battle at Llanrwst and being obl
1 CONC iged to retreat to Ceredigion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhodri died in 953 and Edwin in 954, leaving Owain to rule Deheubarth alone. Owain did not again try to reclaim Gwynedd, but instead he and his son Einon turned eastwards to attack the kingdom of Morgannwg (modern Glamorgan) in 96
1 CONC 0, 970 and 977. On a further raid on the east in 984, Einon was killed by the noblemen of Gwent.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Owain grew older, his son Maredudd took over the leadership in war, and in 986 did what his father had failed to do by seizing the throne of Gwynedd, ousting Ieuaf's son Cadwallon ab Ieuaf. The following year Owain died and Mar
1 CONC edudd became king of Deheubarth as well as Gwynedd.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT John Edward Lloyd (1911) A history of Wales from the earliest times to the Edwardian conquest (Longmans, Green & Co.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Howell the Good King of Deheubarth
1 CONT 950–987 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Maredudd ab Owain 
