0 @N6000000003949663608@ NOTE One of the kings installed in Wales by Earl Harold of Wessex (the future Harold II) and Edward the Confessor after the defeat of Gruffudd ap Llywelyn in 1063. He is normally associated with the kindgom of Powys but may also have r
1 CONC uled in Gwynedd. Despite his early client status, he was well regarded by Welsh chroniclers, and followed the traditional policy of his predecesors. He asssited Earl Edwin of Mercia in a fruitless rebellion against William I the C
1 CONC onqueror. Source: Who's Who in British History. Collins & Brown. London. 2000.
1 CONT Bleddyn was the son of Cynfyn ap Gwerstan of the princely house of Mathrafal, and Angharad, daughter of Maredudd ab Owain of the Dinefwr dynasty of Deheubarth. Bleddyn was married to Hear of Powys. When Gruffydd ap Llywelyn was ki
1 CONC lled by his own men after being defeated by Harold Godwinson in 1063, his realm was divided among several princes. Bleddyn and his brother Rhiwallon submitted to Harold and from him received Gwynedd and Powys. K.L. Maund is of the
1 CONC  opinion that Bleddyn ruled Gwynedd and Rhiwallon Powys. In 1067 Bleddyn and Rhiwallon joined with the Mercian Eadric the Wild in an attack on the Normans at Hereford, then in 1068 allied with Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar 
1 CONC of Northumbria in another attack on the Normans.
1 CONT Bleddyn was challenged by the two sons of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, but defeated them at the battle of Mechain in 1070, one being killed and the other dying of exposure after the battle. Bleddyn's brother Rhiwallon was also killed in 
1 CONC this battle, and he ruled Gwynedd and Powys alone until his death. He was killed in 1075 by Rhys ab Owain of Deheubarth and the nobility of Ystrad Tywi in South Wales, a killing which caused much shock throughout Wales. When Rhys 
1 CONC ab Owain was defeated in battle and forced to become a fugitive by Bleddyn's cousin and successor as king of Gwynedd, Trahaearn ap Caradog in 1078 and killed by Caradog ap Gruffydd of Gwent shortly afterwards, this was hailed as "
1 CONC vengeance for the blood of Bleddyn ap Cynfyn".
1 CONT He was responsible for a revision of Welsh law in the version used in Gwynedd. After his death Gwynedd was seized by Trahaearn ap Caradog and later recovered for the line of Rhodri the Great by Gruffydd ap Cynan, but in Powys Bled
1 CONC dyn was the founder of a dynasty which lasted until the end of the 13th century.
0 @N6000000004533071351@ NOTE All of the following information came from Jane Williams Flank, WorldConnect db=jwflank, rootsweb.com:
1 CONT
1 CONT Bleddyn Cynfyn ruled Powys 1063-1075
1 CONT
1 CONT _____________________
1 CONT From http://homepages.tesco.net/~plk33/plk33/History/KingListBritain:
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1063, Blethyn (Bleddyn) rules a mostly united Wales, and Powys isdetached from Gwynedd & Deheubarth for, or by, his son.
1 CONT
1 CONT _____________________
1 CONT
1 CONT Bleddyn and his brother Rhiwallon were half-brothers of Gruffydd apLlywelyn. Their mother, Angharad, married Cynfyn ap Gwerstan o thedeath of Llywelyn ap Seisyll. Cynfyn ap Bwerstan may have been a Powysnobleman; there is also som
1 CONC e suggestion that he may have been halfSaxon. Bleddyn remained a strong supporter of the Mercians in theirbattles against the Normans.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bleddyn and Rhiwallon also had to contend with internal revolution.The sons of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, Maredudd and Idwal, attempted toreestablish their claim to the throne. However, they were also killedin the Battle of Mechain in 
1 CONC 1070; Bleddyn now ruled without internalchallenge.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is reported to having been a generous king, giving to church andpopulous. He was the first king in over 100 years to revise the lawsdeveloped under Hywel Dda.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unfortunately, in 1073, the Normans began their advance through northWales, and defeated Bleddyn in a surprise attack. Weakened, he becamevurnerable to his southern neighbor, Rhys ab Owain of Deheubarth. In1075, Rhys betrayed Bled
1 CONC dyn, leading to his murder. However, Bleddyn'sdescendents became the main ruling family of Powys. [The Welsh Kings]
1 CONT
1 CONT _______________________
1 CONT
1 CONT During the later years of the reign of his half-brother, Gruffyd, theEnglish had brought Wales into at least nominal subjection. Gruffydwas killed by the English 5 Aug 1063. Bleddyn and his brotherRhiwallon, having submitted to Ki
1 CONC ng Edward, were made rules of Gwyneddand Powys. The Normans who came with William the Conqueror soonencroached upon the Welsh who resisted them and Rhiwallon was slain inbattle in 1070. Bleddyn was then the sole Ruler. [The Weaver
1 CONC Genealogy]
1 CONT
1 CONT ___________________________
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1075, Bleddyn was killed by Rhys, the brother of Maredudd ab Owain,and Rhys in turn was likeed in 1078 by Caradog ap Gruffudd. Bleddyn'skingdom passed to his cousin, Trahaearn ap Caradog, but Trahaearn waskilled, along with Car
1 CONC adog ap Gruffudd, in the battle of Mynydd Carnin 1081. [A History of Wales; John Davies]
0 @N6000000001744818901@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT BLEDDYN (son of No. 21). During the later years of the reign of his half-brother, Gruffyd, the
1 CONT           English had brought Wales into at least nominal subjection. Gruffyd was killed by the English August
1 CONT           5, 1063. Bleddyn and his brother Rhiwallon, having submitted to King Edward, were made rulers of
1 CONT           Gwynedd and Powys. The Normans who came with William the Conqueror soon encroached upon
1 CONT           the Welsh who resisted them and Rhiwallon was slain in battle in 1070. Bleddyn was then the sole
1 CONT           ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT           Angharad's first husband having died in 1023, let us assume that she married Cynfyn in 1025 and that
1 CONT           Bleddyn was born in 1027. These dates may not be correct, but they are not far astray. He made
1 CONT           many beneficial changes in the laws of his kingdom. Prof. Lloyd, quoting from the Chronicles of the
1 CONT           Princes, says of Bleddyn, "He was the mildest and most clement of kings and did injury to none, save
1 CONT           when insulted, nor loved to avenge the insult when it came. To his kinsmen he was gentle; widows and
1 CONT           orphans and the weak he defended; he was the support of the wise, the glory and corner-stone of the
1 CONT           church, the delight of all lands, openhanded to all, terrible in war, but in peace beloved." The sons of
1 CONT           his half-brother tried to regain the kingdom and he had many conflicts with the Mercians, but he lost
1 CONT           no territory. He died in 1075 and "left five sons, Madog and Phiryd who were slain in 1088, Cadwgan
1 CONT           and Iowerth who both survived until 1111 and Maredudd, who died in 1132 and transmitted the
1 CONT           rights of his family to many future generations." (Stone)BLEDDYN (son of No. 21). During the later years of the reign of his half-brother, Gruffyd, the
1 CONT           English had brought Wales into at least nominal subjection. Gruffyd was killed by the English August
1 CONT           5, 1063. Bleddyn and his brother Rhiwallon, having submitted to King Edward, were made rulers of
1 CONT           Gwynedd and Powys. The Normans who came with William the Conqueror soon encroached upon
1 CONT           the Welsh who resisted them and Rhiwallon was slain in battle in 1070. Bleddyn was then the sole
1 CONT           ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT           Angharad's first husband having died in 1023, let us assume that she married Cynfyn in 1025 and that
1 CONT           Bleddyn was born in 1027. These dates may not be correct, but they are not far astray. He made
1 CONT           many beneficial changes in the laws of his kingdom. Prof. Lloyd, quoting from the Chronicles of the
1 CONT           Princes, says of Bleddyn, "He was the mildest and most clement of kings and did injury to none, save
1 CONT           when insulted, nor loved to avenge the insult when it came. To his kinsmen he was gentle; widows and
1 CONT           orphans and the weak he defended; he was the support of the wise, the glory and corner-stone of the
1 CONT           church, the delight of all lands, openhanded to all, terrible in war, but in peace beloved." The sons of
1 CONT           his half-brother tried to regain the kingdom and he had many conflicts with the Mercians, but he lost
1 CONT           no territory. He died in 1075 and "left five sons, Madog and Phiryd who were slain in 1088, Cadwgan
1 CONT           and Iowerth who both survived until 1111 and Maredudd, who died in 1132 and transmitted the
1 CONT           rights of his family to many future generations." (Stone)
