0 @N6000000003949663398@ NOTE Iago ab Idwal was king of Gwynedd from 1023 to 1039, but in the latter year he was killed by one of his own men and the throne was seized by Gruffydd ap Llywelyn. Cynan was forced to flee to Ireland and took refuge at the Danish s
1 CONC ettlement of Dublin. He married Ragnaillt daughter of Olaf of Dublin, son of King Sigtrygg Silkbeard and a member of the Hiberno-Norse dynasty. Ragnaillt, who appears in the list of the fair women of Ireland in the Book of Leinste
1 CONC r, was also a descendant of Brian Boru.
1 CONT Cynan may have died fairly soon after the birth of their son Gruffydd ap Cynan, for the near-contemporary biography of Gruffydd details Cynan's ancestry but does not refer to him in its account of Gruffydd's youth; describing Gruf
1 CONC fydd's mother telling him who his father was and what patrimony he could claim. Gruffydd ap Llywelyn was killed in 1063, by his own men according to Brut y Tywysogion. The Ulster Chronicle however states that it was Cynan ap Iago 
1 CONC who killed him.
1 CONT Cynan's claim to the throne of Gwynedd was passed on to his son. When Gruffydd first appeared on the scene in Wales the Welsh annals several times refer to him as "grandson of Iago" rather than the more usual "son of Cynan", indic
1 CONC ating that his father was little known in Wales
0 @N6000000003949818332@ NOTE William lived in the time of King Stephen, 1135-1154, and married Beatrix, daughter of Hugh de Bohun, alias Kyveliock, 5th Earl of Chester, and co-heir to her brother Randle, Earl of Chester. Arms: Gules three pheons argent, impal
1 CONC ing three garbs or. (Last are arms of Randle. Earl of Chester.)
1 CONT Sources for Malpas, Egerton and related families:
1 CONT Ormerod's History of Cheshire, Vol. 1, pp.47, 526; Vol. 2, pp.15, 44, 328/9, 331-333, 347, 350; Vol. 3, pp.162, 169, 188/9, 201, 205.
1 CONT Harleian, Vol. 18, pp. 4, 159; Vol. 29, pp. 424, 95/6.
1 CONT Croston's County Families of Lancashire and Cheshire, pp. 268, 271/2, 118-123, 286.
1 CONT Miscellaneous Genealogica and Heraldrica, Vol. 1, p. 293.
1 CONT Burke's Dormant and Extinct Peerage, pp. 74, 186.
0 @N6000000003299316075@ NOTE One of the more intriguing facts revealed by the Domesday Book is that William Fitz Osbern, the earl of Hereford between the years 1067 and 1071, made a series of grants to a certain "Mariadoth" styled as rex or 'king'.
1 CONT
1 CONT This "Mariadoth" can be no other than Maredudd ab Owain ab Edwin, king of Deheubarth which raises the question as to why a Norman earl was handing out land to a Welsh king particularly one that he had according to Orderic Vitalis,
1 CONC  just defeated in battle in the previous year.
