0 @N6000000001783576665@ NOTE Mistress to Henry I.
1 CONT bore 2 natural children to him: Robert FitzRoy, Earl of Glouster, and Henry FitzRoy who died in 1156 (Wikepedia)
1 CONT
1 CONT Also the grandmother of Milo De Cogan, also related in my line.
0 @N6000000001783576648@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Nest ferch Rhys (died after 1136) was a princess of Deheubarth who was renowned for her beauty. She was the daughter of Prince Rhys ap Tudor Mawr by his wife, Gwladus ferch Rhiwallon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT After her father's death in 1093, Deheubarth was conquered by the Normans and King Henry I of England appointed himself her protector. Nest bore him two sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert FitzRoy, Earl of Glouster
1 CONT Henry FitzRoy who died in 1156.
1 CONT
1 CONT In c. 1095 King Henry decided to marry her to one of his knights, Gerald of Windsor, Castellan of Windsor, whom he appointed constable of Pembroke.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nest and Gerald had five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT William FitzGerald (died 1173)
1 CONT Maurice FitzGerald, Lord of Lanstephen (died September 1, 1177)
1 CONT David FitzGerald, Archdeacon of Cardigan and Bishop of St. David's
1 CONT Angharad de Windsor, who married William de Barry
1 CONT A daughter (possibly Gwladus), the mother of Milo de Cogan
1 CONT
1 CONT During Christmas 1109, Nest and her husband were visited by her cousin, Owain ap Cadwgan, son of Cadwgan ap Bleddyn, prince of Powys. Owain was so taken with Nest's beauty that he and fifteen companions attacked the castle of Cena
1 CONC rth Bychan (possibly Cilgerran Castle), seized Nest, and carried her and her children off. The children were later returned to Gerald. This abduction earned her the nickname "Helen of Wales" because it led to civil war on a small 
1 CONC scale.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nest bore to Owain two sons, Llywelyn and Einion, before finally being returned to her husband.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Gerald's death, Nest became the lover of Stephen, constable of Cardigan, by whom she had another son, Robert Fitz-Stephen who died in 1182.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nest's daughter, Angharad, married Wlliam de Barry and had by him four sons: Robert; Philip, the founder of Ballybeg Abbey at Buttevant in Ireland; Walter; the historian Gerald of Wales. Her sons Philip and Robert campaigned in Ir
1 CONC eland with Strongbow; Robert died there in 1182.
0 @N6000000002577222238@ NOTE Nest Verch Rhys was the Princess of Wales.
0 @N6000000003219709411@ NOTE "Known as the most beautiful woman in Wales. She had many lovers. InChristmas 1108 Owain ap Cadwgan of Cardigan came to visit Gerald andNesta. He so lusted after her that he, that night, attacked the castleand carried her off and 
1 CONC had his way with her. This upset Henry I so muchthat the incident started a war."
1 CONT
1 CONT --borrowed from Brian Tompsett
1 CONT http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/cgi-bin/gedlkup/n=royal?royal05170
