0 @N6000000003097976196@ NOTE The death of Henry I in 1135 was the signal for more vigorous and more hostile policies by the Welsh, though firm action by Stephen (the king who succeeded Henry) and the marcher lords held the promise of successful defence. Henry
1 CONC  II succeeded Stephen in December 1154, determined to restore authority to the kingdom and to repair the damage caused by civil strife and the lack of a strong central administration. By 1157 he was ready to turn his attention to 
1 CONC Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Two princes carried Wales through these difficult years, Owain Gwynedd in the north and Rhys ap Gruffydd  in south Wales. Both were aware of the complex problems to be faced: to deal with rival Welsh dynasties, to deal with marche
1 CONC r lords, and to live in the shadow of a rich and powerful neighbor. Owain gauged the political realities of the day quickly and, however often he had to yield, he did not lose the initiative.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain Gwynedd, Prince of Gwynedd 1137-70, was born circa 1109. In 1137 he succeeded his father Gruffydd ap Cynan  (1081-1137) to the kingdom of Gwynedd, which covered most of north Wales. While England was engaged in civil war, Ow
1 CONC ain used his skill as statesman and soldier to extend his frontiers. In 1157 Henry II led his first campaign against Owain, but it ended in a truce. He was required to do homage to Henry but it was not long before Owain was acting
1 CONC  with complete independence. When Madog ap Maredudd died in 1160, he attacked Powys and extended his influence to the east.
1 CONT
1 CONT Six years later, the Council of Woodstock attempted to reduce the Welsh princes from client status to that of dependent vassalage, and the subsequent uprising was led by Owain and Rhys ap Gruffydd of south Wales. Henry's second at
1 CONC tempt at subduing Wales failed ignominiously and left Owain free to capture Basingwerk and Rhuddlan castles (1166-67). In 1168 he set foot on negotiations with Louis VII of France to build an alliance between Gwynedd and France ag
1 CONC ainst their common enemy. It was a course which required great finesse and firm judgement. In one direction it pointed to a policy which would be used to good effect by later rulers of Gwynedd, the search for recognition and an al
1 CONC liance in Europe. Having openly defied Henry in 1168 by offering to help Louis, Owain maintained his independent position until his death. He left behind him a reputation of wisdom and magnanimity.
1 CONT
1 CONT The reign of Owain Gwynedd marks the most peaceful period of Welsh independence, when the native princes absorbed many of the current European reforming ideas and adapted the more effective structures of both church and state to t
1 CONC heir own society. Monastic foundations were encouraged, diocesan boundaries defined, and many stone churches built. Motte-and-bailey earthwork castles  identical to those built earlier by the Norman invaders were now erected by th
1 CONC e princes as the centers of many of their personal estates. Two of Owain's sons are credited with building the first stone castles in Gwynedd towards the end of the 12th century. The tragedy, recurrent in Welsh history, was that O
1 CONC wain was not followed immediately by a strong ruler. Upon his death in 1170, open warfare broke out between his sons: Dafydd and Rhodri killed their elder half-brother, Hywel, and for the next 20 years Gwynedd was divided between 
1 CONC them and their kinsmen. Gwynedd and Wales would not see another strong leader until Llywelyn the Great  extended his control over most of Wales in the later part of the century.
0 @N6000000002187763528@ NOTE Owain Gwynedd
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain Gwynedd (in English, "Owen") (c. 1100 – November 28, 1170), alternatively known by the patronymic "Owain ap Gruffydd" and also as Owain I of Gwynedd and occasionally Owain I of Wales on account of his domination of the terri
1 CONC tory. He is considered to be the most successful of all the north Welsh princes prior to his grandson, Llywelyn the Great. He was known as Owain Gwynedd to distinguish him from another contemporary Owain ap Gruffydd, ruler of part
1 CONC  of Powys who was known as Owain Cyfeiliog. Owain Gwynedd was a member of the House of Aberffraw, a descendant of the senior branch from Rhodri Mawr.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Accession to the throne and early campaigns
1 CONT     * 3 War with King Henry II
1 CONT     * 4 Disputes with the church and succession
1 CONT     * 5 Fiction
1 CONT     * 6 References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain's father, Gruffydd ap Cynan, was a strong and long-lived ruler who had made the principality of Gwynedd the most influential in Wales during the sixty-two years of his reign, using the island of Anglesey as his power base. H
1 CONC is mother, Angharad ferch Owain, was the daughter of Owain ab Edwin. Owain was the second of three sons of Gruffydd and Angharad.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain is thought to have been born on Anglesey about the year 1100. By about 1120 Gruffydd had grown too old to lead his forces in battle and Owain and his brothers Cadwallon and later Cadwaladr led the forces of Gwynedd against t
1 CONC he Normans and against other Welsh princes with great success. His elder brother Cadwallon was killed in a battle against the forces of Powys in 1132, leaving Owain as his father's heir. Owain and Cadwaladr, in alliance with Gruff
1 CONC ydd ap Rhys of Deheubarth, won a major victory over the Normans at Crug Mawr near Cardigan in 1136 and annexed Ceredigion to their father's realm.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Accession to the throne and early campaigns
1 CONT
1 CONT On Gruffydd's death in 1137, therefore, Owain inherited a portion of a well-established kingdom, but had to share it with Cadwaladr. In 1143 Cadwaladr was implicated in the murder of Anarawd ap Gruffydd of Deheubarth, and Owain re
1 CONC sponded by sending his son Hywel ab Owain Gwynedd to strip him of his lands in the north of Ceredigion. Though Owain was later reconciled with Cadwaladr, from 1143, Owain ruled alone over most of north Wales. In 1155 Cadwaladr was
1 CONC  driven into exile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain took advantage of the civil war in England between King Stephen and the Empress Matilda to push Gwynedd's boundaries further east than ever before. In 1146 he captured the castle of Mold and about 1150 captured Rhuddlan and 
1 CONC encroached on the borders of Powys. The prince of Powys, Madog ap Maredudd, with assistance from Earl Ranulf of Chester, gave battle at Coleshill, but Owain was victorious.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT War with King Henry II
1 CONT
1 CONT All went well until the accession of King Henry II of England in 1154. Henry invaded Gwynedd in 1157 with the support of Madog ap Maredudd of Powys and Owain's brother Cadwaladr. The invasion met with mixed fortunes. King Henry wa
1 CONC s nearly killed in a skirmish near Basingwerk and the fleet accompanying the invasion made a landing on Anglesey where it was defeated. Owain was however obliged to come to terms with Henry, being obliged to surrender Rhuddlan and
1 CONC  other conquests in the east.
1 CONT
1 CONT Madog ap Maredudd died in 1160, enabling Owain to regain territory in the east. In 1163 he formed an alliance with Rhys ap Gruffydd of Deheubarth to challenge English rule. King Henry again invaded Gwynedd in 1165, but instead of 
1 CONC taking the usual route along the northern coastal plain, the king's army invaded from Oswestry and took a route over the Berwyn hills. The invasion was met by an alliance of all the Welsh princes, with Owain as the undisputed lead
1 CONC er. However there was little fighting, for the Welsh weather came to Owain's assistance as torrential rain forced Henry to retreat in disorder. The infuriated Henry mutilated a number of Welsh hostages, including two of Owain's so
1 CONC ns.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry did not invade Gwynedd again and Owain was able to regain his eastern conquests, recapturing Rhuddlan castle in 1167 after a siege of three months.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Disputes with the church and succession
1 CONT
1 CONT The last years of Owain's life were spent in disputes with the Archbishop of Canterbury, Thomas Becket, over the appointment of a new Bishop of Bangor. When the see became vacant Owain had his nominee, Arthur of Bardsey, elected. 
1 CONC The archbishop refused to accept this, so Owain had Arthur consecrated in Ireland. The dispute continued, and the see remained officially vacant until well after Owain's death. He was also put under pressure by the Archbishop and 
1 CONC the Pope to put aside his second wife, Cristin, who was his first cousin, this relationship making the marriage invalid under church law. Despite being excommunicated for his defiance, Owain steadfastly refused to put Cristin asid
1 CONC e. Owain died in 1170, and despite having been excommunicated was buried in Bangor Cathedral by the local clergy. The annalist writing Brut y Tywysogion recorded his death "after innumerable victories, and unconquered from his you
1 CONC th".
1 CONT
1 CONT He is believed to have commissioned the propaganda text, The Life of Gruffydd ap Cynan an account of his father's life. Following his death, civil war broke out between his sons. Owain was married twice, first to Gwladus ferch Lly
1 CONC warch ap Trahaearn, by whom he had two sons, Maelgwn ab Owain Gwynedd and Iorwerth Drwyndwn, the father of Llywelyn the Great, then to Cristin, by whom he had three sons including Dafydd ab Owain Gwynedd and Rhodri ab Owain Gwyned
1 CONC d. He also had a number of illegitimate sons, who by Welsh law had an equal claim on the inheritance if acknowledged by their father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain had originally designated Rhun ab Owain Gwynedd as his successor. Rhun was Owain's favourite son, and his premature death in 1147 plunged his father into a deep melancholy, from which he was only roused by the news that his 
1 CONC forces had captured Mold castle. Owain then designated Hywel ab Owain Gwynedd as his successor, but after his death Hywel was first driven to seek refuge in Ireland by Cristin's sons, Dafydd and Rhodri, then killed at the battle o
1 CONC f Pentraeth when he returned with an Irish army. Dafydd and Rhodri split Gwynedd between them, but a generation passed before Gwynedd was restored to its former glory under Owain's grandson Llywelyn the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to legend, one of Owain's sons was Prince Madoc, who is popularly supposed to have fled across the Atlantic and colonised America. Altogether the prodigeous Owain Gwynedd had the following children from two wives and at 
1 CONC least four mistresses:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Rhun ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Hywel ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Iorwerth ab Owain Gwynedd (from first wife Gwladys ferch Llywarch)
1 CONT     * Maelgwn ab Owain Gwynedd, Lord of Ynys Môn
1 CONT     * Gwenllian ferch Owain Gwynedd
1 CONT     * Dafydd ab Owain Gwynedd (from second wife Cristina ferch Gronw)
1 CONT     * Rhodri ab Owain Gwynedd
1 CONT     * Angharad ferch Owain Gwynedd
1 CONT     * Margaret ferch Owain Gwynedd
1 CONT     * Iefan ab Owain Gwynedd
1 CONT     * Cynan ab Owain Gwynedd, Lord of Meirionydd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Rhirid ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Madoc ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Cynwrig ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Gwenllian ferch Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate - also shared the same name with a sister!)
1 CONT     * Einion ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Iago ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Ffilip ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Cadell ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Rotpert ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Idwal ab Owain Gwynedd (illegitimate)
1 CONT     * Other daughters
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Fiction
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain is a major character in "The summer of the Danes" by Ellis Peters, and also appears in other novels in this writer's Brother Cadfael series. He also appears in "Silver on the Tree," the last book in Susan Cooper's The Dark I
1 CONC s Rising series.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * John Edward Lloyd (1911) A history of Wales from the earliest times to the Edwardian conquest (Longmans, Green & Co.)
1 CONT     * K.L. Maund (ed) (1996). Gruffudd ap Cynan : a collaborative biography, Boydell Press. ISBN 0-851153895.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Gruffudd II Prince of Gwynedd
1 CONT 1137–1170 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Dafydd I
1 CONT Maelgwn II
1 CONT Rhodri III
0 @N6000000001744815182@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 1137-1170. Source: Royal Genealogies <http://ftp.cac.psu.edu/~saw/royal/>.  Also WFT 6, #1556.
1 CONT
1 CONT From http://www.castlewales.com/owain_g.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain Gwynedd
1 CONT
1 CONT (Sources: Davies 1987; Walker 1990 )
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of Henry I in 1135 was the signal for more vigorous and more hostile
1 CONT policies by the Welsh, though firm action by Stephen (the king who succeeded
1 CONT Henry) and the marcher lords held the promise of successful defence. Henry II
1 CONT succeeded Stephen in December 1154, determined to restore authority to the kingdom and to repair the damage caused by civil strife and the lack of a strong central administration. By 1157 he was ready to turn his attention to Wale
1 CONC s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Two princes carried Wales through these difficult years, Owain Gwynedd in the north and Rhys ap Gruffydd in south Wales. Both were aware of the complex problems to be faced: to deal with rival Welsh dynasties, to deal with marcher
1 CONC  lords, and to live in the shadow of a rich and powerful neighbor. Owain gauged the political realities of the day quickly and, however often he had to yield, he did not lose the initiative.
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain Gwynedd, Prince of Gwynedd 1137-70, was born circa 1109. In 1137 he succeeded his father Gruffydd ap Cynan (1081-1137) to the kingdom of Gwynedd, which covered most of north Wales. While England was engaged in civil war, Owa
1 CONC in used his skill as statesman and soldier to extend his frontiers. In 1157 Henry II led his first campaign against Owain, but it ended in a truce. He was required to do homage to Henry but it was not long before Owain was acting 
1 CONC with complete independence. When Madog ap Maredudd died in 1160, he attacked Powys and extended his influence to the east. Six years later, the Council of Woodstock attempted to reduce the Welsh princes from client status to that 
1 CONC of dependent vassalage, and the subsequent uprising was led by Owain and
1 CONT Rhys ap Gruffydd of south Wales. Henry's second attempt at subduing Wales failed ignominiously and left Owain free to capture Basingwerk and Rhuddlan castles (1166-67).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1168 he set foot on negotiations with Louis VII of France to build an alliance between Gwynedd and France against their common enemy. It was a course which required great finesse and firm judgement. In one direction it pointed 
1 CONC to a policy which would be used to good effect by later rulers of Gwynedd, the search for recognition and an alliance in Europe. Having openly defied Henry in 1168 by offering to help Louis, Owain maintained his independent positi
1 CONC on until his death. He left behind him a reputation of wisdom and magnanimity.
1 CONT
1 CONT The reign of Owain Gwynedd marks the most peaceful period of Welsh independence, when the native princes absorbed many of the current European reforming ideas and adapted the more effective structures of both church and state to t
1 CONC heir own society. Monastic foundations were encouraged, diocesan boundaries defined, and many stone churches built. Motte-and-bailey earthwork castles identical to those built earlier by the Norman invaders were now erected by the
1 CONC  princes as the centers of many of their personal estates. Two of Owain's sons are credited with building the first stone castles in Gwynedd towards the end of the 12th century. The tragedy, recurrent in Welsh history, was that
1 CONT Owain was not followed immediately by a strong ruler. Upon his death in 1170, open warfare broke out between his sons: Dafydd and Rhodri killed their elder half-brother, Hywel, and for the next 20 years Gwynedd was divided between
1 CONC  them and their kinsmen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwynedd and Wales would not see another strong leader until Llywelyn the Great extended his control over most of Wales in the later part of the century.Ruled 1137-1170. Source: Royal Genealogies <http://ftp.cac.psu.edu/~saw/royal/
1 CONC >.  Also WFT 6, #1556.
1 CONT
1 CONT From http://www.castlewales.com/owain_g.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Owain Gwynedd
1 CONT
1 CONT (Sources: Davies 1987; Walker 1990 )
