0 @N6000000004969751193@ NOTE David FitzGerald became the second bishop of St. Davids.  He was Archdeacon of Cardigan when consecrated bishop on December 19, 1148 at Canterbury by Archbishop Theobald. The writings of Gerald of Wales (David FitzGerald's nephew)
1 CONC  is the source of this information. The bishopric of St Davids was the largest and richest in Wales. David FitzGerald was said to be constantly at loggerheads with his chapter, and spent much of his time in England. It is also rec
1 CONC orded that the River Alun at St Davids ran with wine during his time there. He died on May 23, 1176 and there is a monument to him in the cathedral. The statue has been defaced - probably by Cromwellians, but a sketch does exist f
1 CONC rom earlier documents, of what the face was like. David was described as a greedy man, ambitious and a despoiler of his bishopric. He was involved in the politics of the invasion of Ireland.
0 @N6000000004969779307@ NOTE The oldest of Gerald and Nesta’s three sons became Lord of Carew (Curru, Carreu, Caru, Carriou) Castle which means that he owned it by inheritance. He was born, married and died in Carew Castle. He married Katherine de Kingsley an
1 CONC d had six children. He was less involved in the invasion of Ireland than his brothers and his sons.
0 @N6000000004969859420@ NOTE William succeeded  to Carew Castle and was ancestor of the family of Carew.  
1 CONT William Fitz Gerald, of Carru Castle, Pembs, eldest son of GeraldFitzWalter, Constable of Pem broke Castle temp. Henry I, and brother ofMaurice FitzGerald, Lord of Maynooth, went to Ireland with Strongbow[Richard de Clare] in 1171
1 CONC  but died in England 1173. [Burke's Peerage,p. 11 31]
1 CONT The genuine Cambro-Norman Carews, that is the senior branch of the Cambro-Norman Fitzgeralds, stemming from the sons of William FitzGerald de Carew son of Welsh Princess Nesta and Norman Gerald FitzWalter, were situated from very 
1 CONC early times in South East County Cork. Their base was at Garryvoe. They appear in the Fiants; Robert de Carreu was one of the 15 most important men in Ireland in 1235, Sir Nicholas de Karreu 1298, Morris de Carew is in Edward 1's 
1 CONC Irish Parliament in 1300, Maurice de Carreu and Raymund de Carreu in 1302, Nicholas de Carrew and John de Carreu are barons of Idrone in Co. Carlow by 1349. Later, still in Garryvoe, John Careue was pardoned for rebellion in 1582 
1 CONT William Fitzgerald de Carew, Lord of Emlyn d about 1173 married maria Montgomery daughter of Arnulf de Montgomery and Lafracouth ua Brian. Wiliam Fitzgerald de Carew's father ws Gerald Fitzwalter de Windsor constable of Pembroke C
1 CONC astle in Wales, his father was Walter Fitzotho, keeper of the forest and castle in the 10th century. William's mother was Nesta, Helen of Wales, daughter of Rhys ap Tewdwr, Prince of South Wales who was married to Gwladys fil Rhiw
1 CONC allon of Wales. William Fitzgerald of Carew and Maria Montgomery has a large family. Daughter Mabilia Fitzgerald de Carew married Nicholas de Caunteton (Condon) and they migrated to Ireland as part of the Norman invasion and were 
1 CONC awarded the lands in the area of Tipperary and cork later known as the Barony of the condons. The family ultimately built about 26 castles in the area around the Blackwater. Other siblings of Mabilia were Gerald de Carew, Lord of 
1 CONC Carew. Griffin of Knocktopher( Baron Fitzgerald). Raymond Le Gros Fitzgerald who married Basilia sister of Strongbow. Otho de Carew b 1140 d 1202. Wiliam Fitzgerald de Carew (Gerards) and Isabel de Carew who married Rhys ap Gruffy
1 CONC dd.
1 CONT the brother William Fitzgerald de Carew (Gerards) had a son Nicholas and a son William Fitzwilliam Fitzgerald 1140 Lord of Moiety. One of those two has a son Robert Carew of Devon who went to Ireland and established the family Car
1 CONC ew of Castleboro.
1 CONT Succeed his father in his office as castellan of Pembroke, lord of Carew and Emlyn. He lived at Pembroke and died there in 1173. He held 2 knights' fees in Hermitage, co. Bucks, and the Manor of Spershot, Co. Berks, 9 Henry II.
1 CONT Generation No. 1 
1 CONT William Fitz Gerald of Carew Castle, Co. Pembroke, went to Ireland with Strongbow in 1171, and died in England 1173. 
1 CONT Children: 
1 CONT Otho 
1 CONT Raymond 
1 CONT William
