1 CONT ______________________________________________________________________
1 CONT From "100 Welsh Heroes" website
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys ap Gruffudd (The Lord Rhys)
1 CONT Leaders (51 votes)
1 CONT
1 CONT 1132 – 1197
1 CONT
1 CONT Powerful ruler of the kingdom of the Deheubarth who held the first ever ‘eisteddfod’.
1 CONT In the deeply divided Wales of the12th century, Rhys ap Gruffudd’s main opponents were the Norman Marcher Lords. He successfully reduced their power by capturing Ceredigion, Emlyn and Cantref Bychan. As the grandson of Rhys ap Tew
1 CONC dwr and related through marriage, to Royal House of Gwynedd he was the only southern Welsh leader who desired political independence for Wales. 
1 CONT
1 CONT So complete was his grip on Dyfed that he caused the Normans – in the shape of Richard, Earl of Pembroke - to look for other areas to control . Rhys assisted Richard to seize the throne of Leinster in Ireland - much to the annoyan
1 CONC ce of Henry II, who wanted Ireland for himself. As Dr John Davies asserts in his book A History Of Wales: “The sorrowful history of English involvement in Ireland has Welsh roots.”
1 CONT
1 CONT When Henry II came to the English throne, Rhys lost most of the land he had previously gained and rose in rebellion three times. During one campaign in 1162, Henry decided it was time to visit Wales himself. Geraldus Cambrensis, r
1 CONC elates how the King came across the ‘Old Man of Pencader’ who, in a celebrated encounter, told the King that he would never conquer his country: 
1 CONT
1 CONT “If it were not for your allies within her/Division and officiousness and blood”.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys proceeded to reclaim his lands until finall Henry realised it was wiser to have him on his own side, appointing him ‘justice’ of south Wales. In 1172, the Rhys demonstrated he was unquestionably the single most powerful figur
1 CONC e in the land by holding an eisteddfod in his castle at Cardigan.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death, his kingdom was destroyed by his unruly sons. As the the Old Man of Pencader put it : “The weaknesses of my nation makes me an old man/and lose my temper completely.”
1 CONT What you said
1 CONT Without his aggressive stewardship of south-west Wales the Norman incursion would have taken hold in its first wave; he ran things for over half a century and without that what would be our conception of Cymru the
1 CONT
1 CONT Without him, Wales could be a completely different place today.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys ap Gruffudd or the Lord Rhys was the last of the rulers of Deheubarth to enjoy a wide degree of power. In 1171, Henry II recognised him as the justiciar of south Wales. A man of wide sympathies, he was a patron of the Cisterc
1 CONC ian order and in 1174 pre
1 CONT
1 CONT © 2003/2004. All rights reserved Culturenet Cymru
1 CONT
1 CONT  From NationMaster.com
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Encyclopedia > The Lord Rhys
1 CONT Rhys ap Gruffydd (1132–1197) was the ruler of the kingdom of Deheubarth (South Wales) from 1155 until his death. Rhys was one of the more successful and powerful Welsh princes, but even he was forced to acknowledge English suprema
1 CONC cy, and did so by accepting the title of "Lord" rather than "Prince" or "King", becoming The Lord Rhys (in Welsh, "Yr Arglwydd Rhys").
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Rhys was the youngest son of Gruffydd ap Rhys, a prince of Deheubarth, and of Gwenllian, a sister of Owain Gwynedd. He was probably born in Ireland. Both his parents died when he was around four years old, Gwenllian as a result of
1 CONC  leading her husband's army into battle in his absence, Gruffydd himself on his return. He was brother to Anarawd, Cadell, Maredudd. After the death of their father in 1137, all four brothers ruled and worked to expand the kingdom
1 CONC  and raid neighbouring princes. Following the death (Anarawd (1143) and Maredudd (1155)) or disablement (Cadell) of all his brothers he became sole ruler of the kingdom by 1155. He proceeded to challenge the new king of England, H
1 CONC enry II (reigned 1154–1189). Having successfully held out against Henry for a year or two, Rhys was defeated and most of his territory was taken from him. This was the point at which he agreed to pay homage to Henry, and peace tem
1 CONC porarily reigned.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1162, Rhys took advantage of Henry's absence in Normandy to attempt to recover some of his lost lands. This resulted once again in defeat, following which Rhys was taken to England as a prisoner. Once released, however, he went
1 CONC  back to his old defiant ways. Henry, preoccupied with domestic problems, could not respond effectively, and the failure of the English troops in Wales and troubles in England helped Rhys to prosper. After the death of Owain Gwyne
1 CONC dd in 1170 he was acknowledged leader of the Welsh princes and called himself "Prince of South Wales".
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1171 he signed a pact with Henry II and helped the king suppress the rebellion of 1173–74. After Henry's death, Rhys was again active, revolting against Richard I and deeply involved in internal Welsh feuds. Rhys found it diffi
1 CONC cult to maintain his position, and was obliged to enter into a protracted struggle against the Norman lord, William de Braose. He died peacefully, an old man at the peak of his power, in 1197.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by Gruffydd ap Rhys.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
0 @N6000000003949663342@ NOTE Poet and Prince of southern Powys. Known as Owain Cyfeilog (the name of the district which his uncle Madog ap Maredudd gave him to rule in 1149). One of the claimants to Powys after Madog's death in 1160, he agreed in 1166 to a pa
1 CONC rtition which resulted in the permanent division of the former kingdom. A patron of poets and a distinguished poet himself, in his ode Hirlas Owain (Owain's Long Blue Drinking Horn) he addressed his companions in arms, alive and d
1 CONC ead, in the traditional tones of a lord of warriors. In politics he generally and sensibly bowed to the reality of English power. Source: Who's Who in British History. Collins & Brown. London. 2000.
