1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 1194-1240. Source: Royal Genealogies <http://ftp.cac.psu.edu/~saw/royal/>
1 CONT
1 CONT from http://www.castlewales.com/llewelyn.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn the Great
1 CONT
1 CONT In Gwynedd, Llywelyn ap Iorweth had come to power in the classic way of Welsh princes bedeviled by the dividing rule of Welsh inheritance - he seized it from his uncle. He proved to be the greatest and most constructive Welsh stat
1 CONC esman
1 CONT of the Middle Ages. In his long career he succeeded, by constant warfare, by tactful yielding under pressure and by masterly resilience the moment that pressure was relieved, in bringing under his control most of Pura Walia. When 
1 CONC he died in 1240, full of honor and glory, he left a principality which had the possibility of expanding into a truly national state of Wales. There was a moment when an independent Wales seemed about to become a reality.
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn deliberately set out on a policy of reconstructing the whole basis of Welsh political life, and not every Welshman was happy about it. Llywelyn lived in an age which saw the emergence of the centralized feudal state. Both
1 CONC  France and England presented the spectacle of societies elaborating their administrative machinery, putting their taxation on a new and sounder footing and systematizing their codes of justice, but Llywelyn's principality was sma
1 CONC ll and lacking resources. Hostile English observers could wax satirical about its pretensions to international status.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwynedd had always been the core of the power of the princes, and the expansion of Llywelyn's territory gave him the ability to do many things beyond the power of previous Welsh rulers. We find Llywelyn ap Iorwerth (the Great) and
1 CONC  Llywelyn ap Gruffydd (the Last) developing castle building on a considerable scale. The remains of Castell y Bere or of Ewloe, Dolbadarn and Dolwyddelan even show distinctive Welsh style. The princes gave charters to the small to
1 CONC wns growing in their domains. They supported the abbeys and the friaries. We sense a new Wales coming into being, and, at the moment, it was basically an independent Wales. The great question was, would this new Wales be able to d
1 CONC evelop to its full potential without interference from without or protests from within? Looming over it was the king of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT For over all this hung the vexing yet vital question of the exact terms of  Llywelyn's homage to the king. The king was always acknowledged as being at the head of the pyramid and by the 13th century Welsh rulers also accepted the
1 CONC  principle that homage should be paid to the King of England. Hywel Dda had done do, far back in the 10th century, and both Owain Gwynedd and the Lord Rhys had done homage to Henry II. The problem was that Llywelyn claimed that th
1 CONC e status in relation to the King of England was the same enjoyed by the King of Scotland - that barons were to pay their homage directly to him and not the king, but King John took a different view. He felt that the barons should 
1 CONC also do their homage to him. This gave him the right of continual interference in Welsh
1 CONT affairs. At times the relationship between Llywelyn and the king were mutually supportive, in part because Llywelyn managed to marry Joan, the illegitimate daughter of King John, in 1205. But even this family tie soon broke down o
1 CONC ver the question of homage and disputed territories.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the Welsh princes were strong they could enforce a grudging acknowledgement of their position from the king. When they were weak, the king granted treaties firmly maintaining his view of homage. Llywelyn
1 CONT the Great had sought to solve the problem before his death. He had two sons, Gruffydd by a Welsh lady and the younger, David, by his wife Joan. Welsh law at the time said that both sons should inherit - a law which had been the ca
1 CONC use of so many of those disputed successions which had brought ruin to Wales
1 CONT in the past. Llywelyn made a bold and successful attempt to put this dangerous Welsh law aside in favor of the English system, and finally got the consent of King Henry III - or his advisors - to agree to the succession of David a
1 CONC s his sole heir. Then, shortly before his death, he called all the princes of Wales together at Strata Florida Abbey in 1238 and made them swear allegiance to David. Henry III allowed him to succeed, but refused David the direct h
1 CONC omage of the barons, eventually leading to war between the king and the new Welsh prince. David and his forces had no chance against Henry's large army and withdrew. David died childless shortly thereafter, and it would be up to L
1 CONC lywelyn's grandson, Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, to assert Wales' independence once again.
1 CONT
1 CONT Statue of Llywelyn in Conwy. Coffin of Llywelyn is in Gwydir Chapel, Llanrwst parish
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Wales - A History, Wynford Vaughan-Thomas, Michael Joseph Ltd. Publishing, London WC1, 1985.Ruled 1194-1240. Source: Royal Genealogies <http://ftp.cac.psu.edu/~saw/royal/>
1 CONT
1 CONT from http://www.castlewales.com/llewelyn.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn the Great
1 CONT
1 CONT In Gwynedd, Llywelyn ap Iorweth had come to power in the classic way of Welsh princes bedeviled by the dividing rule of Welsh inheritance - he seized it from his uncle. He proved to be the greatest and most constructive Welsh stat
1 CONC esman
1 CONT of the Middle Ages. In his long career he succeeded, by constant warfare, by tactful yielding under pressure and by masterly resilience the moment that pressure was relieved, in bringing under his control most of Pura Walia. When 
1 CONC he died in 1240, full of honor and glory, he left a principality which had the possibility of expanding into a truly national state of Wales. There was a moment when an independent Wales seemed about to become a reality.
1 CONT
1 CONT Llywelyn deliberately set out on a policy of reconstructing the whole basis of Welsh political life, and not every Welshman was happy about it. Llywelyn lived in an age which saw the emergence of the centralized feudal state. Both
1 CONC  France and England presented the spectacle of societies elaborating their administrative machinery, putting their taxation on a new and sounder footing and systematizing their codes of justice, but Llywelyn's principality was sma
1 CONC ll and lacking resources. Hostile English observers could wax satirical about its pretensions to international status.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwynedd had always been the core of the power of the princes, and the expansion of Llywelyn's territory gave him the ability to do many things beyond the power of previous Welsh rulers. We find Llywelyn ap Iorwerth (the Great) and
1 CONC  Llywelyn ap Gruffydd (the Last) developing castle building on a considerable scale. The remains of Castell y Bere or of Ewloe, Dolbadarn and Dolwyddelan even show distinctive Welsh style. The princes gave charters to the small to
1 CONC wns growing in their domains. They supported the abbeys and the friaries. We sense a new Wales coming into being, and, at the moment, it was basically an independent Wales. The great question was, would this new Wales be able to d
1 CONC evelop to its full potential without interference from without or protests from within? Looming over it was the king of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT For over all this hung the vexing yet vital question of the exact terms of  Llywelyn's homage to the king. The king was always acknowledged as being at the head of the pyramid and by the 13th century Welsh rulers also accepted the
1 CONC  principle that homage should be paid to the King of England. Hywel Dda had done do, far back in the 10th century, and both Owain Gwynedd and the Lord Rhys had done homage to Henry II. The problem was that Llywelyn claimed that th
1 CONC e status in relation to the King of England was the same enjoyed by the King of Scotland - that barons were to pay their homage directly to him and not the king, but King John took a different view. He felt that the barons should 
1 CONC also do their homage to him. This gave him the right of continual interference in Welsh
1 CONT affairs. At times the relationship between Llywelyn and the king were mutually supportive, in part because Llywelyn managed to marry Joan, the illegitimate daughter of King John, in 1205. But even this family tie soon broke down o
1 CONC ver the question of homage and disputed territories.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the Welsh princes were strong they could enforce a grudging acknowledgement of their position from the king. When they were weak, the king granted treaties firmly maintaining his view of homage. Llywelyn
1 CONT the Great had sought to solve the problem before his death. He had two sons, Gruffydd by a Welsh lady and the younger, David, by his wife Joan. Welsh law at the time said that both sons should inherit - a law which had been the ca
1 CONC use of so many of those disputed successions which had brought ruin to Wales
1 CONT in the past. Llywelyn made a bold and successful attempt to put this dangerous Welsh law aside in favor of the English system, and finally got the consent of King Henry III - or his advisors - to agree to the succession of David a
1 CONC s his sole heir. Then, shortly before his death, he called all the princes of Wales together at Strata Florida Abbey in 1238 and made them swear allegiance to David. Henry III allowed him to succeed, but refused David the direct h
1 CONC omage of the barons, eventually leading to war between the king and the new Welsh prince. David and his forces had no chance against Henry's large army and withdrew. David died childless shortly thereafter, and it would be up to L
1 CONC lywelyn's grandson, Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, to assert Wales' independence once again.
1 CONT
1 CONT Statue of Llywelyn in Conwy. Coffin of Llywelyn is in Gwydir Chapel, Llanrwst parish
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: Wales - A History, Wynford Vaughan-Thomas, Michael Joseph Ltd. Publishing, London WC1, 1985.
