0 @N6000000002187761924@ NOTE William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Braose, Fourth Lord of Bramber (1140/1150 - August 9, 1211) at his peak was also lord of Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth, Radnor, Kington, Limerick, Skenfrith, Grosmont, and Whitecastle. His rise and fall at the h
1 CONC ands of king John is often taken as an example of that king's arbitrary and capricious behavior towards his barons.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was the son of William de Braose, Third Lord of Bramber and Bertha of Hereford, daughter of Miles Fitz Walter, Earl of Hereford and his wife, formerly Sibyl de Neufmarche. From his father he inherited the Rape of Bramber, 
1 CONC in Sussex, and through his mother he inherited a large estate in the Welsh Marches.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1175, William carried out the Massacre of Abergavenny, killing several Welsh princes to avenge the death of his uncle Henry, Earl of Hereford, after having invited them to a feast at Abergavenny Castle. This resulted in great h
1 CONC ostility against him among the Welsh, who named him the "Ogre of Abergavenny".
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1199, William fought beside King Richard the Lion-heart at Chalus, where Richard was killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was greatly favored by King John early in his reign. John granted him all that he might conquer from the Welsh in Radnor, gave him lordship over Limerick in Ireland (save for the city itself), possession of Glamorgan castle, an
1 CONC d then lordship over Gower.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1203, William was put in charge of Arthur of Brittany, whom he had personally captured the previous year. William was suspected of involvement in Arthur's disappearance, although no concrete evidence ever came to light. There i
1 CONC s somewhat better evidence that he at least knew the truth of the matter.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1206 John gave William the three great castles of Gwent (Skenfrith, Grosmont, and Whitecastle). At this point only an earldom separated him from the greatest in England.
1 CONT
1 CONT But soon after William fell out of favor with the king. The precise reasons remain obscure. John's stated reasons regard money de Braose owed the crown. But the king's actions went far beyond what would be necessary to recover the
1 CONC  debt. Instead, he evidently wanted to break de Braose, and to that end invaded Wales to seize the de Braose domains there. Beyond that, he sought de Braose's wife, who, the story goes, had made no secret of her belief that John h
1 CONC ad murdered Arthur of Brittany.
1 CONT
1 CONT De Braose fled to Ireland, then returned to Wales as John hunted him in Ireland. In Wales, William allied himself to the Welsh prince Llywelyn the Great and helped him in rebellion against King John.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1210, William fled in disguise to France and died the following year at Corbeil. William's wife, Maud de St. Valery, and eldest son, William, were captured and murdered by King John, possibly starved to death.
1 CONT
1 CONT While William had aroused the jealousy of the other barons during his rise, the arbitrary and violent manner of his fall very likely discomfited them and played a role in the baronial uprisings of the next decade. The historian Si
1 CONC dney Painter, in his biography of King John, called it "the greatest mistake John made during his reign, as the king revealed to his barons once and for all his capacity for cruelty".
1 CONT
1 CONT Eventually, William's third son, Reginald de Braose reacquired some of his father's titles and lands. The middle son, Giles, was Bishop of Hereford from 1200 until his death in 1215.
1 CONT
1 CONT William's eldest daughter Matilda (also called Maud) married Gruffydd ap Rhys II of Deheubarth. Another daughter, Margaret, married Walter de Lacy, Lord of Meath.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT http://freespace.virgin.net/doug.thompson/BraoseWeb/William3.htm 
1 CONT Holden, Brock W., "King John, the Braoses, and the Celtic Fringe, 1207-1216", Albion: Journal of British Studies v.33 (2001) 
0 @N6000000001744810562@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Burke's Extinct Peerage: "This feudal lord was a personage of great power and influence during the reigns of Henry II and Richard I, from the former of whom he obtained a grant of the "whole kingdom of Limeric, in Ireland," for th
1 CONC e service of 60 knights feews, to be held to the king and his younger brother John. For several years he appears to have enjoyed the favor of King John... but in the 10th year of John's reign, and John.... demanded hostages from h
1 CONC is barons to ensure their allegiance...his officers came upon the mission to the Baron de Braose, were met by Maud his wife, and peremptorily informed that she would not intrust any of her children to the king, who had so basely m
1 CONC urdered her nephew, Prince Arthur. De Braose rebuked her, however, for speaking this, and said that if he had in any thing offended the king, he was ready to make satisfaction....An order was immediately transmitted to seize.. the
1 CONC  baron, but Braose ...fled with his family into Ireland." However, Burke notes that there are different versions, one being that John banished him for his cruelty to the Welsh and that his wife and son died as prisoners at Corfe C
1 CONC astle. Others blame Maud for the cruelty to the Welsh as revenge for the murder of Henry de Hereford. Matthew of Westminster said that de Braose escaped from Scorham disguised as a beggar and died in Paris, where he is buried in t
1 CONC he Abbey of St. Victor, while Matthew paris said that he fled from Ireland to France and died at Ebula, with a burial at St. Victor.
1 CONT
1 CONT "William de Braose, Lord of Brecon, an ambitious lesser baron who rose in King John of England's service. He captured John's nephew Arthur, Duke of Brittany, at Mirebeau in western France in 1202 and was rewarded with many lands  
1 CONC and offices in South Wales, including the post of Sheriff of Herefordshire (1206). His power was increasingly a threat to the Crown, and in 1206 John began to curb it. The king tried to take William's sons hostage in 1208; William
1 CONC  refused, and his family escaped to Ireland just ahead of soldiers sent to arrest them. He raised an unsuccessful rebellion in Wales (1210) and died in exile in France."  Quoted from Elizabeth Hallam, The Plantagenet Encyclopedia 
1 CONC (NY: Crescent Books, 1996) 36.
1 CONT
1 CONT From http://www.castlewales.com/abergav.html:
1 CONT
1 CONT Abergavenny Castle (South of the town centre, Gwent, southeast Wales )
1 CONT by Elisabeth Whittle 1992
1 CONT
1 CONT Enough remains of this castle to show that it must have been impressive indeed, with very high walls which the 16th-century historian Leland said were "likely not to fall." Alas, most of them have, the victims of Civil War slighti
1 CONC ng and for general plunder. From its early beginnings this was an important castle, the headquarters of the Norman lordship of Abergavenny, used for accommodation by kings if they were in the locality. It stands on a spur above th
1 CONC e river Usk, in a good position to secure the valley and prevent Welsh incursions into the lowlands.
1 CONT
1 CONT To the right of the gatehouse is an impressive stretch of curtain wall standing almost to its full height and retaining most of its facing stone. It's the main remnant of the castle of the second half of the 12th century, built wh
1 CONC en William de Braose held the lordship. This was a turbulent time, and the castle was the scene of two particularly treacherous incidents. In 1175 William de Braose murdered Seisyllt ap Dyfnwal, lord of Castell Arnallt, a Welsh st
1 CONC ronghold a few miles to the south-east, here on Christmas Day. In retaliation the Welsh
1 CONT lord of Caerleon, Hywel ap Iorwerth, burnt the castle in 1182 and went on to destroy Dingestow Castle (now reduced to a grassy mound). William Camden, the 16th-century antiquary, said that Abergavenny Castle "has been oftner stain
1 CONC 'd with the infamy of treachery, than any other castle in Wales." Only fragments of the rest of the curtain wall remain, mainly on the east side where the stub of a rectangular projecting tower is visible. Built into a later cotta
1 CONC ge, now part of a museum, is the fragment of a tower, and on the north side the curtain wall is much reduced and was landscaped into a rock garden in the late 19th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Within the walls, the circular mound, on which a rather incongruous Victorian "keep" of 1819 sits, is the oldest part of the castle. It is the motte thrown up by Hamelin de Ballon, Norman conqueror of this area, before 1090. Early
1 CONC  in the 12th century de Ballon founded the Benedictine priory of Abergavenny. Soon afterwards a stone keep was built on the motte, and the present building probably stands on its foundations. During the 12th century the hall, whic
1 CONC h was between the gatehouse  and the tall ruined towers to the west, remained a
1 CONT timber building.
1 CONT
1 CONT There was much building during the 13th and 14th centuries when the castle was held by the Hastings family. The most prominent remains from this period are the towers in the west corner, one circular and one semi-circular. Only th
1 CONC eir outer walls survive, but these stand to four storeys high in some places. The octagonal tower has large window openings, mostly now without their dressed stone surrounds, and the base of a spiral staircase. Attached to the eas
1 CONC tern end of the towers is a cross-wall which divided the castle ward into two. Its northern end was one wall of the hall block, and has a doorway which led into the
1 CONT rooms below the hall. The hall stood where the present ground is sunken, and was a large and rectangular room at first-floor level. Its inner wall has completely gone. In the middle of the lawn is an underground room, thought to h
1 CONC ave been a dungeon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jeff Thomas 1996 adds:
1 CONT
1 CONT Although the description above mentions the castle's notorious lord, William de Braose, it's worth mentioning further that de Braose was quite possibly the cruelest and most hated of all the great Norman Marcher Lords. Practically
1 CONC  all the Marcher Lords were forced to deal with a rebellious and resentful Welsh population in violent ways in order to protect their newly-awarded "kingdoms," but de Braose time and time again seems to have gone out of his way to
1 CONC  commit acts of cruelty that went beyond his contemporaries. Although some would say his family eventually got what they deserved, the extinction of the male line and a forfeiture of all lands, de Braose stands out as an example o
1 CONC f what the
1 CONT native Welsh population were up against, and why they rebelled so ferociously against the Norman invaders.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gerald of Wales alludes to the horrible event in the history of Abergavenny Castle described above, during his famous journey through Wales of 1188, but refuses to mention the incident specifically, saying least (the story) serves
1 CONC  to encourage other equally infamous men. Here Gerald is referring to the Massacre of Abergavenny in 1175. Henry, the third son of Milo FitzWalter, earl of Hereford, was killed by Seisyll ap Dyfnwal in 1175. William the fourth son
1 CONC  did not live to succeed. Mahel, the fifth son, was killed a little later in 1175 in Bronllys Castle, when a stone fell on his head during a fire. There was no other male heir, and Brecknockshire and Upper Gwent passed to William 
1 CONC de Braose through his mother Bertha, a daughter of Milo FitzWalter. William de Braose decided to
1 CONT avenge the death of his uncle Henry. On the pretext he summoned Seisyll ap Dyfnwal, his son Geoffrey and a number of other Welshmen from Gwent to Abergavenny Castle, and there they were all murdered out of hand. At the same time d
1 CONC e Braose's retainers ravaged Seisyll's lands, killed his son Cadwaladr and captured his wife. This is just one incident in the cruel career of de Braose.
1 CONT
1 CONT The castle was one of many that passed back and forth between Marcher and native control in the turbulent years of the 12th century. Gerald also mentions Abergavenny in a later passage following it's recapture from the Welsh by En
1 CONC glish forces. As the Welsh were besieging the castle "two (Norman) men-at-arms were rushing across a bridge to take refuge in the tower which had been built on a great mound of earth. The Welsh shot at them from behind, and with t
1 CONC he arrows which sped from their bows they actually penetrated the oak doorway of the tower, which was almost as think as a man's palm. As a
1 CONT permanent reminder of the strength of their impact, the arrows have been left sticking in the door just where their iron heads struck." Gerald notes that the men of Gwent "are more skilled with the bow and arrow than those who com
1 CONC e from other parts of Wales."Burke's Extinct Peerage: "This feudal lord was a personage of great power and influence during the reigns of Henry II and Richard I, from the former of whom he obtained a grant of the "whole kingdom of
1 CONC  Limeric, in Ireland," for the service of 60 knights feews, to be held to the king and his younger brother John. For several years he appears to have enjoyed the favor of King John... but in the 10th year of John's reign, and John
1 CONC .... demanded hostages from his barons to ensure their allegiance...his officers came upon the mission to the Baron de Braose, were met by Maud his wife, and peremptorily informed that she would not intrust any of her children to 
1 CONC the king, who had so basely murdered her nephew, Prince Arthur. De Braose rebuked her, however, for speaking this, and said that if he had in any thing offended the king, he was ready to make satisfaction....An order was immediate
1 CONC ly transmitted to seize.. the baron, but Braose ...fled with his family into Ireland." However, Burke notes that there are different versions, one being that John banished him for his cruelty to the Welsh and that his wife and son
1 CONC  died as prisoners at Corfe Castle. Others blame Maud for the cruelty to the Welsh as revenge for the murder of Henry de Hereford. Matthew of Westminster said that de Braose escaped from Scorham disguised as a beggar and died in P
1 CONC aris, where he is buried in the Abbey of St. Victor, while Matthew paris said that he fled from Ireland to France and died at Ebula, with a burial at St. Victor.
1 CONT
1 CONT "William de Braose, Lord of Brecon, an ambitious lesser baron who rose in King John of England's service. He captured John's nephew Arthur, Duke of Brittany, at Mirebeau in western France in 1202 and was rewarded with many lands  
1 CONC and offices in South Wales, including the post of Sheriff of Herefordshire (1206). His power was increasingly a threat to the Crown, and in 1206 John began to curb it. The king tried to take William's sons hostage in 1208; William
1 CONC  refused, and his family escaped to Ireland just ahead of soldiers sent to arrest them. He raised an unsuccessful rebellion in Wales (1210) and died in exile in France."  Quoted from Elizabeth Hallam, The Plantagenet Encyclopedia 
1 CONC (NY: Crescent Books, 1996) 36.
1 CONT
1 CONT From http://www.castlewales.com/abergav.html:
