1 CONT
1 CONT Born: probably 1140/50
1 CONT Died: 9th August 1211 at Corbeuil
1 CONT At his peak, William was Lord of Bramber, Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth, Radnor, Kington, Limerick and the three castles of Skenfrith, Grosmont (right) and Whitecastle.
1 CONT He inherited Bramber, Builth and Radnor from his father; Brecknock and Abergavenny through his mother. He was the strongest of the Marcher Lords involved in constant war with the Welsh and other lords. He was particularly hated by
1 CONC  the Welsh for the massacre of three Welsh princes, their families and their men, which took place during a feast at his castle of Abergavenny in 1175. He was sometimes known as the "Ogre of Abergavenny". One of the Normans' forem
1 CONC ost warriors, he fought alongside King Richard at Chalus in 1199 (when Richard received his fatal wound).
1 CONT William immediately transferred his loyalty to Prince John and supported his claim to the throne. John's entry to England was via William's port of Shoreham in Sussex.
1 CONT John extended William's landholdings. He received Limerick, without the city, in 1201 and was also given custody of Glamorgan, Monmouth and Gwynllwg in return for large payments.
1 CONT William captured Arthur, Count of Brittany at Mirebeau in 1202 and was in charge of his imprisonment for King John. He was well rewarded in February 1203 with the grant of Gower. He may have had knowledge of the murder of Arthur a
1 CONC nd been bribed to silence by John with the city of Limerick in July. His honours reached their peak when he was made Sheriff of Herefordshire by John for 1206-7. He had held this office under Richard from 1192 to 1199.
1 CONT His fall began almost immediately. William was stripped of his office as bailiff of Glamorgan and other custodies by King John in 1206/7. Later he was deprived of all his lands and, sought by John in Ireland, he returned to Wales 
1 CONC and joined the Welsh Prince Llywelyn in rebellion. He fled to France in 1210 via Shoreham "in the habit of a beggar" and died in exile near Paris. Despite his stated intention to be interred at St. John's, Brecon, he was buried in
1 CONC  the Abbey of St. Victoire, Paris by Stephen Langton, the Archbishop of Canterbury, another of John's chief opponents who was also taking refuge there. His wife and son William were murdered by John, possibly starved to death at W
1 CONC indsor Castle.
1 CONT Note: The arms shown above are attributed to this William by Matthew Paris (see Aspilogia II , MP IV No7)
1 CONT Father: William de Braose, 3rd Lord of Bramber, Sheriff of Herefordshire
1 CONT Mother: Bertha de Pîtres
1 CONT Married to Maud de St Valery ("before 1170" - Powicke's Loretta)
1 CONT Child 1: William de Braose
1 CONT Child 2: Maud (Susan) = Gruffyd ap Rhys
1 CONT Child 3: Giles, Bishop of Hereford
1 CONT Child 4: Roger
1 CONT Child 5: Philip
1 CONT Child 6: Bertha = William de Beauchamp
1 CONT Child 7: Thomas
1 CONT Child 8: Walter
1 CONT Child 9: John = Amabil de Limesi
1 CONT Child 10: Margaret = Walter de Lacy
1 CONT Child 11: Henry
1 CONT Child 12: Annora = Hugh de Mortimer
1 CONT Child 13: Loretta = Robert de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Leicester
1 CONT Child 14: Reginald de Braose
1 CONT Child 15: Flandrina, Abbess of Godstow
1 CONT Child 16: Bernard
1 CONT This ordering of the children follows the Braose genealogy given in the 13th century MS
1 CONT (British Library, Cotton Julius D, x) on the history of the Lords of Brecon.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------
0 @N6000000004533059821@ NOTE OWNERS of the LORDSHIP of ABERGAVENNY (XI)
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Briouze, Lord of Briouze [in Normandy], Bramber, Brecon,Over-Gwent, etc., son and heir (a).  He m. Maud de St Valery, "Lady ofLa Haie."  In consequence of his well-known quarrel with King John,his lands were forfeited i
1 CONC n 1208, and his wife and 1st son starved todeath in the dungeons of Corfe (or of Windsor) in 1210.  He d. atCorbeil near Paris, 9, and was buried 10 Aug 1211, in the Abbey of St.Victor at Paris.  [Complete Peerage I:22]
1 CONT
1 CONT (a) He slaughtered Seisyll ap Dyvnwal and a host of unarmed Welshmen,in the castle of Abergavenny in 1175, in revenge for the death of hisuncle Henry of Hereford.  Seisyll was owner of Castle Arnold, and issaid in an inaccurate ve
1 CONC rsion of the Brut to have captured Abergavennyin 1172, the slaughter being dated 1177.  But the better version ofthe Brut, on the contrary, states that Seisyll was captured in 1172 bythe garrison of Abergavenny.
1 CONT
1 CONT --------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT At his peak Lord of Bramber, Gower, Abergavenny, Brecknock, Builth,Radnor, Kington, Limerick, and the three castles of Skenfrith,Grosmont, and Whitecastle.
1 CONT
1 CONT William inherited Bramber, Builth, and Radnor from his father,Brecknock and Abergavenny through his mother.  He was the strongest ofthe Marcher Lords involved in constant war with the Welsh and otherlords.  He was particularly hat
1 CONC ed by the Welsh for the massacre ofthree Welsh princes, their families and their men which took placeduring a feast at his castle of Abergavenny in 1175.  He was sometimesknown as the "Ogre of Abergavenny".  One of the Normans' fo
1 CONC remostwarriors, he fought alongside King Richard at Chalus in 1199 (whereRichard was killed).
1 CONT
1 CONT William received Limerick in 1201 from King John.  He was also givencustody of Glamorgan, Monmouth, and Gwynllwg in return for largepayments.
1 CONT
1 CONT William captured Arthur, Count of Brittany at Mirebeau in 1202 and wasin charge of his imprisonment for King John.  He was rewarded inFebruary 1203 with the grant of Gower.  He may have had knowledge ofthe murder Prince Arthur and
1 CONC  been bribed to silence by John with thecity of Limerick in July.  His honors reached their peak when he wasmade Sheriff of Herefordshire by John in 1206-7.  He had held thisoffice under Richard from 1192-1199.
1 CONT
1 CONT His fall began almost immediately.  William was stripped of his officeas bailiff of Glamorgan and other custodies in 1206-7.  Later he wasdeprived of all his lands and, sought by John in Ireland, he returnedto Wales and joined the
1 CONC  Welsh Prince Llewelyn in rebellion.  He fledto France in 1210 via Shoreham "in the habit of a beggar" and died inexile near Paris.  Despite intending to be interred at St John's,Brecon, he was buried in the Abbey of St Victorie, 
1 CONC Paris by StephenLangton, the Archbishop of Canterbury, another of John's chiefopponents who was also taking refuge there.
1 CONT
1 CONT His wife and son were murdered by King John-starved to death atWindsor Castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT See Castle of Grosmont
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Braose inherited the large estates of his grandmother,Berta de Gloucester, and besides possessed the Honour of Braose, inNormandy. This feudal lord was a personage of great power andinfluence during the reigns of Henry 
1 CONC II and Richard I, from the formerof whom he obtained a grant of the "whole kingdom of Limeric, inIreland," for the service of sixty knight's fees, to be held of theking and his younger son, John. For several years after this perio
1 CONC d,he appears to have enjoyed the favour of King John and his power andpossessions were augmented by divers grants from the crown. In the10th of the king's reign [1209], when the kingdom laboured under aninterdiction and John deeme
1 CONC d it expedient to demand hostages from hisbarons to ensure their allegiance should the Pope proceed to thelength of absolving them from obedience to the crown, his officers whocame upon the mission to the Baron de Braose were met 
1 CONC by Maud, hiswife, and peremptorily informed that she would not entrust any of herchildren to the king, who had so basely murdered his own nephew,Prince Arthur. de Braose rebuked her for speaking thus, however, andsaid that if he h
1 CONC ad in anything offended the king, he was ready tomake satisfaction according to the judgment of the court and thebarons, his peers, upon an appointed day and at any fixed placewithout, however, giving hostages. This answer being c
1 CONC ommunicated tothe king, an order was immediately transmitted to seize upon thebaron's person, but Braose having notice thereof fled with his familyinto Ireland.
1 CONT
1 CONT This quarrel between de Braose and King John is, however, differentlyrelated by other authorities. The monk of Llanthony stated that KingJohn disinherited and banished him for his cruelty to the Welsh in hiswar with Gwenwynwyn, an
1 CONC d that his wife Maud and William, his son andheir, died prisoners in Corfe Castle. Another writer relates, "thatthis William de Braose, son of Philip de Braose, Lord of Buelt, heldthe lands of Brecknock and Went for the whole time
1 CONC  of King Henry II,Richard I, and King John without any disturbance until he took to wifethe Lady Maud de St. Walerie, who, in revenge of Henry de Hereford,cause divers Welshmen to be murthered in the castle of Bergavenny asthey sa
1 CONC t at meat; and that for this, and for some other pickt quarrel,King John banished him and all his out of England. Likewise, that inhis exile, Maud his wife, with William, galled, Gam, his son, weretaken and put into prison where s
1 CONC he died the 10th year after herhusband fought with Gwenwynwyn and slew three thousand Welch." Fromthese various relations, says Dugdale, it is no easy matter todiscover what his demerits were, but what usage he had at last, takehe
1 CONC re the credit of these two historians who lived near that time."This year, viz. anno 1240," quoth Matthew of Westminster, "the noblelady Maud, wife of William de Braose, with William, their son andheir, were miserably famished at 
1 CONC Windsor by the command of King John;and William, her husband, escaping from Scorham, put himself into thehabit of a beggar and, privately getting beyond sea, died soon afterat Paris, where he had burial in the abbey of St. Victor.
1 CONC " And MatthewParis, putting his death in anno 1212 (which differs a little intime), says, "That he fled from Ireland to France and, dying at Ebula,his body was carried to Paris and there honourably buried in the abbeyof St. Victor
1 CONC ." "But after these great troubles in his later days,"continues Dugdale, "I shall now say something of his pious works.Being by inheritance from his mother, Lord of Bergavenny, he madegreat grants to the monks of that priory, cond
1 CONC itionally, that theabbot and convent of St. Vincent, in Maine (to which this priory ofBergavenny was a cell) should daily pray for the soul of him, the saidWilliam, and the soul of Maud, his wife."
1 CONT
1 CONT This great but unfortunate personage had issue by his wife, Maud deSt. Walerie, I. William; II. Giles: III. Reginald; IV. Sir John; I.Joane; II. Loretta; III. Margaret; IV. Maud.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the contest between King John and the barons broke out, Giles deBraose, bishop of Hereford, arraying himself under the baronialbanner, was put in possession by the people of Bergavenny and theother castles of the deceased lor
1 CONC d, and eventually King John, in thelast year of his reign, his wrath then being assuaged, granted part ofthose lands to the bishop's younger brother and heir. [Sir BernardBurke, Dormant, Abeyant, Forfeited and Extinct Peerages, Bu
1 CONC rke'sPeerage, Ltd., London, 1883, p. 72, Braose, Baron Braose, of Gower]
0 @N6000000003219688469@ NOTE William held the manor of Abergavenny Castle, which was the scene of two treacherous incidents. In 1175 William murdered Seisyllt ap Dyfnwal,Lord of Castle Arnallt, on Christmas Day in retaliation for the death of William's uncle 
1 CONC Henry Fitzwalter. Meantime, William's men ravaged Seisyllt's lands, murdered both his sons, and captured his wife.William was perhaps the cruelest and most hated Norman Baron, as he seemed to go out of his way to commit violent an
1 CONC d vengeful acts. He abandoned his wife by fleeing to France, while Maud was killed by King John, after he walled her up in her castle with her son William and left them to starve to death.
