1 CONT Reginald de Braose , 13th Lord of Abergavenny 
1 CONT
1 CONT Name: Reynold de Braiose [Ref: Weis AR7 27:28 & 132C:29] 
1 CONT
1 CONT REGINALD DE BRAIOSE, d. 1227/8; m. Grace (or Gracia) de Briwere, d. bef. 1215, dau. William de Briwere and Beatrice de Vaux. (CP I:22) [Ref: Weis AR7 177:7] 
1 CONT
1 CONT There was a definite and timely reason that Reginald de Broase married Gwladys Ddu in 1215. His brother Giles, Bishop of Hereford, rebelled as soon as he received a royal grant of 'his' patrimony and joined Llywelyn (as leader of 
1 CONC the Welsh forces) in general rebellion against John (remember that this was also about the time the North of England, etc., rebelled against John). Reginald immediately joined his brother in marching on their patrimony in the Marc
1 CONC hes to secure it by force. To cement their alliance (Giles being a bishop and all), Reginald was married to a daughter of Prince Llywelyn. Her name happened to be Gwladys, the dark eyed. [Ref: Paul Reed 1 Dec 2001 msg to SGM] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reginald de Braose betrayed Llywelyn in 1217 by going over to King John's side. Llywelyn attacked some of Llywelyn's holdings, and took Reginald's nephew's side. [Ref: Paul Reed 1 Dec 2001 msg to SGM] 
1 CONT
1 CONT His [Giles de Braose, Bishop of Hereford] brother, Reginald, seized possession, and not until after the death of John did he come to terms with the Crown, 23 June 1217. Reginald's defection roused the anger of Llywelyn, who attack
1 CONC ed his lands of Brecon and Gower and supported the claim to the family lands of John de Braose, the eldest of the four sons of William, who had starved to death in 1210. The four sons-John, Giles, Philip, and Walter-were released 
1 CONC from captivity in 1218. John is said to have been brought up secretly by a Welshman in Gower, and he was later given into custody of Bishop Giles, his uncle. John's claim, however was not sustained in the courts, but Reginald, by 
1 CONC a convention, ceded to him the baronies of Gower and Bramber and thus was established the junior branch of the family in its own right. Reginald d. June 1228 and was succeeded by his son, William, the 7th baron, husband of Eva Mar
1 CONC shall. W. R.* [Ref: The Dictionary of Welsh Biography Down to 1940, editors, Sir John Edward Lloyd, R. T. Jenkins, (1959), p. 49] 
1 CONT * Professor-Emeritus William Rees, D.SC., F.S.A., Cardiff, author of this article. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The elder William de Braose died in 1211. Theoretically, the lands should have descended to his grandson John de Braose (son of William de Braose who had been put to death by King John in 1210.) John de Braose was one of four youn
1 CONC g sons of William who were kept as hostages by King John until 1218. 
1 CONT But this John had two uncles, Giles de Braose, Bishop of Hereford, and Reginald de Braose. Giles returned from exile (because of the interdict) in 1213, and appeared at Court in 1214. King John acquiesced to Giles and granted him 
1 CONC the lands that had been held by his father in May 1215. 
1 CONT Once the grant was made, Giles broke out in open rebellion against King John (in spite of his nephews being in royal custody), siding with the princes of Wales. He and his brother Reginald moved quickly to secure their patrimony b
1 CONC y force. 
1 CONT But Giles de Braose died in November 1215, having just made peace with King John. Reginald de Braose then immediately moved to secure the patrimony, and allied himself with the princes of Wales. Cementing an alliance with Llywelyn
1 CONC  was the obvious motivation for marriage to Gwladys in 1215. Though King John made overtures to reconcile Reginald, he remained in rebellion against the King (and thus loyal to Llywelyn) until John's death on 19 October 1216. 
1 CONT William Marshal, acting regent of the minor Henry III, offered the same rights to Reginald de Braose which King John had granted to his brother Bishop Giles. Reginald finally came into the King's fealty June 1217 and swore homage 
1 CONC for the Braose inheritance in England and Wales. 
1 CONT Reginald's nephew John de Braose was released with his brothers in 1218, and sued for his inheritance in England and Wales. Reginald was considered in rebellion by Llywelyn (for siding with the Crown in 1217). Llywelyn marched on 
1 CONC Reginald's Welsh holdings in 1217, with other allies. Reginald surrendered Swansea, and Gower was occupied by Rhys Gryg. Freed in 1218, John was married to Llywelyn's daughter Margaret in 1219 and placed in control of Gower by Lly
1 CONC welyn (at least by 1220). 
1 CONT The King ordered that seisin of Swansea Castle be given to Reginald de Braose in September 1223, but earlier that month Llywelyn had laid siege to Reginald's castle at Builth. Reginald died in 1228. 
1 CONT Brut y Tywysogion gives a very detailed account for 1215 and 1216. In 1215, after the Welsh marched across Wales and the Marches, in rebellion against King John, 'Giles, bishop of Hereford, made peace with the king, from fear of t
1 CONC he pope; and on the ride, going to the king, he was taken ill; and he died at Gloucester, about the feast of St. Martin [10/11 Nov.]; and his patrimony came to his brother Rheinallt/Reinald/Reinallt [Reginald] de Bruse, who took f
1 CONC or his wife the daughter of Llywelyn, son of Iorwerth, prince of Gwynedd.' [the original is in Wlesh] The chronicle then goes on, describing how that year Llywelyn and his forces conquered more of Wales., listing all those loyal t
1 CONC o Llywelyn. [Ref: Paul Reed 28 Nov 2001 msg to SGM] 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Died: by 1228, Said to be buried at St. John's, Brecon
1 CONT
1 CONT Reginald supported Giles in his rebellions against King John. They were both active against the King in the barons' war. Neither was present at the signing of Magna Carta because they were still rebels who refused to compromise Ki
1 CONC ng John aquiesced to Reginald's claims to the de Braose estates in Wales in May 1216. He became Lord of Brecon, Abergavenny, Builth and other Marcher Lordships but was very much a vassal of Llewelyn Fawr, Prince of Gwynedd and now
1 CONC  his father-in-law. Henry III restored Reginald to favour and the Bramber estates (confiscated from William by King John) in 1217. At this seeming betrayal, Rhys and Owain, Reginald's nephews who were princes of Deheubarth, were i
1 CONC ncensed and they took Builth (except the castle). Llewelyn Fawr also became angry and beseiged Brecon. Reginald eventually surrendered to Llewelyn and gave up Seinenydd (Swansea). By 1221 they were at war again with Llewelyn layin
1 CONC g seige to Builth. The seige was relieved by Henry III's forces. From this time on Llewelyn tended to support the claims of Reginald's nephew John concerning the de Braose lands.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reginald was a witness to the re-issue of Magna Carta by Henry III in 1225.
1 CONT aka Reynold
1 CONT
1 CONT ------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Reginald de Braose, 9th Baron Abergavenny (died 1227) was the son of William de Braose, 7th Baron Abergavenny and Matilda de St. Valery. He was a scion of the powerful Marcher family of Braose, and related by marriage to the Princ
1 CONC es of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT He supported his brother Giles in his rebellions against King John. They were both active against the King in the baron's war. Neither was present at the signing of Magna Carta because they were still rebels who refused to comprom
1 CONC ise. John acquiesced to Reginald's claims to the de Braose estates in Wales in May 1216. He became Lord of Brecon, Abergavenny, Builth and other Marcher Lordships but was very much a vassal of Llewelyn Fawr, Prince of Gwynedd who 
1 CONC became his father-in-law in 1222 when Reginald married Llywelyn's daughter, Gwladus Ddu. Henry III restored Reginald to favour and the Bramber estates (confiscated from William by King John) in 1217. At this seeming betrayal, Rhys
1 CONC  and Owain, Reginald's nephews who were princes of Deheubarth, were incensed and they took Builth (except the castle). Llywelyn Fawr also became angry and besieged Brecon. Reginald eventually surrendered to Llewelyn and gave up Se
1 CONC inhenydd (Swansea). By 1221 they were at war again with Llewelyn laying siege to Builth. The siege was relieved by Henry III's forces. From this time on Llewelyn tended to support the claims of Reginald's nephew John de Braose con
1 CONC cerning the de Braose lands.
1 CONT Reginald was a witness to the re-issue of Magna Carta by Henry III in 1225. He died two years later and was succeeded by his son, the ill-fated William de Braose, Lord of Abergavenny. His daughter Matilda was the wife of Rhys Mech
1 CONC yll, Prince of Deheubarth.
1 CONT
1 CONT -----------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT DOUG THOMPSON, The Barons de Braose:
1 CONT
1 CONT Died: by 1228
1 CONT Reginald is said to be buried at St. John's, Brecon (right).
1 CONT Reginald supported Giles in his rebellions against King John. They were both active against the King in the barons' war. Neither was present at the signing of Magna Carta because they were still rebels who refused to compromise. K
1 CONC ing John aquiesced to Reginald's claims to the de Braose estates in Wales in May 1216. He became Lord of Brecon, Abergavenny, Builth and other Marcher lordships but was very much a vassal of Llywelyn Fawr, Prince of Gwynedd and no
1 CONC w his father-in-law. Henry III restored Reginald to favour and the Bramber estates (confiscated from William by King John) in 1217. At this seeming betrayal, Rhys and Owain, Reginald's nephews who were princes of Deheubarth, were 
1 CONC incensed and they took Builth (except the castle). Llewelyn Fawr also became angry and besieged Brecon. Reginald eventually surrendered to Llewelyn and gave up Seinhenydd (Swansea). By 1221 they were at war again with Llewelyn lay
1 CONC ing siege to Builth. The seige was relieved by Henry III's forces. From this time on Llewelyn tended to support the claims of Reginald's nephew John concerning the de Braose lands.
1 CONT
1 CONT Reginald was a witness to the re-issue of Magna Carta by Henry III in 1225.
