0 @N6000000000232592510@ NOTE ==========
1 CONT William de Ferrers, 4th Earl of Derby
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT William II de Ferrers (also spelled "de Ferrer"), 4th Earl of Derby [b~1168 d 1247] was a favourite of King John. 
1 CONT
1 CONT William was a Sheriff of Nottinghamshire and Derbyshire for seven weeks in 1194, after the attack and siege by King Richard's forces upon Nottingham on the 28th March 1194, a year in which John Major the Scottish Chronicler says R
1 CONC obin Hood was outlawed. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferrers led the forces loyal to King Richard and was aided by Earl David Ceannmhor. 
1 CONT
1 CONT William II was a patron of at least 2 abbeys and 4 priories. He supported the canopy at King Richard's coronation on 17th April 1194 and in 1215 took the cross. He was bailiff of the Peak Forest and warden of the Peak Castle [Peve
1 CONC ril Castle] and Bolsover Castle from 13th Aug 1216 to June 1222. [i.e. the grant was made in the final year of King John] 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a Crusader from May 1214 to July 1220. He remained faithful to each king - Richard, John, Henry III. He was married to Agnes De Kevelioch for 55 years. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was present at the Siege of Lincoln.
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Ferrers%2C_4th_Earl_of_Derby"
1 CONT ==========
