0 @N6000000001503830216@ NOTE William II de Ferrers (also spelled "de Ferrer"), 4th Earl of Derby, [c.1168–c.1247] was a favourite of King John of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was a Sheriff of Nottinghamshire and Derbyshire for seven weeks in 1194, after the attack and siege by King Richard's forces upon Nottingham on the 28th March 1194. Ferrers led the forces loyal to King Richard and was aide
1 CONC d by Earl David Ceannmhor. He supported the canopy at King Richard's coronation on 17th April 1194.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was a patron of at least 2 abbeys and 4 priories. He was bailiff of the Peak Forest and warden of the Peak Castle [Peveril Castle] and Bolsover Castle from 13th Aug 1216 to June 1222 [i.e. the grant was made in the final y
1 CONC ear of King John]. He was a Crusader from May 1214 to July 1220. He was present at the Siege of Lincoln.
1 CONT
1 CONT He remained faithful to each king he served - Richard, John, and Henry III. He was married to Agnes De Kevelioch for 55 years.
