0 @N6000000002188014010@ NOTE Henry de Bohun, 1st Earl of Hereford
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry de Bohun, 1st Earl of Hereford (1176 - 1220) was an English nobleman. He was Earl of Hereford and Hereditary Constable of England from 1199 to 1220.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Humphrey de Bohun and Princess Margaret, daughter of Henry of Scotland, 3rd Earl of Huntingdon, a son of David I of Scotland. His paternal grandmother was Margaret, daughter of Miles de Gloucester, 1st Earl of He
1 CONC reford and Constable of England. Bohun's half-sister was Constance, Duchess of Brittany.
1 CONT
1 CONT The male line of Miles of Gloucester having failed, on the accession of King John of England, Bohun was created Earl of Hereford and Constable of England (1199). He married Maud of Essex, daughter of Geoffrey Fitzpeter, 1st Earl o
1 CONC f Essex. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Humphrey de Bohun, 2nd Earl of Hereford 
1 CONT Margery de Bohun, married Waleran de Beaumont, 4th Earl of Warwick. 
1 CONT Bohun was one of the 25 sureties of the Magna Carta, and was subsequently excommunicated by the Pope. He was also a supporter of King Louis VIII of France and was captured at the Battle of Lincoln in 1217. He died whilst on pilgri
1 CONC mage to the Holy Land.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Humphrey de Bohun Lord High Constable
1 CONT 1199–1220 Succeeded by:
1 CONT The Earl of Hereford 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT New Creation Earl of Hereford
1 CONT 1199–1220 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Humphrey de Bohun 
0 @N6000000003853670696@ NOTE 1st Earl of Hereford, Sheriff of Kent, Hereditary Constable of England
0 @N6000000003051159860@ NOTE Lord High Constable of England
1 CONT 28 Apr 1199: 1st Earl of Hereford
1 CONT 1215: Surety for the Magna Charta
1 CONT Sheriff of Kent
1 CONT
1 CONT Because he took prominent part with the barons against King John, his lands were sequestered, but he received them again at the granting of the Magna Charta. He was excommunicated by the Pope with the other barons, and did not ret
1 CONC urn to his allegience on the death of King John, but was one of the commanders in the army of the Dauphin, at the battle of Lincoln and was taken prisoner by William Marshall. After this defeat, he joined Saher de Quincy and other
1 CONC  Magna Charta barons in a pilgrimage to the Holy Land in 1220. and died on the passage. His body was brought homeand buried in the chapter-house of Llanthony Abbey in Gloucestershire.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Weis 93] sheriff of Kent, Hereditary Constable of England, Magna Chart Surety, 1215.
