0 @N6000000002188318677@ NOTE Gilbert de Gant, Earl of Lincoln
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gilbert de Gant, Earl of Lincoln (c. 1126 – 1156) was the son of Walter de Gant and Maud of Brittany.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT He was made Earl of Lincoln by King Stephen, despite William de Roumare (Romayre) still enjoying the Earldom. He was also known as comte de Lincolne Gislebert II de Gand.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Rohese de Clare, daughter of Richard fitz Gilbert, Lord of Clare and Adeliza de Meschines; He was taken prisoner, while still in his youth, with King Stephen at the Battle of Lincoln in 1142. He was later compelled by R
1 CONC anulph de Gernon, 2nd Earl of Chester, to marry his niece, the Lady Hawyse de Roumare, daughter and heir of William de Roumare, in whose right he eventually became Earl of Lincoln. They had daughter Alice de Gant, married to Simon
1 CONC  of St Liz III, 7th Earl of Huntingdon and Northampton.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT He founded, as Earl of Lincoln, Rufford Abbey c. 1148 in Nottinghamshire, England. He became the fourth Earl of Lincoln in England, in 1147.
1 CONT
1 CONT His uncle was Robert de Gant, who was Lord Chancellor for King Stephen.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ a b Charles F H Evans. England, Earls created 1138-1143 (v1.2 edition). Foundation for Medieval Genealogy. Retrieved on 20080121.
1 CONT    2. ^ Crouch, David The Reign of King Stephen: 1135-1154 Harlow, Essex: Longman Pearson 2000 ISBN 0-582-22657-0 p. 160
0 @N6000000004533909460@ NOTE Ancestral File Number:<AFN> MQD6-J0
0 @N6000000003949830789@ NOTE Agnes de Gaunt; m. William de Mohun, adult by 1131, d. in or before 1155, Earl of Somerset, held barony of Dunster, Somerset, son of Sir William de Mohun, d. after 1190, lord of Dunster, Somerset, Sheriff of Somerset 1084 & 1086, 
1 CONC by his wife Adeliz. The proof of Agnes de Gaunt's identity is the manor of Whichford, co. Warwick, a Gaunt family property, which she received in her maritagium. Agnes and her husband William de Mohun later gave the church at Whic
1 CONC hford to Bridlington Priory, which priory was founded by Agnes' father, Walter de Gaunt. [Ancestral Roots]
1 CONT He [William de Moion] married Agnes, daughter of Walter DE GANT. He d. probably in or before 1155. Stephen did not recognise his Earldom, and as Henry II did not grant a new charter to William or his son, the Earldom of Somerset (
1 CONC so far as it existed) lapsed at or before his death. [Complete Peerage XII/1:37-9, (transcribed by Dave Utzinger)]
1 CONT The following post to SGM, 6 Nov 2001, by Douglas Richardson, indicates that, despite the fact that Ancestral Roots indicates the mother is Maud of Brittany, Agnes is daughter of an unidentified first wife:
1 CONT From: Douglas Richardson (royalancestry@msn.com)  Subject: Re: Alice Mohun's royal ancestors 
1 CONT Newsgroups: soc.genealogy.medieval    Date: 2001-11-06 09:33:36 PST 
1 CONT I think if you check the chronology, you'll find that Agnes de Gaunt (your #33), wife of William de Mohun, can't possibly be the daughter of Walter de Gaunt's marriage to Maud of Brittany. Rather, it appears that Agnes was either 
1 CONC Walter's daughter by a hitherto unidentified first wife, or Walter's sister. This matter deserves further study.
1 CONT This correction comes courtesy of my good friend, Elma Bradshaw, of Salt Lake City, who recently pointed out the Gaunt-Mohun chronology problem to me. Elma is a former employee of the Medieval Families Unit here at the Family Hist
1 CONC ory Library in Salt Lake City. I was interested in the problem as I descend from the Mohun family through my immigrant ancestress, Agnes (Harris) (Spencer) Edwards, of Hartford, Connecticut. Elma graciously shared her findings wit
1 CONC h me.
